/*
 * OCR B A-Level Chemistry (Salters, H433) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { storylineId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Grouped by OCR's ten storylines; ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Salters papers are context-led: every question is set in its
 * storyline (the elements of life, fuels, the sea, ozone, medicines, industry,
 * polymers, oceans, metals, colour), opens with a stem — a process, data, an unknown,
 * a student's method — and the parts hang off it. So nearly every item here carries
 * a storyline stem, and each storyline includes practical items. Paper 1's multiple
 * choice is not in this bank.
 *
 * TARIFFS. H433 parts are worth 1 to 6 marks, most 1–3, with 6-mark Level of
 * Response items (6*). Mix per storyline: 20% 1, 28% 2, 24% 3, 14% 4, 6% 5, 8% 6.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES follow OCR's layout: one point per line with a tick, then ALLOW /
 * IGNORE / DO NOT ALLOW / ECF guidance; 6* items give indicative points by strand
 * and OCR's Level 3/2/1 descriptors.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 */

const CHEMISTRY_OCR_B_PRACTICE = {
  'EL': {
    name: 'Elements of Life',
    questions: [
      { q: `Magnesium is an essential element in the human body; most of it is stored in bones. Naturally occurring magnesium contains the isotopes ²⁴Mg, ²⁵Mg and ²⁶Mg. The atomic number of magnesium is 12.\n\nState the number of neutrons in one atom of ²⁶Mg.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `14 ✓` },
      { q: `Calcium ions, Ca²⁺, are needed in the body for muscle contraction and for building bones and teeth. The atomic number of calcium is 20.\n\nWrite the full electron configuration of a Ca²⁺ ion, using sub-shell notation.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW [Ar] (a full configuration is asked for)\nIGNORE 4s⁰` },
      { q: `In the core of the Sun, hydrogen nuclei fuse together. The first step in this process can be written as:\n\n¹₁H + ¹₁H → ²₁H + X\n\nIdentify particle X. Give its symbol, including its mass number and charge.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `⁰₊₁e / positron (with mass number 0 and charge +1 shown) ✓\nALLOW ⁰₁e or β⁺ with 0 and +1 shown\nDO NOT ALLOW an electron, ⁰₋₁e` },
      { q: `Iodine is needed by the thyroid gland to make the hormones that control metabolism. Iodine is in Period 5 and Group 17 of the Periodic Table.\n\nWrite the electron configuration of the outer shell of an iodine atom, using sub-shell notation.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `5s² 5p⁵ ✓\nIGNORE 4d¹⁰ if included` },
      { q: `Before an X-ray of the digestive system, a patient drinks a 'barium meal'. This is a suspension of insoluble barium sulfate, made by mixing solutions of barium chloride and sodium sulfate.\n\nWrite the ionic equation, with state symbols, for the formation of barium sulfate.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s) ✓\nAll formulae and state symbols needed for the mark\nDO NOT ALLOW equations including spectator ions Na⁺ or Cl⁻` },
      { q: `An indigestion tablet contains 0.500 g of calcium carbonate, CaCO₃, to neutralise excess stomach acid.\n\nCalculate the amount, in mol, of CaCO₃ in one tablet. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nAr: C 12.0, O 16.0, Ca 40.1`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mr = 100.1; amount = 0.500 ÷ 100.1 = 5.00 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nALLOW 0.00500 mol\nALLOW 4.99 × 10⁻³ mol (Mr rounded differently) — DO NOT ALLOW 2 s.f. answer 5.0 × 10⁻³` },
      { q: `A student titrates a solution of limewater, Ca(OH)₂(aq), with dilute hydrochloric acid to find how much calcium hydroxide is dissolved. The student adds the acid from a burette, rather than from a 50 cm³ measuring cylinder.\n\nSuggest why a burette is used.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `A burette measures volume more precisely / has a smaller (percentage) uncertainty / allows the acid to be added dropwise near the end point ✓\nALLOW more accurate\nIGNORE 'easier'` },
      { q: `Phosphorus is the second most abundant mineral element in the body. It is found in bone mineral and in DNA as phosphate ions, PO₄³⁻. In a phosphate ion, the phosphorus atom is surrounded by four groups of electrons, each a bond to an oxygen atom, and there are no lone pairs on phosphorus.\n\nState the O–P–O bond angle in a phosphate ion.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `109.5° ✓\nALLOW 109° or 109–110°` },
      { q: `Milk of magnesia is a suspension of magnesium hydroxide in water and is used as an antacid. Magnesium hydroxide is only sparingly soluble in water.\n\nState the trend in the solubility of the Group 2 hydroxides going down the group from magnesium hydroxide to barium hydroxide.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Solubility increases (down the group) ✓\nALLOW 'become more soluble'` },
      { q: `When the light from a star is passed through a spectroscope, a continuous spectrum is seen crossed by a series of dark lines.\n\nState what causes the dark lines.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Atoms/ions (of elements in the outer layers/atmosphere of the star) absorb light of particular frequencies ✓\nALLOW absorb specific wavelengths/energies\nIGNORE references to 'gases blocking light'` },
      { q: `A sample of magnesium extracted from a mineral was analysed in a mass spectrometer. The percentage abundances of its isotopes are shown below (data written for this practice question).\n\n²⁴Mg 78.99%\n²⁵Mg 10.00%\n²⁶Mg 11.01%\n\nCalculate the relative atomic mass, Ar, of magnesium in this sample. Give your answer to 1 decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(24 × 78.99) + (25 × 10.00) + (26 × 11.01) = 2432.02 ✓\n÷ 100 = 24.3 ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW 24.32 for 1 mark only if not given to 1 d.p.` },
      { q: `The spectrum of a hot star shows a strong red line from hydrogen at a wavelength of 656 nm.\n\nCalculate the frequency of light with this wavelength. Give your answer to 3 significant figures and include units.\n\nc = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ν = c ÷ λ = 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 656 × 10⁻⁹ ✓ (conversion of nm to m)\n= 4.57 × 10¹⁴ Hz ✓\nALLOW s⁻¹ for Hz\nUnits needed for second mark\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Sodium street lamps glow orange because sodium atoms emit light of wavelength 589 nm.\n\nCalculate the energy, in J, of one photon of this light. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nh = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s; c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ν = 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 589 × 10⁻⁹ = 5.09 × 10¹⁴ Hz OR E = hc/λ set up ✓\nE = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 5.09 × 10¹⁴ = 3.38 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW 3.37 × 10⁻¹⁹ from rounded intermediate` },
      { q: `Nitrogen is excreted from the body partly as ammonium ions, NH₄⁺, in urine. An ammonium ion forms when an ammonia molecule bonds to a hydrogen ion, H⁺.\n\nDraw a dot-and-cross diagram of an ammonium ion. Show outer-shell electrons only.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Four bonding pairs between N and four H atoms, with no lone pair on N ✓\nOne bonding pair shown as two electrons from N (dative bond) and the overall charge + shown (e.g. square brackets with +) ✓\nIGNORE circles\nALLOW any consistent symbols for electrons from N and H` },
      { q: `When a sample of helium is heated in a discharge tube, it emits light. The emission spectrum seen through a spectroscope is a series of separate coloured lines on a dark background, not a continuous spectrum.\n\nExplain why the emission spectrum consists of lines.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Electrons can only occupy fixed/discrete energy levels (energy is quantised) ✓\nElectrons fall from higher to lower levels emitting photons of specific energies, so only specific frequencies (ΔE = hν) are emitted ✓\nALLOW wavelengths for frequencies` },
      { q: `A student makes a standard solution of sodium carbonate to use in a titration. The student dissolves 1.06 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate, Na₂CO₃, in water and makes the solution up to 250.0 cm³ in a volumetric flask.\n\nCalculate the concentration, in mol dm⁻³, of the sodium carbonate solution.\n\nAr: C 12.0, O 16.0, Na 23.0`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Amount Na₂CO₃ = 1.06 ÷ 106.0 = 0.0100 mol ✓\nConcentration = 0.0100 ÷ 0.250 = 0.0400 mol dm⁻³ ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nECF from incorrect amount` },
      { q: `Magnesium reacts very slowly with cold water but reacts quickly when heated in steam, glowing brightly and leaving a white solid.\n\nWrite the equation, with state symbols, for the reaction of magnesium with steam.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Mg + H₂O → MgO + H₂ ✓\nState symbols: Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → MgO(s) + H₂(g) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW Mg(OH)₂ as a product for the first mark\nState symbols mark dependent on correct species` },
      { q: `Neon collected from the atmosphere was injected into a mass spectrometer. The mass spectrum showed three peaks, at m/z = 20, 21 and 22.\n\nExplain why neon gives three peaks.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Neon has three isotopes ✓\nAtoms with the same number of protons (10) but different numbers of neutrons (10, 11, 12), so different masses ✓\nALLOW ions ²⁰Ne⁺, ²¹Ne⁺, ²²Ne⁺ have different m/z` },
      { q: `Nuclear fusion of hydrogen only takes place in the core of a star, where the temperature is about 1.5 × 10⁷ K.\n\nExplain why fusion needs such a high temperature.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Nuclei are (both) positively charged so repel each other ✓\nAt high temperature the nuclei have enough kinetic energy / move fast enough to overcome the repulsion and get close enough to fuse ✓\nIGNORE references to atoms colliding without mention of nuclei/charge` },
      { q: `Sweat contains chloride ions. A student tests a sample of sweat collected on a filter paper by soaking it in distilled water, acidifying with dilute nitric acid and adding silver nitrate solution.\n\nDescribe what the student would observe, and write the ionic equation, with state symbols, for the reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `White precipitate ✓\nAg⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s) ✓\nState symbols required for the equation mark` },
      { q: `A student titrated 25.0 cm³ portions of limewater with dilute hydrochloric acid. The titres recorded were (data written for this practice question):\n\nRough 23.45 cm³; Run 1 23.10 cm³; Run 2 23.15 cm³; Run 3 23.05 cm³\n\nCalculate the mean titre the student should use, showing which titres you have used.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Uses concordant titres only: 23.10, 23.15 and 23.05 (rough titre excluded) ✓\nMean = 23.10 cm³ ✓\nALLOW 23.1 cm³\nDO NOT ALLOW a mean including the rough titre (23.19 cm³)` },
      { q: `In a titration, a student records a titre of 23.10 cm³. Each burette reading has an uncertainty of ±0.05 cm³.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the titre.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Total uncertainty = 2 × 0.05 = ±0.10 cm³ ✓\nPercentage uncertainty = 0.10 ÷ 23.10 × 100 = 0.43% ✓\nALLOW 0.4%\nALLOW 0.22% for 1 mark only (one reading)` },
      { q: `Iron is present in haemoglobin, which carries oxygen in red blood cells. Iron tablets taken for anaemia usually contain iron(II) sulfate. The atomic number of iron is 26.\n\nWrite the full electron configurations, using sub-shell notation, of an iron atom and of an Fe²⁺ ion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Fe: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁶ 4s² ✓\nFe²⁺: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁶ ✓\nALLOW 4s before 3d in the atom\nDO NOT ALLOW Fe²⁺ as 3d⁴ 4s²` },
      { q: `Quicklime, calcium oxide, is an ionic compound formed when limestone is heated strongly.\n\nDraw a dot-and-cross diagram for calcium oxide. Show outer-shell electrons only.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Ca²⁺ ion shown with no outer electrons (or an empty/full shell of 8) AND O²⁻ ion with 8 outer electrons, 2 of them shown as crosses/dots from Ca ✓\nCorrect charges on both ions (2+ and 2–) ✓\nIGNORE inner shells\nDO NOT ALLOW any shared pair of electrons` },
      { q: `The concentration of calcium ions in human blood plasma is normally about 2.40 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³. An adult has about 5.00 dm³ of blood (data written for this practice question; treat all of it as plasma).\n\nCalculate the total mass, in g, of calcium ions in this volume of blood.\n\nAr: Ca 40.1`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Amount = 2.40 × 10⁻³ × 5.00 = 0.0120 mol ✓\nMass = 0.0120 × 40.1 = 0.481 g ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW 0.48 g` },
      { q: `Bone mineral contains a calcium phosphate compound. A sample of the compound was analysed and found to contain, by mass, 38.7% calcium, 20.0% phosphorus and 41.3% oxygen.\n\nCalculate the empirical formula of this compound. Show your working.\n\nAr: O 16.0, P 31.0, Ca 40.1`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Amounts: Ca 38.7 ÷ 40.1 = 0.965; P 20.0 ÷ 31.0 = 0.645; O 41.3 ÷ 16.0 = 2.58 ✓\nRatio Ca : P : O = 1.5 : 1 : 4 ✓\nEmpirical formula Ca₃P₂O₈ ✓\nALLOW Ca₃(PO₄)₂\nDO NOT ALLOW Ca₁.₅PO₄ for the third mark` },
      { q: `When Group 2 carbonates are heated, they decompose to form the metal oxide and carbon dioxide. Magnesium carbonate decomposes at a much lower temperature than barium carbonate.\n\nExplain why magnesium carbonate is less thermally stable than barium carbonate.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Mg²⁺ is smaller than Ba²⁺ (same charge) ✓\nSo Mg²⁺ has a higher charge density ✓\nMg²⁺ polarises/distorts the (electron cloud of the) carbonate ion more, weakening the C–O bonds so CO₂ is released more easily ✓\nALLOW 'ionic radius increases down the group' for the first mark` },
      { q: `Limewater is a saturated solution of calcium hydroxide. A student titrated 25.0 cm³ of limewater with 0.0500 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid. The mean titre was 18.40 cm³.\n\nCa(OH)₂(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + 2H₂O(l)\n\nCalculate the concentration, in mol dm⁻³, of calcium hydroxide in the limewater. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Amount HCl = 0.0500 × 18.40 ÷ 1000 = 9.20 × 10⁻⁴ mol ✓\nAmount Ca(OH)₂ = 9.20 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 2 = 4.60 × 10⁻⁴ mol ✓\nConcentration = 4.60 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 0.0250 = 0.0184 mol dm⁻³ ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nECF throughout` },
      { q: `Helium was discovered in the Sun in 1868, before it had been found on Earth, when astronomers saw a yellow line in the spectrum of the Sun that did not match any known element.\n\nExplain why each element has a unique line spectrum, and how this allowed helium to be identified in the Sun.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Each element has a different/unique set of electron energy levels (because of its different nuclear charge/number of electrons) ✓\nSo the energy gaps, and therefore the frequencies of lines (ΔE = hν), are unique to that element ✓\nThe pattern of lines in the Sun's spectrum did not match any known element's spectrum, so had to be a new element (later matched by helium on Earth) ✓\nALLOW 'like a fingerprint' for the idea of uniqueness only if linked to energy levels` },
      { q: `Ammonia is made in the liver when amino acids are broken down. In an ammonia molecule, NH₃, the H–N–H bond angle is 107°.\n\nExplain the shape of an ammonia molecule and why the bond angle is 107°.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Three bonding pairs and one lone pair around N ✓\nElectron pairs repel to get as far apart as possible, giving a (trigonal) pyramidal shape ✓\nThe lone pair repels more strongly than bonding pairs, so the bond angle is reduced from 109.5° (to 107°) ✓\nALLOW 'four groups of electrons' for four electron pairs` },
      { q: `The isotopes in a meteorite sample of strontium were analysed by mass spectrometry. The results are shown below (data written for this practice question).\n\nm/z 84: 0.56%\nm/z 86: 9.86%\nm/z 87: 7.00%\nm/z 88: 82.58%\n\nCalculate the relative atomic mass of strontium in this sample. Give your answer to 1 decimal place.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(84 × 0.56) + (86 × 9.86) + (87 × 7.00) + (88 × 82.58) ✓\n= 8771.04 (÷ 100) ✓\nAr = 87.7 ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW 87.71 for 2 marks only` },
      { q: `A line in the spectrum of a distant star is caused by electrons in hydrogen atoms moving between two energy levels that differ by 4.09 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.\n\nCalculate the wavelength, in nm, of this line. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nh = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s; c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ν = E ÷ h = 4.09 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ = 6.17 × 10¹⁴ Hz ✓\nλ = c ÷ ν = 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 6.17 × 10¹⁴ = 4.86 × 10⁻⁷ m ✓\n= 486 nm ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW λ = hc/E in one step` },
      { q: `The Big Bang produced mainly hydrogen and helium. Most of the carbon and oxygen in the human body was made later, inside stars.\n\nExplain how carbon nuclei are formed in stars.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `When the hydrogen in the core runs low, the core contracts and its temperature rises (to about 10⁸ K) ✓\nHelium nuclei then fuse together (three ⁴He nuclei form ¹²C) ✓\nThe high temperature gives nuclei enough energy to overcome the (greater) repulsion between the more highly charged nuclei ✓\nALLOW 3 ⁴₂He → ¹²₆C for the second mark` },
      { q: `A student adds a small piece of calcium to cold water in a beaker and then adds a few drops of universal indicator.\n\nDescribe two observations the student would make and write an equation, with state symbols, for the reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two observations from: fizzing/bubbles; calcium sinks then rises/moves; calcium disappears/gets smaller; cloudy white suspension forms; indicator turns blue/purple; beaker gets warm ✓✓\nCa(s) + 2H₂O(l) → Ca(OH)₂(aq) + H₂(g) ✓\nALLOW Ca(OH)₂(s)\nIGNORE 'hydrogen gas given off' as an observation` },
      { q: `Carbon dioxide and water are both produced in the body by respiration. A carbon dioxide molecule is linear, but a water molecule is bent with a bond angle of about 104.5°.\n\nExplain the difference in shape between these two molecules.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `CO₂: two groups of electrons (two double bonds) around C, no lone pairs, so they repel to 180° / linear ✓\nH₂O: four electron pairs around O (two bonding pairs, two lone pairs) ✓\nLone pairs repel more than bonding pairs, so the angle is less than 109.5° ✓\nALLOW 'two areas of electron density' for CO₂` },
      { q: `Epsom salts are hydrated magnesium sulfate, MgSO₄·xH₂O, used in baths to soothe muscles. A student heated 2.46 g of Epsom salts in a crucible until the mass was constant. The mass of anhydrous MgSO₄ remaining was 1.20 g.\n\nCalculate the value of x. Show your working.\n\nAr: H 1.0, O 16.0, Mg 24.3, S 32.1`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Amount MgSO₄ = 1.20 ÷ 120.4 = 9.97 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nMass water = 1.26 g; amount H₂O = 1.26 ÷ 18.0 = 0.0700 mol ✓\nx = 0.0700 ÷ 9.97 × 10⁻³ = 7.02, so x = 7 ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nECF from incorrect amounts` },
      { q: `About 65% of the mass of the human body is oxygen. A typical adult has a mass of 70 kg (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the number of oxygen atoms in this person's body. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nAr: O 16.0; Avogadro constant NA = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mass of oxygen = 0.65 × 70 000 = 45 500 g ✓\nAmount of O atoms = 45 500 ÷ 16.0 = 2840 mol (2843.75) ✓\nNumber of atoms = 2843.75 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.71 × 10²⁷ ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nDO NOT ALLOW use of Mr = 32 unless answer doubled (ECF for 2 marks if 8.56 × 10²⁶)` },
      { q: `The melting points of some Period 3 elements are given below.\n\nNa 98 °C\nSi 1414 °C\nP (white, P₄) 44 °C\n\nExplain the differences in melting point between sodium, silicon and phosphorus in terms of their structure and bonding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Na: giant metallic lattice with (relatively weak) metallic bonding — attraction between positive ions and delocalised electrons ✓\nSi: giant covalent (network) structure ✓\nMany strong covalent bonds must be broken to melt Si, so very high melting point ✓\nP: simple molecular (P₄), only weak intermolecular (instantaneous dipole–induced dipole) bonds broken on melting, so low melting point ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW covalent bonds broken when P melts` },
      { q: `A student needs to make 250.0 cm³ of a standard solution of sodium carbonate, using about 2.65 g of the anhydrous solid, for use in titrations.\n\nDescribe how the student should prepare this solution so that its concentration is known accurately.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Weigh the solid in a weighing bottle on a (2 d.p. or better) balance, transfer to a beaker and reweigh the bottle / record mass by difference ✓\nDissolve in a small volume of distilled water (stirring) ✓\nTransfer to a 250 cm³ volumetric flask, rinsing the beaker and glass rod into the flask with distilled water ✓\nMake up to the mark with distilled water so the bottom of the meniscus is on the line, stopper and invert to mix ✓` },
      { q: `Astronomers study the elements in stars using both emission spectra and absorption spectra.\n\nDescribe how an atomic absorption spectrum differs in appearance from an atomic emission spectrum of the same element, and explain how each type of spectrum is produced.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Emission: coloured lines on a black/dark background ✓\nAbsorption: black lines on a continuous (coloured) spectrum, at the same frequencies as the emission lines ✓\nEmission: electrons that have been excited fall to lower energy levels, giving out photons of specific frequency ✓\nAbsorption: electrons absorb photons of specific frequency (ΔE = hν) and are promoted to higher energy levels ✓` },
      { q: `Carbonate ions, CO₃²⁻, and sulfate ions, SO₄²⁻, are found in minerals such as chalk and gypsum.\n\nIn CO₃²⁻ there are three groups of bonding electrons around the carbon atom and no lone pairs.\nIn SO₄²⁻ there are four groups of bonding electrons around the sulfur atom and no lone pairs.\n\nPredict the shape and bond angle of each ion. Explain your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `CO₃²⁻: trigonal planar, 120° ✓\nSO₄²⁻: tetrahedral, 109.5° ✓\nGroups of electrons repel each other ✓\nAnd get as far apart as possible / minimise repulsion (and with no lone pairs all repulsions are equal) ✓\nShape and angle both needed for each of the first two marks` },
      { q: `A technician has three unlabelled bottles containing solutions of magnesium chloride, calcium chloride and barium chloride. A student has dilute sodium hydroxide solution and dilute sodium sulfate solution available.\n\nDescribe how the student could use these two reagents to identify each solution. Include the expected observations.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Add sodium sulfate solution to a sample of each: barium chloride gives a (dense) white precipitate ✓\nMagnesium and calcium chloride give no precipitate (or only slight with Ca) ✓\nAdd sodium hydroxide solution to fresh samples of the other two: magnesium chloride gives a white precipitate (Mg(OH)₂ is sparingly soluble) ✓\nCalcium chloride gives little or only slight precipitate (Ca(OH)₂ more soluble) — so the two are distinguished ✓\nALLOW correct ionic equations as support` },
      { q: `Farmers spread calcium oxide on acidic fields. A student adds water to a sample of calcium oxide and then adds universal indicator. The student then adds dilute hydrochloric acid until the indicator turns green.\n\nWrite equations for the two reactions, and state the approximate pH of the solution after water is added and explain why.`, marks: 4, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ ✓\nCa(OH)₂ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + 2H₂O ✓\npH about 11–13 / alkaline ✓\nCalcium hydroxide dissolves to release OH⁻ ions ✓\nALLOW CaO + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O for the second equation\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `Phosphorus atoms are present in DNA and in ATP. The atomic number of phosphorus is 15.\n\nDraw an 'electrons-in-boxes' diagram for the 3s and 3p sub-shells of a phosphorus atom. Then state the number of unpaired electrons and explain why the 3p electrons are arranged in this way.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `3s box with two electrons of opposite spin ✓\nThree 3p boxes each with one electron, all with the same spin (parallel arrows) ✓\nThree unpaired electrons ✓\nElectrons occupy orbitals singly before pairing because electrons repel / pairing requires more energy ✓\nALLOW arrows all up or all down in 3p` },
      { q: `A student found the mass of calcium carbonate in an indigestion tablet. The tablet was added to 50.0 cm³ of 0.500 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid, an excess. When the reaction had finished, all of the resulting solution was titrated with 0.200 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide; 22.50 cm³ was needed to neutralise the excess acid.\n\nCaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)\nHCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) → NaCl(aq) + H₂O(l)\n\nCalculate the mass, in g, of calcium carbonate in the tablet. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nAr: C 12.0, O 16.0, Ca 40.1`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Amount HCl at start = 0.500 × 50.0 ÷ 1000 = 0.0250 mol ✓\nAmount NaOH = amount excess HCl = 0.200 × 22.50 ÷ 1000 = 4.50 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nAmount HCl that reacted with CaCO₃ = 0.0250 – 0.00450 = 0.0205 mol ✓\nAmount CaCO₃ = 0.0205 ÷ 2 = 0.01025 mol ✓\nMass = 0.01025 × 100.1 = 1.03 g ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nECF throughout` },
      { q: `The absorption spectrum of a star shows dark lines at the following frequencies (data written for this practice question):\n\nLine 1: 6.17 × 10¹⁴ Hz\nLine 2: 4.57 × 10¹⁴ Hz\nLine 3: 5.09 × 10¹⁴ Hz\n\nWavelengths of some lines from laboratory emission spectra:\nhydrogen: 486 nm and 656 nm\nhelium: 447 nm and 588 nm\nsodium: 589 nm\n\nCalculate the wavelength, in nm, of each absorption line and deduce which elements are present in the star's atmosphere.\n\nc = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹`, marks: 5, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `λ = c ÷ ν used ✓\nLine 1 = 486 nm ✓\nLine 2 = 656 nm ✓\nLine 3 = 589 nm ✓\nHydrogen (lines 1 and 2) and sodium (line 3) are present ✓\nALLOW ECF for element identification from calculated wavelengths\nDO NOT ALLOW helium for line 3 (589 nm matches sodium, not 588 nm)` },
      { q: `A student investigated the thermal stability of Group 2 carbonates. 1.0 g of magnesium carbonate was heated in a boiling tube with a Bunsen burner, and the gas was bubbled into limewater. The time taken for the limewater to turn cloudy was recorded. The experiment was repeated with 1.0 g of calcium carbonate and then 1.0 g of barium carbonate.\n\nEvaluate this method and suggest improvements that would make the results more valid and reliable.`, marks: 5, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Any five from:\nEqual masses do not contain equal amounts (mol) of carbonate; use equal amounts in mol ✓\nBunsen flame height/heat output may differ between runs; keep the same flame/distance or use the same burner setting ✓\nSame volume (and concentration) of limewater must be used ✓\n'Cloudy' is subjective; use a cross under the tube or a light sensor/colorimeter ✓\nBaCO₃ may not decompose noticeably at Bunsen temperature, so no result in a reasonable time; set a maximum time ✓\nSuck-back risk: remove delivery tube from limewater before stopping heating ✓\nRepeat each experiment and calculate a mean to improve reliability/identify anomalies ✓\nUse the same particle size/powder ✓` },
      { q: `*An article on the origins of the elements states: 'We are made of star stuff — almost every atom in your body other than hydrogen was made inside a star.'\n\nDescribe how elements are formed in stars, and explain how scientists use spectroscopy to find out which elements are present in stars.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nFormation of elements (fusion)\n• Big Bang produced mainly hydrogen (and helium)\n• In star cores, hydrogen nuclei fuse to form helium, e.g. ¹H + ¹H → ²H + ⁰₊₁e\n• Very high temperature and pressure needed so nuclei overcome repulsion between positive charges\n• Later, helium nuclei fuse to form carbon and oxygen; heavier stars make heavier elements; elements heavier than iron mainly in supernovae\nSpectroscopy\n• Electrons in atoms occupy fixed energy levels\n• White light from the star passes through the outer layers; atoms absorb specific frequencies, promoting electrons: ΔE = hν\n• Absorption spectrum: dark lines on continuous spectrum\n• Line pattern unique to each element; compared with laboratory emission spectra (lines at same frequencies)\nEvidence\n• Helium identified in the Sun from lines before being found on Earth\n• Line intensities indicate relative abundance\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `*Many molecules and ions in the body have characteristic shapes. Three examples are:\n\nmethane, CH₄, made by gut bacteria\nwater, H₂O\nthe hydroxonium ion, H₃O⁺, present in stomach acid\n\nUse electron pair repulsion theory to explain the shapes and bond angles of these three species. Include a description of how the dative covalent bond in H₃O⁺ forms.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nTheory\n• Groups of electrons (bonding pairs and lone pairs) repel and get as far apart as possible\n• Lone pairs repel more than bonding pairs\nShapes and angles\n• CH₄: four bonding pairs, tetrahedral, 109.5°\n• H₂O: two bonding pairs, two lone pairs, bent/V-shaped, about 104.5°\n• H₃O⁺: three bonding pairs, one lone pair, pyramidal, about 107°\n• Bond angle decreases as number of lone pairs increases\nDative bond\n• A lone pair on the oxygen of H₂O is donated to H⁺\n• Both electrons in the shared pair come from the same atom (O)\n• Once formed, all three O–H bonds are identical\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `*A student wants to find the concentration of calcium hydroxide in a sample of limewater by titration with hydrochloric acid of known concentration (about 0.05 mol dm⁻³).\n\nCa(OH)₂(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + 2H₂O(l)\n\nDescribe how the student should carry out the experiment to obtain accurate results. Include the apparatus and method, how to ensure reliable results, and how to process the results to calculate the concentration.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nApparatus and method\n• Filter limewater to remove any undissolved Ca(OH)₂\n• Rinse pipette with limewater and transfer 25.0 cm³ to a conical flask using a pipette filler\n• Add a few drops of indicator, e.g. phenolphthalein (pink to colourless) or methyl orange (yellow to orange)\n• Rinse burette with acid, fill, remove air bubble from the jet, read at bottom of meniscus\nReliability and accuracy\n• Carry out a rough titration, then add acid dropwise near the end point, swirling\n• White tile under flask to see colour change\n• Repeat until concordant titres (within 0.10 cm³)\n• Wash sides of flask with distilled water near the end point\nProcessing\n• Mean of concordant titres\n• Amount HCl = concentration × titre ÷ 1000\n• Amount Ca(OH)₂ = amount HCl ÷ 2\n• Concentration = amount ÷ 0.0250 dm³\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `*Group 2 compounds are widely used in medicine: magnesium hydroxide as an antacid, calcium carbonate in indigestion tablets and calcium supplements, and barium sulfate in barium meals.\n\nDescribe and explain the trends down Group 2, from magnesium to barium, in the reactions of the elements with water, the solubility of the hydroxides and the thermal stability of the carbonates. Include equations where appropriate.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nReaction with water\n• Reactivity increases down the group: Mg very slow with cold water (fast with steam), Ca steady fizzing, Ba vigorous\n• e.g. Ca + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + H₂; Mg + H₂O → MgO + H₂ (steam)\n• Atoms larger, outer electrons further from nucleus and more shielded, so more easily lost (ionisation enthalpy decreases)\nSolubility of hydroxides\n• Increases down the group: Mg(OH)₂ sparingly soluble, Ba(OH)₂ fairly soluble\n• Solutions more alkaline down the group\n• Mg(OH)₂ safe as antacid because low solubility means not strongly alkaline\nThermal stability of carbonates\n• Increases down the group: MgCO₃ decomposes most easily, BaCO₃ least\n• e.g. CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂\n• Ionic radius increases, so charge density decreases; less polarisation of carbonate ion\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'DF': {
    name: 'Developing Fuels',
    questions: [
      { q: `Petrol is a blend of hydrocarbons. One component added to raise the octane number has the structural formula (CH₃)₃CCH₂CH(CH₃)₂.\n\nGive the systematic name of this compound.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `2,2,4-trimethylpentane ✓\nALLOW missing commas or hyphens if unambiguous\nDO NOT ALLOW 2,4,4-trimethylpentane\nIGNORE isooctane` },
      { q: `In an oil refinery, long-chain alkanes from crude oil are cracked over a zeolite catalyst to give shorter alkanes for petrol and alkenes for the chemical industry. Decane, C₁₀H₂₂, can be cracked to give octane and one other product.\n\nWrite the equation for this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄ ✓\nALLOW structural or displayed formulae\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `When a car engine is running with too little air, some of the octane in the petrol burns incompletely. One product is a toxic gas that combines with haemoglobin in the blood.\n\nWrite a balanced equation for the incomplete combustion of octane, C₈H₁₈, to form this toxic gas and water only.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₈H₁₈ + 8½O₂ → 8CO + 9H₂O ✓\nALLOW multiples, e.g. 2C₈H₁₈ + 17O₂ → 16CO + 18H₂O\nIGNORE state symbols\nDO NOT ALLOW equations that include C or CO₂` },
      { q: `A catalytic converter in a car exhaust removes two pollutant gases at the same time by making them react together on the surface of a platinum–rhodium catalyst. The products are both gases found naturally in air.\n\nWrite the equation for the reaction between carbon monoxide and nitrogen monoxide in a catalytic converter.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂ ✓\nALLOW multiples\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `Reforming converts straight-chain alkanes into cyclic hydrocarbons, which burn more smoothly in petrol engines. In one reforming reaction, hexane is converted into cyclohexane.\n\nWrite the equation for this reaction, using molecular formulae.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₆H₁₄ → C₆H₁₂ + H₂ ✓\nALLOW skeletal formula for cyclohexane\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `Cracking produces pent-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₂CH₃, which can be added to petrol in small amounts. In one isomer of pent-2-ene, the CH₃ group and the CH₂CH₃ group are on opposite sides of the C=C double bond.\n\nName this isomer, including its E/Z prefix.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `(E)-pent-2-ene ✓\nALLOW E-pent-2-ene / trans-pent-2-ene\nDO NOT ALLOW Z-pent-2-ene` },
      { q: `Bioethanol for blending into petrol (E10 petrol contains up to 10% ethanol) is made by fermenting glucose from sugar cane or sugar beet using yeast.\n\nWrite the equation for the fermentation of glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂ ✓\nALLOW C₂H₆O or CH₃CH₂OH for ethanol\nIGNORE state symbols and reference to yeast/enzymes` },
      { q: `The standard enthalpy change of formation of octane is −250 kJ mol⁻¹. This value is not found by reacting carbon with hydrogen in a calorimeter; it is calculated from enthalpy changes of combustion.\n\nSuggest one reason why the enthalpy change of formation of octane cannot be measured directly.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Carbon and hydrogen do not react together (under standard conditions) to form octane / a mixture of many other hydrocarbons would be formed / reaction is too slow / has too high an activation enthalpy ✓\nIGNORE 'it is dangerous'` },
      { q: `Fuel manufacturers compare fuels using standard enthalpy changes. Data books list the standard enthalpy change of formation of ethanol as −277 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nState what is meant by the term standard enthalpy change of formation.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `Enthalpy change when one mole of a compound/substance is formed from its elements ✓\nWith all substances in their standard states under standard conditions (100 kPa and a stated temperature, usually 298 K) ✓\nALLOW 1 atm / 101 kPa for pressure\nDO NOT ALLOW 'energy needed' for the first mark` },
      { q: `Ethanol, C₂H₅OH, is used as a biofuel. Its standard enthalpy change of formation is −277 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nWrite the equation, with state symbols, for the reaction that represents the standard enthalpy change of formation of ethanol.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `2C + 3H₂ + ½O₂ → C₂H₅OH with correct formulae and balancing (one mole of ethanol only) ✓\nState symbols: C(s), H₂(g), O₂(g), C₂H₅OH(l) ✓\nALLOW C(graphite) for C(s)\nDO NOT ALLOW multiples such as 4C + 6H₂ + O₂ → 2C₂H₅OH for the first mark` },
      { q: `A student burns ethanol in a spirit burner under a copper can of water. The temperature of the water rises from 19.0 °C to 40.5 °C, a rise of 21.5 °C. The mass of ethanol burned is 0.690 g. The thermometer reads to ±0.5 °C and the balance reads to ±0.005 g.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the temperature rise and in the mass of ethanol burned.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Temperature rise: (2 × 0.5) ÷ 21.5 × 100 = 4.7% ✓\nMass: (2 × 0.005) ÷ 0.690 × 100 = 1.4% ✓\nALLOW 4.65% and 1.45%\nALLOW ECF if only one reading uncertainty used for both: 2.3% and 0.72% scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `Using average bond enthalpies, a student calculates the enthalpy change of combustion of methane as −818 kJ mol⁻¹. The data book value for the standard enthalpy change of combustion of methane is −890 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nExplain why the two values are different.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Average bond enthalpies are averaged over many different compounds, so are not exact for the bonds in methane/CO₂/H₂O ✓\nThe bond enthalpy calculation gives water as a gas (H₂O(g)); under standard conditions water is a liquid so the condensation enthalpy is not included ✓\nALLOW bond enthalpies apply only to gaseous species` },
      { q: `Propane is used as a fuel for camping stoves. A student mixes 50 cm³ of propane with 300 cm³ of oxygen and ignites the mixture. Complete combustion occurs:\nC₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(l)\nAll volumes are measured at room temperature and pressure.\n\nCalculate the total volume of gas remaining after the reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `O₂ used = 250 cm³ so 50 cm³ O₂ left over; CO₂ formed = 150 cm³ ✓\nTotal = 200 cm³ ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for 550 cm³ or 350 cm³ (water counted as gas / excess O₂ omitted with water included)\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `Data for some standard enthalpy changes of formation are given.\nΔfH (C₂H₅OH(l)) = −277.1 kJ mol⁻¹\nΔfH (CO₂(g)) = −393.5 kJ mol⁻¹\nΔfH (H₂O(l)) = −285.8 kJ mol⁻¹\nC₂H₅OH(l) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l)\n\nCalculate the standard enthalpy change of combustion of ethanol, a biofuel.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔcH = [2(−393.5) + 3(−285.8)] − (−277.1) ✓\n= −1367 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW −1367.3\nALLOW 1 mark for +1367 (sign error)\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `Pent-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₂CH₃, is formed when fractions from crude oil are cracked. It exists as two E/Z isomers, but its structural isomer pent-1-ene, CH₂=CHCH₂CH₂CH₃, does not.\n\nExplain why pent-2-ene has E/Z isomers but pent-1-ene does not.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `There is no (free) rotation about the C=C double bond ✓\nIn pent-2-ene each carbon of the C=C has two different groups attached, but in pent-1-ene one carbon of the C=C has two identical (H) atoms ✓\nALLOW π bond prevents rotation` },
      { q: `Methane, CH₄, is the main component of natural gas, used as a fuel in homes. Each carbon atom in an alkane is bonded to four other atoms.\n\nState the bond angle around the carbon atom in methane and explain why the molecule has this shape.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `109.5° ✓\nFour (bonding) pairs of electrons around carbon repel each other equally and get as far apart as possible (tetrahedral) ✓\nALLOW 109° / 109.5° ± 0.5°\nDO NOT ALLOW 'atoms repel' or 'bonds repel' without reference to electron pairs` },
      { q: `Octane numbers measure the tendency of a petrol to auto-ignite (knock) in an engine. Heptane has an octane number of 0, while its branched isomer 2,2,3-trimethylbutane has an octane number of about 112.\n\nExplain why refineries isomerise straight-chain alkanes before adding them to petrol.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Branched alkanes have higher octane numbers / are less likely to auto-ignite ✓\nSo petrol burns more smoothly/efficiently in the engine with less knocking (and less damage to the engine) ✓\nIGNORE 'burn better'` },
      { q: `Leaded petrol was once used in cars. Cars fitted with catalytic converters must only use unleaded petrol, because lead compounds in the exhaust gases poison the catalyst.\n\nExplain how lead poisons the catalyst in a catalytic converter.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Lead (compounds) is adsorbed (strongly) onto the surface of the catalyst ✓\nThis blocks the active sites so CO/NO can no longer be adsorbed (catalyst becomes less effective) ✓\nALLOW lead bonds to the surface and is not desorbed` },
      { q: `Air is about 78% nitrogen. Nitrogen is very unreactive, yet car exhaust gases contain significant amounts of nitrogen monoxide, NO.\n\nExplain how nitrogen monoxide forms in a car engine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `N₂ + O₂ → 2NO ✓\nThe very high temperature (of combustion/spark) in the engine provides the energy to overcome the high activation enthalpy / break the strong N≡N triple bond ✓\nALLOW nitrogen from the air (not from the fuel)` },
      { q: `A student measures the enthalpy change of neutralisation for the reaction used to monitor acidic emissions. 25.0 cm³ of 2.00 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid is mixed with 25.0 cm³ of 2.00 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide in a polystyrene cup. The temperature rises by 13.4 °C.\nAssume the density of the solution is 1.00 g cm⁻³ and its specific heat capacity is 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of neutralisation, in kJ mol⁻¹. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `q = 50.0 × 4.18 × 13.4 = 2801 J ✓\nn(H₂O) = 0.0250 × 2.00 = 0.0500 mol ✓\nΔneutH = −2.801 ÷ 0.0500 = −56.0 kJ mol⁻¹ (negative sign and 3 sf) ✓\nALLOW ECF throughout\nDO NOT ALLOW mass of 25.0 g\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Octane is a major component of petrol. Its enthalpy change of formation can be found using a Hess cycle.\nC₈H₁₈(l) + 12½O₂(g) → 8CO₂(g) + 9H₂O(l)    ΔcH = −5470 kJ mol⁻¹\nΔfH (CO₂(g)) = −393.5 kJ mol⁻¹\nΔfH (H₂O(l)) = −285.8 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the standard enthalpy change of formation of octane.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Correct cycle or expression: ΔfH(C₈H₁₈) = 8ΔfH(CO₂) + 9ΔfH(H₂O) − ΔcH(C₈H₁₈) ✓\n= 8(−393.5) + 9(−285.8) + 5470 = −3148 − 2572.2 + 5470 ✓\n= −250 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW −250.2\nALLOW 2 marks for +250\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Natural gas is mainly methane. The equation shows its combustion with all substances as gases:\nCH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g)\nAverage bond enthalpies / kJ mol⁻¹: C–H 413, O=O 498, C=O 805, O–H 464\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of this reaction using the bond enthalpies.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Bonds broken = (4 × 413) + (2 × 498) = 2648 kJ ✓\nBonds made = (2 × 805) + (4 × 464) = 3466 kJ ✓\nΔrH = 2648 − 3466 = −818 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW ECF for final mark\nALLOW 2 marks for +818\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student burns ethanol in a spirit burner under a copper can containing water. She obtains a value of −599 kJ mol⁻¹ for the enthalpy change of combustion of ethanol. The data book value is −1367 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nSuggest three reasons why the student's value is much less exothermic than the data book value.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any three from:\nHeat/energy lost to the surroundings (air) rather than to the water ✓\nIncomplete combustion of ethanol (soot seen on the can) ✓\nEnergy used to heat the copper can/apparatus not included in the calculation ✓\nEthanol evaporates from the wick so mass burned is overestimated ✓\nConditions not standard / water formed as vapour, not liquid ✓\nIGNORE 'human error' / 'heat lost' without saying where` },
      { q: `Cracking reactions can be studied using enthalpy changes of formation.\nC₁₀H₂₂(l) → C₈H₁₈(l) + C₂H₄(g)\nΔfH / kJ mol⁻¹: C₁₀H₂₂(l) −300.9; C₈H₁₈(l) −250.1; C₂H₄(g) +52.4\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change for this cracking reaction and use your answer to explain why the refinery must supply heat to the reactor.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔrH = [(−250.1) + (+52.4)] − (−300.9) ✓\n= +103 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nPositive so reaction is endothermic; energy must be supplied (to break bonds / keep the reaction going) ✓\nALLOW +103.2\nALLOW ECF for third mark from a calculated value` },
      { q: `Burning petrol releases carbon dioxide, a greenhouse gas. Assume that petrol is pure octane, C₈H₁₈ (Mr = 114.0), with a density of 0.70 g cm⁻³, and that it burns completely.\nC₈H₁₈ + 12½O₂ → 8CO₂ + 9H₂O\n\nCalculate the mass of carbon dioxide, in kg, produced when 1.00 dm³ of petrol is burned. Mr of CO₂ = 44.0`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mass of octane = 1000 × 0.70 = 700 g; n = 700 ÷ 114.0 = 6.14 mol ✓\nn(CO₂) = 8 × 6.14 = 49.1 mol ✓\nMass CO₂ = 49.1 × 44.0 = 2160 g = 2.16 kg ✓\nALLOW 2.2 kg\nALLOW ECF\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Petrol vapour and air do not react until ignited by a spark, but the combustion of petrol is very exothermic. A catalyst lowers the activation enthalpy of a reaction.\n\nDraw a labelled enthalpy profile for the combustion of petrol, showing the activation enthalpy and the enthalpy change of reaction, ΔH.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Axes labelled enthalpy (y) and progress of reaction (x), with products below reactants ✓\nActivation enthalpy shown from reactants to top of the hump ✓\nΔH shown from reactants to products, arrow pointing down / labelled as negative ✓\nALLOW Ea for activation enthalpy\nDO NOT ALLOW energy profile with products above reactants` },
      { q: `Hydrogen is being developed as a fuel for buses. The equation for its combustion with water formed as a gas is:\n2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(g)\nAverage bond enthalpies / kJ mol⁻¹: H–H 436, O=O 498, O–H 464\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of combustion of hydrogen, in kJ per mole of hydrogen, using these bond enthalpies.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Bonds broken = (2 × 436) + 498 = 1370 kJ ✓\nBonds made = 4 × 464 = 1856 kJ ✓\nΔH = −486 kJ for 2 mol H₂, so −243 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW 2 marks for −486 kJ mol⁻¹\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Fuel for a vehicle must release a lot of energy without being too heavy.\nΔcH / kJ mol⁻¹: hydrogen −286; octane −5470; ethanol −1367\nMr: H₂ 2.0; C₈H₁₈ 114.0; C₂H₅OH 46.0\n\nCalculate the energy released per gram for each fuel and identify which fuel releases most energy per gram.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Hydrogen: 286 ÷ 2.0 = 143 kJ g⁻¹ ✓\nOctane: 5470 ÷ 114.0 = 48.0 kJ g⁻¹ AND ethanol: 1367 ÷ 46.0 = 29.7 kJ g⁻¹ ✓\nHydrogen releases most energy per gram ✓\nALLOW 142 kJ g⁻¹ (using 2.016)\nALLOW negative signs` },
      { q: `A student burns ethanol, C₂H₅OH (Mr = 46.0), in a spirit burner to heat 100 cm³ of water in a copper can. The temperature of the water rises by 21.5 °C. The mass of ethanol burned is 0.690 g.\nSpecific heat capacity of water = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹; density of water = 1.00 g cm⁻³\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of combustion of ethanol in kJ mol⁻¹. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `q = 100 × 4.18 × 21.5 = 8987 J ✓\nn(ethanol) = 0.690 ÷ 46.0 = 0.0150 mol ✓\nΔcH = 8.987 ÷ 0.0150 = 599 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nAnswer given as −599 kJ mol⁻¹ (negative sign and 3 sf) ✓\nALLOW ECF throughout\nDO NOT ALLOW mass of ethanol used in q = mcΔT` },
      { q: `In a car engine, octane burns completely in air. Air contains 21% oxygen by volume.\nC₈H₁₈ + 12½O₂ → 8CO₂ + 9H₂O\nMr of octane = 114.0; molar volume of gas at RTP = 24.0 dm³ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the volume of air, in dm³ at RTP, needed for the complete combustion of 1.00 g of octane. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(octane) = 1.00 ÷ 114.0 = 8.77 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nn(O₂) = 12.5 × 8.77 × 10⁻³ = 0.110 mol ✓\nV(O₂) = 0.110 × 24.0 = 2.63 dm³ ✓\nV(air) = 2.63 ÷ 0.21 = 12.5 dm³ ✓\nALLOW ECF throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A catalytic converter contains a thin layer of platinum and rhodium spread over a ceramic honeycomb. The catalyst is a heterogeneous catalyst for the reaction 2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂.\n\nExplain how the catalyst speeds up this reaction, and why the metal is spread thinly over a honeycomb.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `CO and NO molecules are adsorbed onto the (active sites on the) metal surface ✓\nBonds in the reactants are weakened / reactants held close together in a favourable orientation, so the activation enthalpy is lower ✓\nProducts (CO₂ and N₂) are desorbed, freeing the surface for further molecules ✓\nHoneycomb/thin layer gives a large surface area so more active sites while using less expensive metal ✓\nALLOW 'heterogeneous: catalyst in a different phase from reactants' as part of the first mark` },
      { q: `Ethene, C₂H₄, is produced by cracking and is used to make poly(ethene) and ethanol.\n\nDescribe the bonding between the two carbon atoms in ethene and state the shape and bond angles around each carbon atom.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `The C=C double bond consists of one σ bond and one π bond ✓\nσ bond formed by overlap of orbitals directly between the nuclei (end-on) ✓\nπ bond formed by sideways overlap of p orbitals, above and below the plane of the molecule ✓\nTrigonal planar around each carbon, bond angle 120° ✓\nALLOW 'planar' for trigonal planar` },
      { q: `Hexane, C₆H₁₄, from crude oil has a low octane number. Isomerisation converts it into branched isomers with higher octane numbers.\n\nDraw the skeletal formulae of two branched structural isomers of hexane and give the systematic name of each.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Any two of the following, each correctly drawn ✓✓ and named ✓✓:\n2-methylpentane\n3-methylpentane\n2,2-dimethylbutane\n2,3-dimethylbutane\nALLOW structural or displayed formulae\nDO NOT ALLOW hexane or unbranched structures\nDO NOT ALLOW names such as 4-methylpentane or 2-ethylbutane` },
      { q: `A household gas boiler burns methane to heat water. 12.0 dm³ of methane, measured at RTP, is burned. Only 60% of the energy released is transferred to the water, which is heated from 15 °C to 60 °C.\nΔcH (CH₄) = −890 kJ mol⁻¹; molar volume at RTP = 24.0 dm³ mol⁻¹; specific heat capacity of water = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the mass of water, in kg, that is heated. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(CH₄) = 12.0 ÷ 24.0 = 0.500 mol ✓\nEnergy released = 0.500 × 890 = 445 kJ ✓\nEnergy to water = 0.60 × 445 = 267 kJ ✓\nm = 267 000 ÷ (4.18 × 45) ✓\n= 1420 g = 1.42 kg ✓\nALLOW ECF throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student finds the Mr of the fuel in a disposable lighter. She releases gas from the lighter into an upturned measuring cylinder full of water. The lighter loses 0.120 g and 50.0 cm³ of gas is collected at 20 °C and 100 kPa.\nR = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the Mr of the gas using pV = nRT, and suggest why the value is slightly higher than that of butane (58.0).`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Conversion: V = 50.0 × 10⁻⁶ m³ AND T = 293 K ✓\nn = pV ÷ RT = (100 000 × 50.0 × 10⁻⁶) ÷ (8.314 × 293) ✓\n= 2.05 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nMr = 0.120 ÷ 2.05 × 10⁻³ = 58.5 ✓\nSome gas dissolves in the water / escapes before collection / lighter is wet so mass loss too high, so volume measured is too small ✓\nALLOW ECF\nALLOW 58.4–58.5` },
      { q: `Bioethanol from sugar cane is blended into petrol. Some people claim that bioethanol is a 'carbon-neutral' fuel.\nData: ΔcH (ethanol) = −1367 kJ mol⁻¹ (Mr 46.0); ΔcH (octane) = −5470 kJ mol⁻¹ (Mr 114.0)\n\nEvaluate the use of bioethanol in place of petrol as a fuel for cars.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1: Carbon neutrality\n• CO₂ absorbed by sugar cane during photosynthesis (6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂)\n• Same amount released by fermentation (2CO₂) and combustion of ethanol (C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O)\n• Not truly carbon neutral: fossil fuels used for farming, fertiliser, harvesting, distillation, transport\nStrand 2: Energy\n• Ethanol releases 29.7 kJ g⁻¹, octane 48.0 kJ g⁻¹\n• So more fuel mass/larger tank needed per journey; lower energy density\n• Per mole of CO₂ released the energies are similar (about 684 kJ)\nStrand 3: Other factors\n• Renewable resource; fossil fuels finite\n• Land used for fuel instead of food; deforestation releases CO₂ and destroys habitats\n• Ethanol contains oxygen so burns more completely, less CO/unburnt hydrocarbons; no sulfur so no SO₂\n• Ethanol absorbs water/corrodes some engine parts, so only blends like E10 used in standard engines\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A city council is replacing its diesel and petrol buses with buses that run on hydrogen. Exhaust from petrol engines contains carbon monoxide, oxides of nitrogen, sulfur dioxide, unburnt hydrocarbons and particulates. Most hydrogen today is made by steam reforming of methane: CH₄ + H₂O → CO + 3H₂.\n\nEvaluate the advantages and disadvantages of hydrogen compared with petrol as a fuel for buses. Refer to pollutants and how they form.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1: Pollutants from petrol and how they form\n• CO and particulates (soot) from incomplete combustion when oxygen supply limited\n• Unburnt hydrocarbons from fuel that passes through without burning\n• NOx from N₂ and O₂ in air at high engine temperature\n• SO₂ from sulfur impurities in the fuel; acid rain; CO toxic; particulates cause respiratory problems\nStrand 2: Hydrogen as fuel\n• Only product of combustion/fuel cell is water: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O\n• No CO₂, CO, SO₂, particulates or unburnt hydrocarbons at point of use\n• High energy per gram (143 kJ g⁻¹ vs 48 kJ g⁻¹ for octane)\n• NOx can still form if hydrogen is burned in air at high temperature (not in a fuel cell)\nStrand 3: Disadvantages / production\n• Steam reforming uses methane (fossil fuel) and produces CO/CO₂, so not carbon neutral overall\n• Electrolysis of water needs much energy; only low-carbon if renewable electricity used\n• Gas with low density so large volume; must be stored under high pressure or liquefied; flammable/explosive\n• Few refuelling stations; expensive infrastructure\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A fuel company wants to know whether the enthalpy change of combustion of alcohols increases by a constant amount for each extra CH₂ group. A technician has methanol, ethanol, propan-1-ol and butan-1-ol, spirit burners, a copper can, a thermometer and a balance.\n\nDescribe how the experiment should be carried out and how the results should be processed to answer the question. Include ways to make the results as accurate as possible.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1: Method\n• Measure known volume/mass of water (e.g. 100 cm³) into copper can; record initial temperature\n• Weigh spirit burner with alcohol and cap before and after burning\n• Burn until temperature rises by about 20 °C; stir; record maximum temperature\n• Keep same volume of water, same distance from flame, same temperature rise for each alcohol\nStrand 2: Processing\n• q = mcΔT using mass of water\n• Moles of alcohol = mass burned ÷ Mr\n• ΔcH = −q ÷ n (in kJ mol⁻¹)\n• Plot ΔcH against number of carbon atoms; a straight line shows a constant increase per CH₂ (bonds C–C and C–H broken, extra CO₂ and H₂O formed)\nStrand 3: Accuracy\n• Use draught shields / lid / insulate can to reduce heat loss\n• Replace cap quickly to reduce evaporation of alcohol\n• Adjust wick so flame is not sooty (reduce incomplete combustion)\n• Use more precise thermometer / larger temperature rise to reduce percentage uncertainty; repeat and calculate mean\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'ES': {
    name: 'Elements from the Sea',
    questions: [
      { q: `Household bleach is made by bubbling chlorine through cold, dilute sodium hydroxide solution. The active ingredient has the formula NaClO.\n\nGive the systematic name of NaClO.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `sodium chlorate(I) ✓\nALLOW chlorate(I) ion for ClO⁻\nIGNORE sodium hypochlorite\nDO NOT ALLOW sodium chlorate OR chlorate(V) OR chloride` },
      { q: `A student investigates the halogens found in seawater. She adds a few drops of chlorine water to aqueous potassium iodide, then adds cyclohexane, shakes the mixture and lets the layers separate.\n\nState the colour of the upper (cyclohexane) layer.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `purple ✓\nALLOW violet OR pink-purple\nDO NOT ALLOW brown OR orange OR black` },
      { q: `A sample of evaporated seawater residue is dissolved in water and acidified with dilute nitric acid. Aqueous silver nitrate is added and a cream precipitate forms. The precipitate does not dissolve in dilute aqueous ammonia but does dissolve in concentrated aqueous ammonia.\n\nIdentify the halide ion present.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `bromide OR Br⁻ ✓\nALLOW silver bromide / AgBr is the precipitate\nDO NOT ALLOW bromine OR Br` },
      { q: `Concentrated sulfuric acid is added to solid potassium bromide in a fume cupboard. Steamy fumes of hydrogen bromide form, together with orange-brown fumes of bromine and a colourless gas with a choking smell. The colourless gas turns acidified potassium dichromate(VI) paper from orange to green.\n\nIdentify the colourless gas.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `sulfur dioxide OR SO₂ ✓\nIGNORE hydrogen bromide (already given)\nDO NOT ALLOW H₂S OR sulfur trioxide` },
      { q: `The chlorate(I) content of a bleach is found by adding excess acidified potassium iodide and titrating the iodine released with sodium thiosulfate solution. Near the end point, when the solution is pale yellow, starch solution is added.\n\nState the colour change at the end point.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `blue-black to colourless ✓\nALLOW blue OR black OR dark blue to colourless\nDO NOT ALLOW blue-black to yellow OR to clear (unless colourless also stated)` },
      { q: `In a demonstration of the properties of the hydrogen halides, the stopper is removed from a bottle of concentrated aqueous ammonia next to a gas jar of hydrogen chloride. A white smoke forms.\n\nWrite an equation, including state symbols, for the formation of the white smoke.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `NH₃(g) + HCl(g) → NH₄Cl(s) ✓\nState symbols required for the mark\nALLOW ⇌` },
      { q: `The halogens chlorine, bromine and iodine can all be obtained from seawater or seaweed. Their boiling points are shown below.\n\nchlorine, Cl₂: −34 °C\nbromine, Br₂: 59 °C\niodine, I₂: 184 °C\n\nExplain the trend in boiling points from chlorine to iodine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `number of electrons (in the molecule) increases down the group ✓\nso instantaneous dipole–induced dipole bonds are stronger, needing more energy to overcome ✓\nALLOW London forces OR dispersion forces OR id–id bonds\nDO NOT ALLOW breaking covalent bonds (scores 0 for second mark)\nIGNORE larger molecules / larger Mr without reference to electrons for first mark` },
      { q: `Chlorine is manufactured by the electrolysis of concentrated brine (aqueous sodium chloride) in a membrane cell. Chlorine forms at the anode, and hydrogen and hydroxide ions form at the cathode.\n\nWrite half-equations for the reactions at the anode and at the cathode.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `anode: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻ ✓\ncathode: 2H₂O + 2e⁻ → H₂ + 2OH⁻ ✓\nALLOW 2Cl⁻ − 2e⁻ → Cl₂\nALLOW cathode 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂\nIf correct equations are given for the wrong electrodes, award 1 mark\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `When chlorine is added to drinking water it reacts as follows.\n\nCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ HCl(aq) + HClO(aq)\n\nExplain, using oxidation states, why this is a disproportionation reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `oxidation state of Cl in Cl₂ is 0, in HCl is −1 and in HClO is +1 ✓\nthe same element (chlorine) is both oxidised and reduced (simultaneously) ✓\nALLOW Cl goes from 0 to −1 (reduced) and from 0 to +1 (oxidised) for both marks\nALLOW −I / +I` },
      { q: `In the Deacon process, hydrogen chloride produced as a by-product elsewhere on a chemical site is converted back into chlorine.\n\n4HCl(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2Cl₂(g) + 2H₂O(g)\n\nCalculate the atom economy for the production of chlorine by this process.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mr of reactants = (4 × 36.5) + 32.0 = 178(.0) AND Mr of 2Cl₂ = 142(.0) ✓\natom economy = 142 ÷ 178 × 100 = 79.8% ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW 79.78% to 79.8%\nDO NOT ALLOW 79.7% (truncation)` },
      { q: `Seawater contains about 65 mg dm⁻³ of bromide ions. In the first stage of bromine extraction, the seawater is acidified and chlorine is bubbled through it.\n\nWrite an ionic equation for the reaction and identify the oxidising agent.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂ ✓\noxidising agent: chlorine OR Cl₂ ✓\nIGNORE state symbols\nALLOW multiples\nDO NOT ALLOW Cl⁻ OR chloride as oxidising agent` },
      { q: `Hydrogen chloride gas, made in a chlor-alkali plant by burning hydrogen in chlorine, is very soluble in water and forms hydrochloric acid.\n\nWrite an equation for the reaction of hydrogen chloride with water and explain why HCl is described as an acid in this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻ ✓\nHCl donates a proton (H⁺) to water ✓\nALLOW HCl(aq) → H⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) for the first mark\nALLOW HCl is a proton donor\nIGNORE references to strong acid` },
      { q: `A student electrolyses concentrated aqueous sodium bromide using inert graphite electrodes.\n\nPredict the product formed at each electrode.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `cathode (negative electrode): hydrogen OR H₂ ✓\nanode (positive electrode): bromine OR Br₂ ✓\nALLOW OH⁻ / sodium hydroxide also forms at cathode\nDO NOT ALLOW sodium at the cathode\nDO NOT ALLOW oxygen at the anode` },
      { q: `A student carries out an iodine–thiosulfate titration to analyse a bleach sample (data written for this practice question). The burette reading is 0.50 cm³ at the start and 22.90 cm³ at the end. Each burette reading has an uncertainty of ±0.05 cm³.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the titre.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `titre = 22.40 cm³ AND total uncertainty = ±0.10 cm³ ✓\npercentage uncertainty = 0.10 ÷ 22.40 × 100 = 0.45% ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW 0.446%\nECF using ±0.05 only: 0.22% scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `In the Deacon process, hydrogen chloride is oxidised to chlorine over a copper(II) chloride catalyst at about 400 °C.\n\n4HCl(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2Cl₂(g) + 2H₂O(g)   ΔH = −114 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nExplain why a temperature of about 400 °C is used rather than a much lower or much higher temperature.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `forward reaction is exothermic so a higher temperature moves the equilibrium position to the left / lowers the equilibrium yield of chlorine ✓\nat a much lower temperature the rate is too slow (even with a catalyst) ✓\nso 400 °C is a compromise between (acceptable) rate and (acceptable) yield ✓\nALLOW catalyst not active enough at low temperature for second mark\nIGNORE cost of energy unless linked to compromise` },
      { q: `A chemical engineer is considering raising the operating pressure of a Deacon process reactor.\n\n4HCl(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2Cl₂(g) + 2H₂O(g)   ΔH = −114 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nPredict and explain the effect of increasing the pressure on the equilibrium yield of chlorine, and suggest one disadvantage of operating at very high pressure.`, marks: 3, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `equilibrium yield of chlorine increases / position of equilibrium moves to the right ✓\nthere are fewer moles (molecules) of gas on the right (4) than on the left (5) so the system shifts to reduce the pressure ✓\nhigh pressure is expensive (to generate) OR needs stronger / thicker equipment OR greater safety risk (e.g. of leaks of toxic chlorine / HCl) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW rate argument as the explanation for yield` },
      { q: `A chlor-alkali plant electrolyses brine. In one batch, 5.85 tonnes of sodium chloride were electrolysed and 3.20 tonnes of chlorine were collected (data written for this practice question).\n\n2NaCl(aq) + 2H₂O(l) → 2NaOH(aq) + Cl₂(g) + H₂(g)\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of chlorine.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(NaCl) = 5.85 × 10⁶ ÷ 58.5 = 1.00 × 10⁵ mol ✓\ntheoretical mass of Cl₂ = 0.500 × 10⁵ × 71.0 = 3.55 × 10⁶ g OR 3.55 tonnes ✓\npercentage yield = 3.20 ÷ 3.55 × 100 = 90.1% ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW working in tonnes throughout\nECF from incorrect mole ratio` },
      { q: `Concentrated sulfuric acid is added separately to solid sodium chloride and to solid sodium iodide. With sodium chloride, only steamy fumes of hydrogen chloride form. With sodium iodide, a purple vapour, a black solid and a gas smelling of bad eggs (hydrogen sulfide) form.\n\nExplain the difference in these reactions.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `iodide ions are stronger reducing agents than chloride ions ✓\n(because) iodide ions are larger so the outer electron is lost more easily / less strongly held by the nucleus ✓\niodide reduces sulfuric acid (S from +6) to H₂S (S at −2) while being oxidised to iodine; chloride cannot reduce H₂SO₄ ✓\nALLOW I⁻ is oxidised to I₂ and H₂SO₄ is reduced for third mark\nALLOW chloride only undergoes an acid–base reaction` },
      { q: `When chlorine is passed into hot, concentrated sodium hydroxide, a different product from household bleach is formed.\n\n3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O\n\nGive the oxidation state of chlorine in each chlorine-containing species, give the systematic name of NaClO₃ and state the type of redox reaction taking place.`, marks: 3, command: 'State', markScheme: `Cl₂ = 0, NaCl = −1, NaClO₃ = +5 ✓\nsodium chlorate(V) ✓\ndisproportionation ✓\nALLOW +V for +5\nDO NOT ALLOW sodium chlorate without (V)` },
      { q: `In the analysis of iodine extracted from seaweed, the iodine is titrated with sodium thiosulfate solution. The half-equation for the oxidation of thiosulfate is:\n\n2S₂O₃²⁻ → S₄O₆²⁻ + 2e⁻\n\nWrite the overall ionic equation for the reaction of iodine with thiosulfate ions, and calculate the oxidation state of sulfur in S₂O₃²⁻ and in S₄O₆²⁻.`, marks: 3, command: 'Write', markScheme: `I₂ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ → 2I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻ ✓\nS in S₂O₃²⁻ = +2 ✓\nS in S₄O₆²⁻ = +2.5 ✓\nALLOW +5/2 OR 2½\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `A student wants to show that a sample of rock salt contains chloride ions. She dissolves the salt in water, adds dilute nitric acid and then aqueous silver nitrate.\n\nExplain why dilute nitric acid is added first, why dilute hydrochloric acid would not be suitable, and describe how she could confirm that the precipitate is silver chloride rather than silver bromide.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `nitric acid removes / reacts with carbonate (or hydroxide) ions which would otherwise form a precipitate with Ag⁺ ✓\nhydrochloric acid adds chloride ions, which would give a white precipitate anyway ✓\nadd dilute aqueous ammonia: AgCl (white) dissolves, AgBr (cream) does not ✓\nALLOW sulfate or other interfering ions for first mark only if carbonate also implied\nALLOW AgBr only dissolves in concentrated ammonia` },
      { q: `A student compares the reactivity of the halogens by mixing aqueous solutions. She adds chlorine water, bromine water and aqueous iodine separately to aqueous potassium chloride, potassium bromide and potassium iodide, then adds cyclohexane to each tube and shakes.\n\nDescribe what she would observe in the tubes where a reaction occurs, and explain the trend in reactivity of the halogens shown.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `chlorine + bromide: cyclohexane layer turns orange / orange-red ✓\nchlorine + iodide AND bromine + iodide: cyclohexane layer turns purple / violet ✓\noxidising power (reactivity) decreases down the group: Cl₂ greater than Br₂ greater than I₂ ✓\nbecause atoms are larger / more shielding so an incoming electron is less strongly attracted to the nucleus ✓\nALLOW aqueous layer goes orange / brown if cyclohexane not mentioned for one observation mark\nDO NOT ALLOW references to halide ions gaining electrons` },
      { q: `Sodium chloride can be electrolysed either molten or as concentrated aqueous solution (brine).\n\nCompare the products formed at each electrode in the two processes and explain why they differ.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `molten: sodium at cathode AND chlorine at anode ✓\naqueous: hydrogen at cathode AND chlorine at anode (sodium hydroxide left in solution) ✓\nin aqueous solution water is present and is reduced in preference to Na⁺ ✓\nbecause hydrogen / H⁺ / water is more easily reduced than sodium ions / sodium is very reactive ✓\nALLOW 2H₂O + 2e⁻ → H₂ + 2OH⁻ as evidence for third mark\nIGNORE oxygen at anode for dilute solution if chlorine given for concentrated brine` },
      { q: `After seawater is treated with chlorine, the bromine formed is blown out with air and absorbed into a solution of sulfur dioxide. Hydrobromic acid and sulfuric acid are formed. The bromide is later oxidised again with chlorine to give a concentrated bromine solution.\n\nWrite an equation for the reaction of bromine with sulfur dioxide and water. Use oxidation states to explain the role of sulfur dioxide, and suggest why this extra stage is used.`, marks: 4, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O → 2HBr + H₂SO₄ ✓\nS changes from +4 to +6 so SO₂ is oxidised ✓\nso SO₂ is a reducing agent (reduces Br from 0 to −1) ✓\nto concentrate the bromine / bromide (seawater bromine is very dilute) ✓\nALLOW ionic equation Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O → 2Br⁻ + SO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺\nALLOW electron donor for reducing agent` },
      { q: `Potassium chlorate(V), KClO₃, is a powerful oxidising agent. In acidic solution chlorate(V) ions are reduced to chloride ions and water.\n\nConstruct the half-equation for the reduction of ClO₃⁻ in acid, then combine it with the half-equation 2I⁻ → I₂ + 2e⁻ to write the overall ionic equation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Write', markScheme: `ClO₃⁻ + 6H⁺ + 6e⁻ → Cl⁻ + 3H₂O\ncorrect species (ClO₃⁻, H⁺ on left; Cl⁻, H₂O on right) ✓\nbalanced with 6e⁻ ✓\nClO₃⁻ + 6I⁻ + 6H⁺ → Cl⁻ + 3I₂ + 3H₂O\niodide half-equation multiplied by 3 / 6e⁻ cancelled ✓\nfully correct and balanced overall equation ✓\nECF from incorrect half-equation if balancing logic correct\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `A student analyses a sample of thin bleach.\n• 10.0 cm³ of bleach is diluted to 250.0 cm³ with distilled water in a volumetric flask.\n• 25.0 cm³ of the diluted solution is pipetted into a conical flask and excess acidified potassium iodide is added.\n• The iodine released is titrated with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium thiosulfate. The mean titre is 18.60 cm³.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nClO⁻ + 2I⁻ + 2H⁺ → Cl⁻ + I₂ + H₂O\nI₂ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ → 2I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻\n\nCalculate the concentration of NaClO in the original bleach, in g dm⁻³.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(S₂O₃²⁻) = 0.100 × 18.60 ÷ 1000 = 1.86 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nn(I₂) = n(ClO⁻) in 25.0 cm³ = 9.30 × 10⁻⁴ mol ✓\nn(ClO⁻) in 250.0 cm³ (= in 10.0 cm³ bleach) = 9.30 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nconcentration = 9.30 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.0100 = 0.930 mol dm⁻³ ✓\nmass concentration = 0.930 × 74.5 = 69.3 g dm⁻³ ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nECF at each stage\nALLOW 69.28 to 69.3` },
      { q: `Table salt is often iodised by adding potassium iodate(V), KIO₃, to help prevent iodine deficiency. To check the level, 10.0 g of salt is dissolved in water and excess acidified potassium iodide is added. The iodine formed is titrated with 0.00100 mol dm⁻³ sodium thiosulfate; the titre is 14.20 cm³ (data written for this practice question).\n\nIO₃⁻ + 5I⁻ + 6H⁺ → 3I₂ + 3H₂O\nI₂ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ → 2I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻\n\nCalculate the mass of KIO₃, in mg, per kg of salt.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(S₂O₃²⁻) = 0.00100 × 14.20 ÷ 1000 = 1.42 × 10⁻⁵ mol ✓\nn(I₂) = 7.10 × 10⁻⁶ mol ✓\nn(IO₃⁻) = 7.10 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 3 = 2.37 × 10⁻⁶ mol ✓\nmass KIO₃ = 2.37 × 10⁻⁶ × 214(.0) = 5.06 × 10⁻⁴ g (in 10.0 g salt) ✓\nper kg = 5.06 × 10⁻⁴ × 100 = 0.0506 g = 50.6 mg kg⁻¹ ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW 1 IO₃⁻ ≡ 6 S₂O₃²⁻ in a single step for marks 2 and 3\nECF at each stage` },
      { q: `*Chlorine has been added to public drinking water in the UK for over a century. Some campaigners argue that it should be replaced by alternatives such as ozone or ultraviolet light.\n\nDiscuss the chemistry of chlorine in water treatment and evaluate the benefits and risks of using it.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nChemistry\n• Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO (disproportionation)\n• chloric(I) acid / HClO and ClO⁻ are oxidising agents that kill bacteria\n• chlorine stays in the water supply (residual effect) as it travels through pipes\nBenefits\n• kills pathogens, prevents waterborne diseases such as cholera and typhoid\n• cheap and effective at low concentrations; residual protection unlike ozone or UV\nRisks\n• chlorine gas is toxic; risk in transport and storage / leaks\n• chlorine reacts with organic matter to form chlorinated hydrocarbons (e.g. trichloromethane), possibly carcinogenic\n• judgement: benefits far outweigh risks at controlled low concentrations; alternatives lack residual effect\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `*A technician has three unlabelled bottles of white solid taken from a sea-salt processing laboratory. She knows they are sodium chloride, sodium bromide and sodium iodide, but not which is which.\n\nPlan an experiment, using test-tube reactions, that would allow her to identify each solid. Include the expected observations, relevant equations and the safety precautions needed.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• dissolve each solid in distilled water; add dilute nitric acid then aqueous silver nitrate\n• add dilute then concentrated aqueous ammonia to the precipitates\n• OR add concentrated sulfuric acid to small samples of each solid (in fume cupboard)\nObservations and equations\n• Cl⁻: white ppt, soluble in dilute NH₃; Br⁻: cream ppt, soluble only in concentrated NH₃; I⁻: yellow ppt, insoluble in concentrated NH₃\n• Ag⁺(aq) + X⁻(aq) → AgX(s)\n• with conc. H₂SO₄: NaCl gives only steamy HCl fumes; NaBr gives orange-brown Br₂ and SO₂; NaI gives purple I₂, black solid, H₂S (bad eggs)\nSafety\n• concentrated sulfuric acid is corrosive: goggles / gloves\n• HCl, SO₂, H₂S and halogen vapours are toxic: use a fume cupboard\n• concentrated ammonia gives off irritant fumes; use in fume cupboard\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'OZ': {
    name: 'The Ozone Story',
    questions: [
      { q: `Ozone is found in the stratosphere at concentrations of up to about 10 ppm.\n\nCalculate this concentration as a percentage by volume.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.001(%) ✓\nALLOW 1 × 10⁻³ %\nDO NOT ALLOW 0.01% or 0.0001%` },
      { q: `HFC-134a, CF₃CH₂F, has been widely used as a replacement for CFCs in car air-conditioning units and domestic refrigerators.\n\nState the systematic name of CF₃CH₂F.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `1,1,1,2-tetrafluoroethane ✓\nIGNORE missing or extra commas and hyphens\nDO NOT ALLOW 1,2,2,2-tetrafluoroethane` },
      { q: `A student investigates the hydrolysis of haloalkanes. A few drops of 1-iodobutane are added to a test tube containing ethanol and aqueous silver nitrate in a water bath at 50 °C. After a short time a precipitate forms.\n\nState the colour of the precipitate.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `yellow ✓\nALLOW pale yellow\nDO NOT ALLOW cream or white` },
      { q: `CFC molecules that reach the stratosphere are broken down by ultraviolet radiation to form chlorine radicals, Cl•.\n\nState what is meant by the term radical.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `(a species with) an unpaired electron ✓\nALLOW a particle with a single unpaired electron\nDO NOT ALLOW 'a species with a lone pair' or 'a charged species'` },
      { q: `In the stratosphere, chlorine monoxide radicals, ClO•, can react with nitrogen dioxide radicals, NO₂•, to form chlorine nitrate, ClONO₂. Chlorine nitrate acts as a 'reservoir' molecule that temporarily stops chlorine destroying ozone.\n\nWrite an equation for this termination step.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `ClO• + NO₂• → ClONO₂ ✓\nALLOW ClO + NO₂ → ClONO₂ (dots not required)\nALLOW ClONO₂ written as ClNO₃` },
      { q: `Methane and carbon dioxide in the troposphere absorb some of the radiation emitted by the Earth's surface. This absorption makes the bonds in the molecules vibrate more.\n\nState the region of the electromagnetic spectrum that is absorbed.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `infrared ✓\nALLOW IR\nDO NOT ALLOW ultraviolet or visible` },
      { q: `In the stratosphere, ultraviolet radiation breaks the Cl–Cl bond in chlorine molecules:\n\nCl₂ → 2Cl•\n\nIdentify the type of bond fission taking place.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `homolytic (fission) ✓\nALLOW homolysis\nALLOW photodissociation / photolysis\nDO NOT ALLOW heterolytic` },
      { q: `In a practical to compare the rates of hydrolysis of haloalkanes, a student mixes each haloalkane with ethanol before adding aqueous silver nitrate.\n\nSuggest why ethanol is used.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `haloalkanes are insoluble in water / do not mix with aqueous silver nitrate, so ethanol acts as a solvent in which both mix ✓\nALLOW ethanol is a (common) solvent for the haloalkane and the aqueous solution\nIGNORE 'to speed up the reaction'` },
      { q: `Ozone is formed naturally in the stratosphere in two steps. First, high-energy ultraviolet radiation splits oxygen molecules into oxygen atoms. The oxygen atoms then react with more oxygen molecules.\n\nWrite equations for these two steps.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `O₂ → 2O ✓\nO + O₂ → O₃ ✓\nALLOW O₂ + hν → 2O\nALLOW O + O₂ + M → O₃ + M\nALLOW O• for O\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `Chlorine radicals destroy ozone in the stratosphere in the following two propagation steps:\n\nCl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂\nClO• + O → Cl• + O₂\n\nDeduce the overall equation for the two steps and explain why Cl• is described as a catalyst in this process.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `O₃ + O → 2O₂ ✓\nCl• is regenerated / used up in the first step and reformed in the second step (so is not used up overall) ✓\nALLOW Cl• does not appear in the overall equation\nDO NOT ALLOW 'Cl• is not involved in the reaction'` },
      { q: `CFCs were released into the troposphere in large quantities from aerosols and refrigerators from the 1930s onwards. Many CFC molecules remain in the troposphere for decades before they reach the stratosphere.\n\nExplain why CFCs are not broken down in the troposphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `CFCs are unreactive / have strong bonds / are not attacked by other chemicals in the troposphere ✓\nthe ultraviolet radiation reaching the troposphere does not have enough energy / frequency to break C–Cl (or C–F) bonds (high-energy UV is absorbed higher up in the stratosphere) ✓\nALLOW CFCs are insoluble in water so are not removed by rain for the first mark` },
      { q: `Halomethanes were tested as early refrigerants. Their boiling points are shown below.\n\nCH₃Cl: −24 °C\nCH₃Br: 4 °C\nCH₃I: 42 °C\n\nExplain the trend in boiling points.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the number of electrons increases (from CH₃Cl to CH₃I) ✓\nso instantaneous dipole–induced dipole bonds are stronger and more energy is needed to overcome them ✓\nALLOW id–id bonds / London forces / dispersion forces\nDO NOT ALLOW breaking covalent bonds (0 marks for second point)\nIGNORE references to permanent dipoles unless they are said to be the cause of the trend` },
      { q: `Hydrogen fluoride and hydrogen chloride are both used in the manufacture of CFC replacements. Hydrogen fluoride boils at 20 °C, whereas hydrogen chloride boils at −85 °C.\n\nExplain why hydrogen fluoride has a much higher boiling point than hydrogen chloride.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `hydrogen bonds form between HF molecules (but not between HCl molecules) ✓\nhydrogen bonds are stronger than the intermolecular bonds in HCl / need more energy to overcome ✓\nALLOW F is very electronegative and has lone pairs, giving Hδ+ that bonds to a lone pair on F of another molecule (for first mark)\nDO NOT ALLOW references to breaking H–F covalent bonds` },
      { q: `Chlorine radicals react with ozone in the gas phase. In laboratory studies of the reaction, the total pressure of the gas mixture is increased at constant temperature.\n\nExplain, in terms of collision theory, why the rate of reaction increases.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `more particles in the same volume / particles closer together / higher concentration ✓\nso more (successful) collisions per unit time / more frequent collisions ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 'more collisions' without reference to time or frequency for the second mark\nIGNORE references to energy of particles` },
      { q: `Two catalysts are involved in reactions of ozone in the atmosphere:\n• Cl• radicals reacting with gaseous O₃ in the stratosphere\n• ice crystals in polar stratospheric clouds on which gaseous HCl and ClONO₂ react\n\nState the type of catalysis in each case and give the meaning of each type.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `Cl• is a homogeneous catalyst: catalyst in the same phase / physical state as the reactants ✓\nice crystals are a heterogeneous catalyst: catalyst in a different phase / physical state from the reactants ✓\nBoth type and meaning needed for each mark` },
      { q: `The C–Cl bond is more polar than the C–I bond, yet iodoalkanes are hydrolysed faster than chloroalkanes.\n\nBond enthalpies / kJ mol⁻¹: C–Cl 346, C–I 228\n\nExplain why iodoalkanes are hydrolysed faster.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the C–I bond is weaker / has a lower bond enthalpy (than C–Cl) ✓\nless energy needed to break it so (bond enthalpy is more important than bond polarity and) the reaction is faster / has a lower activation enthalpy ✓\nALLOW reverse arguments for C–Cl` },
      { q: `In a practical to compare the rates of hydrolysis of 1-chlorobutane, 1-bromobutane and 1-iodobutane, a student places the test tubes of haloalkane in ethanol and the test tube of aqueous silver nitrate in a water bath at 50 °C for 10 minutes before mixing them.\n\nSuggest why the student does this.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `so that all the solutions are at the same / a known temperature before mixing ✓\nbecause the rate of reaction depends on temperature, so temperature is controlled for a fair comparison ✓\nALLOW to make the reaction fast enough to observe in a reasonable time (for one mark only if neither point above)` },
      { q: `Haloalkanes react with ammonia to form amines. When bromoethane is heated with excess ammonia in a sealed tube, ethylamine is formed.\n\nWrite an equation for the reaction and identify the feature of the ammonia molecule that allows it to act as a nucleophile.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CH₃CH₂Br + NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + HBr ✓\nALLOW C₂H₅Br + 2NH₃ → C₂H₅NH₂ + NH₄Br\nlone pair (of electrons) on the nitrogen atom ✓\nALLOW can donate a pair of electrons` },
      { q: `Carbon dioxide and methane in the troposphere absorb infrared radiation emitted from the Earth's surface.\n\nExplain how this absorption leads to an increase in the temperature of the troposphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the energy absorbed makes the bonds vibrate more / increases the vibrational energy of the molecules ✓\nthis energy is transferred to other molecules in the air by collisions, increasing their kinetic energy ✓\nALLOW energy is re-emitted in all directions, including back towards the Earth (for second mark)` },
      { q: `A sample of stratospheric air contains 5.0 × 10¹⁷ molecules per cm³. The concentration of ozone in the sample is 6.0 ppm.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(i) Calculate the number of ozone molecules per cm³ of the sample.\n(ii) Calculate the concentration of ozone as a percentage.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(i) 5.0 × 10¹⁷ × 6.0 / 10⁶ ✓\n= 3.0 × 10¹² (molecules cm⁻³) ✓\n(ii) 6.0 × 10⁻⁴ % / 0.00060 % ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks for (i)` },
      { q: `The ozone layer is described as protecting life on Earth. Exposure to high-energy ultraviolet radiation increases the risk of skin cancer and damages crops.\n\nExplain how ozone in the stratosphere protects living things from this radiation. Include an equation in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ozone absorbs high-energy / high-frequency ultraviolet radiation (UV-B / UV-C) so less reaches the Earth's surface ✓\nO₃ → O₂ + O ✓\nhigh-energy UV can break bonds in / damage DNA (in skin cells), causing mutations / cancer ✓\nALLOW O₃ + hν → O₂ + O\nIGNORE 'reflects UV'` },
      { q: `Under the Montreal Protocol (1987), CFCs were phased out and replaced in refrigerators by HFCs such as CF₃CH₂F. Under the 2016 Kigali Amendment, HFCs are now being phased down as well.\n\nExplain why HFCs were chosen to replace CFCs, and why HFCs are themselves now being phased down.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `HFCs contain no C–Cl bonds / no chlorine ✓\nC–F and C–H bonds are strong / are not broken by UV in the stratosphere, so no radicals that destroy ozone are formed ✓\nHFCs absorb infrared radiation / are (powerful) greenhouse gases, contributing to global warming ✓\nALLOW HFCs have similar properties (volatile, unreactive, non-toxic) so work as refrigerants – IGNORE if instead of an explanation point\nDO NOT ALLOW 'HFCs destroy ozone more slowly'` },
      { q: `A student prepares 2-chloro-2-methylpropane by shaking 10.0 cm³ of 2-methylpropan-2-ol (density 0.775 g cm⁻³, Mr 74.0) with excess concentrated hydrochloric acid.\n\n(CH₃)₃COH + HCl → (CH₃)₃CCl + H₂O\n\nAfter purification the student obtains 6.20 g of 2-chloro-2-methylpropane (Mr 92.5).\n\nCalculate the percentage yield. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mass of alcohol = 10.0 × 0.775 = 7.75 g and moles = 7.75 / 74.0 = 0.105 (0.1047) mol ✓\nmoles of product = 6.20 / 92.5 = 0.0670 mol OR theoretical mass = 9.69 g ✓\npercentage yield = 64.0 % ✓\nALLOW 63.8 % from rounded intermediate values\nECF from incorrect moles\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A catalyst provides an alternative route for a reaction. In the stratosphere, Cl• radicals catalyse the reaction O₃ + O → 2O₂, which is exothermic.\n\nSketch an enthalpy profile for this reaction showing both the uncatalysed and the catalysed routes. Label the activation enthalpy of the catalysed route and the enthalpy change of reaction, ΔH.`, marks: 3, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `reactants (O₃ + O) drawn higher than products (2O₂) with axes labelled enthalpy and progress of reaction ✓\nuncatalysed hump higher than catalysed route (catalysed route may show two humps) ✓\nactivation enthalpy of catalysed route shown from reactants to top of (highest) catalysed hump AND ΔH shown from reactants to products, arrow pointing down ✓\nALLOW Ea or Eₐ for activation enthalpy\nDO NOT ALLOW ΔH arrow pointing up` },
      { q: `2-Bromopropane can react with hydroxide ions in two different ways depending on the conditions. One reaction forms propan-2-ol; the other forms propene.\n\nCompare the conditions needed for each reaction and write an equation for the formation of propene.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `propan-2-ol (substitution): aqueous (sodium or potassium) hydroxide, heated under reflux ✓\npropene (elimination): (potassium) hydroxide dissolved in ethanol, heated ✓\nCH₃CHBrCH₃ + OH⁻ → CH₃CH=CH₂ + H₂O + Br⁻ ✓\nALLOW CH₃CHBrCH₃ + KOH → CH₃CH=CH₂ + H₂O + KBr\nALLOW ethanolic / alcoholic KOH` },
      { q: `Ice crystals in polar stratospheric clouds catalyse reactions that release chlorine.\n\nExplain, with reference to the Boltzmann distribution of molecular energies, how a catalyst increases the rate of a reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `catalyst provides an alternative route / mechanism with a lower activation enthalpy ✓\na greater proportion / more molecules have energy greater than or equal to the (new) activation enthalpy ✓\nso there are more successful collisions per unit time ✓\nALLOW the area under the Boltzmann curve to the right of the activation enthalpy is larger\nDO NOT ALLOW 'catalyst gives molecules more energy'` },
      { q: `A student compares the rates of hydrolysis of three haloalkanes. Each is added to ethanol and aqueous silver nitrate at 50 °C and the time for a precipitate to appear is recorded.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n1-chlorobutane: no precipitate after 20 minutes\n1-bromobutane: 285 s\n1-iodobutane: 42 s\n\nBond enthalpies / kJ mol⁻¹: C–Cl 346, C–Br 290, C–I 228\n\nDeduce the order of reactivity of the haloalkanes and explain the order using the bond enthalpy data.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `order: 1-iodobutane most reactive, then 1-bromobutane, then 1-chlorobutane least reactive ✓\nC–I bond has the lowest bond enthalpy / is the weakest, C–Cl the highest ✓\nso the C–halogen bond is broken most easily / the halide ion is released fastest (to form the precipitate) ✓\nIGNORE references to bond polarity unless used as the explanation of the order (then max 2)` },
      { q: `The following steps take place when the CFC dichlorodifluoromethane, CF₂Cl₂, reaches the stratosphere.\n\nStep 1: CF₂Cl₂ → CF₂Cl• + Cl•\nStep 2: Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂\nStep 3: ClO• + O → Cl• + O₂\nStep 4: ClO• + NO₂• → ClONO₂\n\nIdentify each step as initiation, propagation or termination and explain why step 1 only happens in the stratosphere and not in the troposphere.`, marks: 3, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `step 1 initiation AND step 4 termination ✓\nsteps 2 and 3 propagation ✓\nultraviolet radiation of high enough energy / frequency to break the C–Cl bond reaches the stratosphere but not the troposphere ✓\nALLOW high-energy UV is absorbed by O₂ / O₃ in the stratosphere before reaching the troposphere` },
      { q: `Hydroxide ions react with 1-bromobutane, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br, when the mixture is heated under reflux with aqueous sodium hydroxide.\n\nDraw the mechanism for this reaction. Show curly arrows, relevant dipoles and lone pairs, and the products.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `dipole on C–Br bond: Cδ+ and Brδ− ✓\ncurly arrow from a lone pair on the O of OH⁻ to the C atom bonded to Br ✓\ncurly arrow from the C–Br bond to Br ✓\nproducts: CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH and Br⁻ ✓\nALLOW the lone pair and the negative charge to be on O\nDO NOT ALLOW curly arrow starting from the H of OH⁻\nALLOW a transition state with partial bonds if correct` },
      { q: `CFCs are broken down in the stratosphere when ultraviolet radiation breaks C–Cl bonds.\n\nBond enthalpy of C–Cl = 346 kJ mol⁻¹\nh = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J Hz⁻¹; c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹; NA = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the maximum wavelength of radiation, in nm, that can break one C–Cl bond. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `energy per bond = 346 × 1000 / 6.02 × 10²³ = 5.75 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ✓\nν = E / h = 5.75 × 10⁻¹⁹ / 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ = 8.67 × 10¹⁴ Hz ✓\nλ = c / ν = 3.00 × 10⁸ / 8.67 × 10¹⁴ = 3.46 × 10⁻⁷ m ✓\n= 346 nm ✓\nECF at each stage\nALLOW 345–347 nm\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `The Antarctic ozone 'hole' develops each spring (September–October). During the dark polar winter, HCl and ClONO₂ react on the surface of ice crystals in polar stratospheric clouds:\n\nHCl + ClONO₂ → Cl₂ + HNO₃\n\nExplain how the ice crystals act as a heterogeneous catalyst for this reaction and why rapid ozone destruction starts only when sunlight returns in spring.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `reactant molecules are adsorbed onto the (ice) surface ✓\nbonds in the reactants are weakened / reactants are held close together in a favourable orientation, so the activation enthalpy is lower ✓\nproducts are desorbed / leave the surface ✓\nin spring, UV radiation breaks Cl₂ into Cl• radicals (Cl₂ → 2Cl•), which then catalyse ozone destruction ✓\nALLOW 'held on the surface' for adsorbed\nDO NOT ALLOW absorbed for adsorbed` },
      { q: `After shaking 2-methylpropan-2-ol with concentrated hydrochloric acid in a separating funnel, a student obtains an impure upper organic layer of 2-chloro-2-methylpropane (boiling point 51 °C) and a lower aqueous layer.\n\nDescribe and explain the steps the student should take to obtain a pure, dry sample of 2-chloro-2-methylpropane.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `run off and discard the lower aqueous layer ✓\nshake the organic layer with sodium hydrogencarbonate solution to remove (neutralise) remaining acid, releasing the pressure / venting CO₂ regularly ✓\nrun off the aqueous layer and add an anhydrous drying agent (e.g. anhydrous sodium sulfate / calcium chloride / magnesium sulfate) to remove water; decant or filter ✓\nredistil and collect the fraction boiling at about 51 °C ✓\nALLOW wash with water after the sodium hydrogencarbonate\nALLOW sodium carbonate solution` },
      { q: `The table shows the mean October ozone concentration over Antarctica in Dobson units (DU).\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n1970: 300 DU\n1980: 225 DU\n1990: 140 DU\n2000: 115 DU\n2010: 125 DU\n2020: 130 DU\n\nThe Montreal Protocol, which phased out the production of CFCs, was agreed in 1987.\n\nAnalyse the data and evaluate the claim that the Montreal Protocol has been effective in protecting the ozone layer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `ozone fell steeply from 1970 to 1990 / fell by 160 DU (about 53%) ✓\nozone continued to fall after 1987 until about 2000 ✓\nexplanation: CFCs already released remain in the atmosphere for decades / take years to reach the stratosphere, so the effect is delayed ✓\nozone has risen slightly since 2000 (to 130 DU), supporting the claim, BUT recovery is slow / still far below 1970 levels / other factors (e.g. temperature, volcanic eruptions) may affect results, so data alone cannot prove cause ✓\nALLOW any valid limitation of the data (only one value per decade, one location)` },
      { q: `Oxygen molecules in the upper stratosphere are split by ultraviolet radiation. The bond enthalpy of O=O is 498 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nh = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J Hz⁻¹; c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹; NA = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹\n\n(i) Calculate the maximum wavelength, in nm, of radiation that can break one O=O bond. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.\n(ii) Ultraviolet radiation of wavelength 280 nm reaches the lower stratosphere. Deduce, with a reason, whether this radiation can split O₂ molecules.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(i) energy per bond = 498 000 / 6.02 × 10²³ = 8.27 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ✓\nν = 8.27 × 10⁻¹⁹ / 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ = 1.25 × 10¹⁵ Hz ✓\nλ = 3.00 × 10⁸ / 1.25 × 10¹⁵ = 2.40 × 10⁻⁷ m ✓\n= 240 nm ✓\nECF at each stage\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks for (i)\n(ii) no: 280 nm is a longer wavelength / lower frequency so each photon has less energy than needed to break an O=O bond ✓\nECF from answer to (i)` },
      { q: `The rate of reaction between Cl• radicals and ozone increases as the temperature of the stratosphere increases.\n\nSketch the Boltzmann distribution of molecular energies for a gas at temperature T₁, and on the same axes for the same gas at a higher temperature T₂. Mark the activation enthalpy, Ea. Use your sketch to explain why a small increase in temperature causes a large increase in rate.`, marks: 5, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `axes labelled: number (or fraction) of molecules (y) and energy (x); curve starts at the origin and does not touch the energy axis at high energy ✓\nT₂ curve has its peak lower and to the right of T₁ curve ✓\nEa marked on the energy axis to the right of both peaks ✓\nat T₂ a greater proportion of molecules have energy ≥ Ea / greater area under curve beyond Ea ✓\nso many more successful collisions per unit time (the increase in successful collisions is much greater than the increase in collision frequency) ✓\nALLOW areas shaded for the fourth mark\nDO NOT ALLOW curves that cross the x-axis at high energy\nDO NOT ALLOW a T₂ curve with a larger total area` },
      { q: `CFCs were once regarded as ideal chemicals for refrigerators and aerosols. In the 1970s, Molina and Rowland proposed that CFCs damage the ozone layer, and CFCs were eventually phased out under the Montreal Protocol and replaced by HFCs.\n\nExplain why CFCs were so widely used, how they cause depletion of the ozone layer, and why HFCs are a better replacement as far as the ozone layer is concerned. Include equations in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nUses and properties of CFCs\n• low toxicity, non-flammable, unreactive\n• volatile / suitable boiling points for refrigerants and aerosol propellants\n• unreactivity means they are not broken down in the troposphere and diffuse to the stratosphere\nHow CFCs deplete ozone\n• high-energy UV in the stratosphere breaks the C–Cl bond (weakest bond in the molecule), e.g. CF₂Cl₂ → CF₂Cl• + Cl•\n• Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂ and ClO• + O → Cl• + O₂\n• Cl• is regenerated, so it acts as a catalyst; chain reaction; one Cl• can destroy many thousands of O₃ molecules\n• rate of ozone destruction greater than rate of formation, so the steady-state concentration falls\nHFCs as replacements\n• no C–Cl bonds; C–F bond (and C–H) much stronger than C–Cl\n• not broken by UV in the stratosphere so no radicals that catalyse ozone destruction are formed\n• similar physical properties, so still suitable as refrigerants\n• (HFCs are greenhouse gases – credit as a limitation)\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `Engineers designing a new refrigerant compared three compounds with similar Mr values.\n\nPropane, CH₃CH₂CH₃: Mr 44.0, boiling point −42 °C\nFluoroethane, CH₃CH₂F: Mr 48.0, boiling point −38 °C\nEthanol, CH₃CH₂OH: Mr 46.0, boiling point 78 °C\n\nExplain the differences in the boiling points of these compounds in terms of the intermolecular bonds present in each. Your answer should explain why fluoroethane does not form hydrogen bonds between its molecules.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nInstantaneous dipole–induced dipole bonds\n• present in all three compounds\n• similar numbers of electrons (similar Mr) so id–id bonds are similar in strength\n• propane has only id–id bonds so has the lowest boiling point\nPermanent dipoles\n• C–F bond is polar because F is much more electronegative than C\n• fluoroethane has permanent dipole–permanent dipole bonds as well as id–id\n• so slightly more energy is needed to separate molecules: higher boiling point than propane\nHydrogen bonding\n• ethanol has an O–H group: Hδ+ attached to highly electronegative O\n• hydrogen bond forms between Hδ+ and a lone pair on O of another molecule\n• hydrogen bonds are the strongest intermolecular bonds, so much more energy needed: highest boiling point\n• in fluoroethane all H atoms are bonded to C, which is not electronegative enough, so there is no Hδ+ able to form hydrogen bonds\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A student is asked to plan an experiment to compare the rates of hydrolysis of 1-chlorobutane, 1-bromobutane and 1-iodobutane, using ethanol and aqueous silver nitrate.\n\nThe student has access to normal laboratory equipment, including a thermostatically controlled water bath and a stopclock.\n\nDescribe a plan for the experiment, including how the variables would be controlled and any safety precautions. Predict and explain the results.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• add the same volume / number of drops of each haloalkane to the same volume of ethanol in separate test tubes\n• place these and a tube of aqueous silver nitrate in a water bath at a set temperature (e.g. 50 °C) to equilibrate\n• add the same volume and concentration of silver nitrate to each tube, start the stopclock\n• time until a precipitate first appears / until a mark under the tube is obscured\nControl of variables and safety\n• control: temperature, volumes, concentration of AgNO₃, same chain length / primary haloalkanes, same size test tubes\n• repeat and calculate a mean time; rate is proportional to 1/time\n• ethanol and haloalkanes are flammable: no naked flames / use a water bath; haloalkanes are harmful: wear gloves / use a fume cupboard; silver nitrate stains skin: wear gloves and eye protection\nPrediction and explanation\n• water / H₂O acts as nucleophile; halide ion released reacts with Ag⁺ to form a precipitate, Ag⁺(aq) + X⁻(aq) → AgX(s)\n• precipitates: AgCl white, AgBr cream, AgI yellow\n• 1-iodobutane fastest, then 1-bromobutane, 1-chlorobutane slowest\n• C–I bond has the lowest bond enthalpy / weakest, so is broken most easily; bond enthalpy is more important than bond polarity\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'WM': {
    name: 'What\'s in a Medicine?',
    questions: [
      { q: `Extracts of willow bark were used for centuries to reduce fever. The active compound in the bark is converted in the body into salicylic acid. A molecule of salicylic acid has a benzene ring with a –COOH group and an –OH group on adjacent ring carbon atoms.\n\nBenzoic acid, C₆H₅COOH, has only the –COOH group on its benzene ring.\n\nState the name of the functional group present in salicylic acid that is not present in benzoic acid.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `phenol ✓\nALLOW phenolic –OH / hydroxyl group attached directly to a benzene ring\nDO NOT ALLOW alcohol\nIGNORE hydroxyl / –OH on its own` },
      { q: `A student tests a solution of salicylic acid, extracted from willow bark, with a few drops of neutral iron(III) chloride solution.\n\nState what the student would observe.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `purple / violet colour forms ✓\nALLOW mauve / deep blue-purple\nIGNORE precipitate\nDO NOT ALLOW orange / green` },
      { q: `A chemist is studying alcohols that could be used as solvents in medicine manufacture. One of these is 2-methylpropan-2-ol, (CH₃)₃COH.\n\nA sample of 2-methylpropan-2-ol is warmed with acidified potassium dichromate(VI) solution.\n\nPredict what would be observed.`, marks: 1, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `(solution) stays orange / no colour change ✓\nALLOW no reaction because it is a tertiary alcohol\nDO NOT ALLOW turns green` },
      { q: `A student purifies crude aspirin by recrystallisation from water. After filtering the crystals under reduced pressure, the student washes them with a small volume of ice-cold water.\n\nState why ice-cold water is used for washing rather than water at room temperature.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `aspirin is less soluble in cold water, so less product dissolves / is lost during washing ✓\nALLOW to minimise loss of product\nIGNORE to remove impurities on its own` },
      { q: `During the development of a new medicine, a chemist needs to convert an alcohol group in an intermediate into a C=C double bond by dehydration. She first tries the reaction on a simple model compound, cyclohexanol.\n\nState a suitable reagent or catalyst for the dehydration of cyclohexanol.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `concentrated phosphoric acid / concentrated sulfuric acid / heated aluminium oxide (Al₂O₃) ✓\nALLOW H₃PO₄ / H₂SO₄ if concentrated is stated\nALLOW Al₂O₃ without heat\nDO NOT ALLOW dilute acid / NaOH` },
      { q: `The mass spectrum of paracetamol, C₈H₉NO₂, has its molecular ion peak at m/z = 151. There is also a very small peak at m/z = 152.\n\nSuggest the cause of the peak at m/z = 152.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `(molecular ions) containing one ¹³C atom ✓\nALLOW containing a heavier isotope (e.g. of carbon / ¹³C, ¹⁵N, ²H)\nDO NOT ALLOW a fragment / impurity` },
      { q: `Aspirin is only slightly soluble in water. "Soluble aspirin" tablets contain the sodium salt of aspirin, in which the –COOH group has been converted into –COO⁻ Na⁺.\n\nExplain why the sodium salt of aspirin is much more soluble in water than aspirin.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the salt is ionic / contains ions ✓\nthe ions are attracted to (polar) water molecules / ion–dipole interactions / ions become hydrated, which are stronger than the hydrogen bonds aspirin forms with water ✓\nALLOW water molecules surround the ions\nDO NOT ALLOW the salt forms hydrogen bonds as the only reason` },
      { q: `Salicylic acid, HOC₆H₄COOH, contains a phenol group and a carboxylic acid group. When sodium carbonate solution is added to salicylic acid, effervescence is seen.\n\nWrite an equation for the reaction of salicylic acid with sodium carbonate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `2HOC₆H₄COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2HOC₆H₄COONa + H₂O + CO₂\ncorrect formulae of all products, with CO₂ and H₂O ✓\nequation balanced AND only the –COOH group reacts (phenol –OH unchanged) ✓\nALLOW HOC₆H₄COO⁻Na⁺\nALLOW ionic equation: 2HOC₆H₄COOH + CO₃²⁻ → 2HOC₆H₄COO⁻ + H₂O + CO₂\nALLOW 1 mark for a balanced equation in which the phenol –OH also reacts` },
      { q: `A student checks the purity of a sample of aspirin she has made using thin-layer chromatography. Her plate shows a single spot that has moved 3.4 cm from the baseline. The solvent front has moved 8.5 cm from the baseline.\n\nReference Rf values in this solvent (data written for this practice question):\nsalicylic acid 0.55\naspirin 0.40\n\nCalculate the Rf value of her spot and state what it shows about her sample.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Rf = 3.4 ÷ 8.5 = 0.40 ✓\n(spot matches aspirin and only one spot, so) aspirin with no salicylic acid detected ✓\nALLOW 0.4\nALLOW sample is (likely to be) pure aspirin\nIGNORE units` },
      { q: `Aspirin is manufactured from salicylic acid and ethanoic anhydride:\n\nHOC₆H₄COOH + (CH₃CO)₂O → CH₃COOC₆H₄COOH + CH₃COOH\n\nMr values: salicylic acid 138.0, ethanoic anhydride 102.0, aspirin 180.0, ethanoic acid 60.0\n\nCalculate the atom economy for the formation of aspirin by this reaction. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `180.0 ÷ (138.0 + 102.0) × 100 ✓\n= 75.0% ✓\nALLOW 180 ÷ (180 + 60) × 100\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW 75% for 1 mark only (not 3 s.f.)` },
      { q: `A student oxidises propan-1-ol with acidified potassium dichromate(VI). The infrared spectrum of her organic product has a strong absorption at 1715 cm⁻¹ and a very broad absorption from 2500 to 3300 cm⁻¹.\n\nData Sheet: O–H (alcohols) 3200–3600 cm⁻¹; O–H (carboxylic acids) 2500–3300 cm⁻¹; C=O (aldehydes) 1720–1740 cm⁻¹; C=O (carboxylic acids) 1700–1725 cm⁻¹\n\nDeduce, with a reason, whether the product is propanal or propanoic acid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `propanoic acid ✓\nvery broad absorption at 2500–3300 cm⁻¹ is O–H in a carboxylic acid (aldehyde has no O–H) ✓\nALLOW 1715 cm⁻¹ is in the carboxylic acid C=O range\nSecond mark is dependent on correct identification` },
      { q: `Menthol is used in medicines to soothe sore throats. In a menthol molecule, the –OH group is attached to a carbon atom of a cyclohexane ring. That carbon atom is also bonded to one hydrogen atom and to two other ring carbon atoms.\n\nClassify menthol as a primary, secondary or tertiary alcohol, and state the type of organic compound formed when menthol is oxidised by acidified dichromate(VI).`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `secondary (alcohol) ✓\nketone ✓\nALLOW menthone\nECF: aldehyde/carboxylic acid for primary; no reaction for tertiary` },
      { q: `A student measures the melting point of the aspirin she has made. Her sample melts over the range 128–133 °C. The data book value for pure aspirin is 136 °C.\n\nExplain what her results show about her sample.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `sample is impure ✓\nbecause it melts below the data book value AND over a range (rather than sharply) ✓\nALLOW impurities lower the melting point and widen the range\nIGNORE suggestions of the identity of the impurity` },
      { q: `In the 1860s, Joseph Lister used phenol, C₆H₅OH, as an antiseptic in surgery. Phenol is a weak acid and dissolves in sodium hydroxide solution.\n\nWrite an equation for the reaction of phenol with sodium hydroxide.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₆H₅OH + NaOH → C₆H₅ONa + H₂O\nC₆H₅ONa / C₆H₅O⁻Na⁺ ✓\nrest of equation correct and balanced ✓\nALLOW ionic equation C₆H₅OH + OH⁻ → C₆H₅O⁻ + H₂O\nALLOW sodium phenoxide named instead of formula for first mark only` },
      { q: `Oil of wintergreen, used in muscle-rub creams, contains methyl salicylate. It can be made by heating salicylic acid, HOC₆H₄COOH, with methanol and a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid.\n\nWrite an equation for this reaction, using structural formulae.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `HOC₆H₄COOH + CH₃OH ⇌ HOC₆H₄COOCH₃ + H₂O\nHOC₆H₄COOCH₃ as the ester ✓\nH₂O as other product and equation balanced ✓\nALLOW → for ⇌\nALLOW H₂SO₄ over the arrow\nDO NOT ALLOW ester formed at the phenol –OH` },
      { q: `Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol are isomers, C₃H₈O, used as solvents in the pharmaceutical industry. A technician finds an unlabelled bottle containing one of these alcohols. Its mass spectrum has a molecular ion peak at m/z = 60 and the most abundant peak at m/z = 45. There is no significant peak at m/z = 31.\n\nDeduce which alcohol is in the bottle. Give the formulae of the ions responsible for the peaks at m/z = 60 and m/z = 45.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `propan-2-ol ✓\nm/z = 60: C₃H₈O⁺ / CH₃CH(OH)CH₃⁺ ✓\nm/z = 45: CH₃CHOH⁺ / C₂H₅O⁺ ✓\nALLOW 45 formed by loss of CH₃ (from propan-2-ol)\nALLOW CH₃CH(OH)⁺\nIons must have + charge; penalise missing charge once only\nALLOW reasoning that propan-1-ol would give CH₂OH⁺ at 31 as credit for first mark` },
      { q: `A student has two unlabelled bottles. One contains butan-2-ol and the other contains 2-methylpropan-2-ol. Both have the formula C₄H₁₀O.\n\nDescribe a chemical test the student could carry out to identify which bottle contains butan-2-ol. Include the reagents, the conditions and the observations for each alcohol.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `acidified potassium dichromate(VI) / K₂Cr₂O₇ and H₂SO₄, warm ✓\nbutan-2-ol: orange → green ✓\n2-methylpropan-2-ol: stays orange ✓\nALLOW heat / water bath for warm\nALLOW H⁺/Cr₂O₇²⁻\nDO NOT ALLOW orange → green for both\nObservation marks can be awarded if reagent is acidified dichromate without conditions` },
      { q: `A student has made a crude sample of aspirin. Aspirin is much more soluble in hot water than in cold water. The impurities are present in small amounts and are soluble in cold water.\n\nOutline how the student should purify the crude aspirin by recrystallisation.`, marks: 3, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `dissolve in the minimum volume of hot water ✓\nallow to cool (slowly) / cool in ice so crystals form ✓\nfilter under reduced pressure / Büchner filtration, wash with a little cold water and dry ✓\nALLOW filter hot to remove insoluble impurities (not required)\nALLOW dry in a desiccator / low-temperature oven / between filter papers\nDO NOT ALLOW evaporate to dryness` },
      { q: `Ethanol is used as a solvent in some liquid medicines because it mixes with water in all proportions. Hexan-1-ol, CH₃(CH₂)₅OH, is much less soluble in water.\n\nExplain the difference in solubility of ethanol and hexan-1-ol in water.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ethanol forms hydrogen bonds with water molecules (via its –OH group) ✓\nhexan-1-ol has a long hydrocarbon chain which cannot hydrogen bond with water / only forms instantaneous dipole–induced dipole bonds ✓\nso the hydrocarbon chain disrupts hydrogen bonding between water molecules / the hydrogen bonds between water molecules broken are not replaced ✓\nALLOW –OH is a smaller part of the molecule in hexan-1-ol\nALLOW London forces / dispersion forces for instantaneous dipole–induced dipole` },
      { q: `During the development of a new medicine, a chemist investigates removing an –OH group from an intermediate by dehydration. As a simple model she heats butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃, with a catalyst of aluminium oxide. Three different alkenes are formed.\n\nDraw the structures of the three alkenes formed and label any that are stereoisomers.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `but-1-ene, CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃ ✓\nbut-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₃ drawn as two geometric isomers ✓\ncorrectly labelled E (trans) and Z (cis) ✓\nALLOW skeletal / displayed / structural formulae\nALLOW cis / trans labels\nThird mark requires both but-2-ene structures to show correct geometry about the C=C` },
      { q: `A bottle of aspirin tablets that has been stored in a warm, damp place for a long time smells of vinegar. A solution of the old tablets gives a purple colour with neutral iron(III) chloride solution, but a solution of fresh tablets does not.\n\nThe structure of aspirin is a benzene ring with a –COOH group and an –OCOCH₃ (ester) group on adjacent carbon atoms.\n\nExplain these observations.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the ester (group in aspirin) has been hydrolysed (by water) ✓\nforming ethanoic acid, which smells of vinegar ✓\nand salicylic acid, which has a phenol group that gives the purple colour with iron(III) chloride (fresh aspirin has no phenol group) ✓\nALLOW equation for hydrolysis to support first two marks\nALLOW ethanoic acid for acetic acid` },
      { q: `Paracetamol is a painkiller. A paracetamol molecule has a benzene ring with an –OH group on one carbon atom and an –NHCOCH₃ group on the ring carbon atom opposite it.\n\nIdentify the functional group present in paracetamol that is also present in salicylic acid. Predict, with reasons, what would be observed when neutral iron(III) chloride solution and, separately, sodium carbonate solution are added to paracetamol.`, marks: 3, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `phenol ✓\niron(III) chloride: purple colour because paracetamol contains a phenol group ✓\nsodium carbonate: no effervescence because paracetamol has no carboxylic acid group / phenols are not acidic enough to react with carbonates ✓\nALLOW violet for purple\nIGNORE references to the amide group` },
      { q: `Aspirin could in principle be made by heating salicylic acid with ethanoic acid. The atom economy for this route would be 90.9%, compared with 75.0% for the route using ethanoic anhydride. However, ethanoic anhydride is used both in school laboratories and in industry.\n\nSuggest reasons why ethanoic anhydride is used rather than ethanoic acid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `reaction with ethanoic acid is (very) slow / phenol –OH reacts only slowly with carboxylic acids ✓\nreaction with ethanoic acid is reversible / reaches equilibrium so yield is low ✓\nreaction with the anhydride is faster AND goes to completion / not reversible / gives a higher yield (and the by-product ethanoic acid can be sold or reused) ✓\nALLOW water is a product with ethanoic acid, which hydrolyses the ester\nALLOW anhydride is more reactive` },
      { q: `Paracetamol was analysed to find its formula. Its percentage composition by mass is C 63.6%, H 6.0%, N 9.3%, O 21.2%. The molecular ion peak in its mass spectrum is at m/z = 151.\n\nAr values: C 12.0, H 1.0, N 14.0, O 16.0\n\nDetermine the empirical formula and the molecular formula of paracetamol. Show your working.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `C 63.6 ÷ 12.0 = 5.30; H 6.0 ÷ 1.0 = 6.0; N 9.3 ÷ 14.0 = 0.664; O 21.2 ÷ 16.0 = 1.325 ✓\ndivide by smallest: C 8.0, H 9.0, N 1, O 2.0 ✓\nempirical formula C₈H₉NO₂ ✓\nMr of empirical formula = 151.0 = M⁺, so molecular formula C₈H₉NO₂ ✓\nECF from incorrect ratio\nCorrect empirical formula with no working scores 1 mark only` },
      { q: `A student wants to use thin-layer chromatography (TLC) to find out whether her aspirin contains unreacted salicylic acid. She has a TLC plate, a sample of her aspirin, pure aspirin, pure salicylic acid, a suitable solvent and a UV lamp.\n\nDescribe how she should carry out the TLC and how she should use the results to reach a conclusion.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `draw a pencil baseline and spot her sample, pure aspirin and pure salicylic acid (dissolved in a volatile solvent) on the baseline ✓\nplace in a beaker/tank with solvent below the baseline and a lid; remove when solvent is near the top and mark the solvent front ✓\ndry and view under UV lamp; mark the spots / calculate Rf values ✓\nif her sample shows a spot at the same height / Rf as salicylic acid, it contains salicylic acid; a single spot matching aspirin shows no salicylic acid detected ✓\nALLOW iodine for locating spots\nDO NOT ALLOW pen baseline for first mark` },
      { q: `A student makes aspirin from 1.50 g of salicylic acid, using an excess of ethanoic anhydride. She filters off her crystals and weighs them straight away. Her product has a mass of 2.08 g.\n\nHOC₆H₄COOH + (CH₃CO)₂O → CH₃COOC₆H₄COOH + CH₃COOH\nMr values: salicylic acid 138.0, aspirin 180.0\n\nCalculate her percentage yield to 3 significant figures, and suggest two reasons for the value and one improvement to her method.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `theoretical mass = 1.50 ÷ 138.0 × 180.0 = 1.96 g ✓\npercentage yield = 2.08 ÷ 1.957 × 100 = 106% ✓\nreasons (any one): product still wet / contains water; product contains impurities such as unreacted salicylic acid / ethanoic acid ✓\nimprovement: dry the product (e.g. in a desiccator / low oven) to constant mass before weighing / recrystallise before weighing ✓\nALLOW 106.3%\nECF from incorrect theoretical mass\nCorrect percentage yield with no working scores first two marks` },
      { q: `A student makes an ester by heating ethanol with ethanoic acid and a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid. She analyses the purified product.\n\nMass spectrum: molecular ion peak at m/z = 88; large peak at m/z = 43.\nInfrared spectrum: strong absorptions at 1740 cm⁻¹ and 1240 cm⁻¹; no broad absorption between 2500 and 3600 cm⁻¹.\n\nData Sheet: O–H (alcohols) 3200–3600 cm⁻¹; O–H (carboxylic acids) 2500–3300 cm⁻¹; C=O (esters) 1735–1750 cm⁻¹; C–O 1000–1300 cm⁻¹\n\nUse the data to show that the product is ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₂CH₃, and that it is free from the starting materials.`, marks: 4, command: 'Show', markScheme: `Mr of CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ = 88(.0), matching M⁺ at m/z = 88 ✓\nm/z = 43 is CH₃CO⁺ ✓\n1740 cm⁻¹ is C=O in an ester AND 1240 cm⁻¹ is C–O ✓\nno broad O–H absorption so no ethanol or ethanoic acid present ✓\nIon must have + charge for second mark\nALLOW C₂H₃O⁺` },
      { q: `A student makes aspirin by warming 2.00 g of salicylic acid with 5.0 cm³ of ethanoic anhydride. She obtains 1.82 g of pure, dry aspirin.\n\nHOC₆H₄COOH + (CH₃CO)₂O → CH₃COOC₆H₄COOH + CH₃COOH\nMr values: salicylic acid 138.0, ethanoic anhydride 102.0, aspirin 180.0\nDensity of ethanoic anhydride = 1.08 g cm⁻³\n\nShow that ethanoic anhydride is in excess and calculate the percentage yield of aspirin. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(salicylic acid) = 2.00 ÷ 138.0 = 0.0145 mol ✓\nmass anhydride = 5.0 × 1.08 = 5.40 g; n = 5.40 ÷ 102.0 = 0.0529 mol, which is greater than 0.0145 mol (1 : 1 ratio) so anhydride is in excess ✓\ntheoretical mass of aspirin = 0.0145 × 180.0 = 2.61 g ✓\npercentage yield = 1.82 ÷ 2.609 × 100 ✓\n= 69.8% ✓\nALLOW 69.7% from rounded intermediate values\nECF throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores last three marks only` },
      { q: `Propan-1-ol can be oxidised by acidified potassium dichromate(VI) to give either propanal or propanoic acid, depending on how the experiment is set up. Boiling points: propan-1-ol 97 °C, propanal 48 °C, propanoic acid 141 °C.\n\nDescribe and explain how a student should set up the experiment to obtain (i) propanal and (ii) propanoic acid. Include the colour change seen.`, marks: 5, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `colour change orange → green ✓\npropanal: heat (gently) and distil the product off as it forms ✓\npropanal has the lowest boiling point (no hydrogen bonding between its molecules) so distils off before it is oxidised further ✓\npropanoic acid: heat under reflux (with excess oxidising agent) ✓\nreflux returns propanal to the flask so it is oxidised further to the acid (without losing volatile substances) ✓\nALLOW add the alcohol to the oxidising agent dropwise (for propanal)\nALLOW excess dichromate for propanoic acid` },
      { q: `* A student is asked to make a pure sample of aspirin from about 2 g of salicylic acid and to check its purity.\n\nThe reaction is: HOC₆H₄COOH + (CH₃CO)₂O → CH₃COOC₆H₄COOH + CH₃COOH\nAspirin is much more soluble in hot water than in cold water. Pure aspirin melts at 136 °C; salicylic acid melts at 159 °C.\n\nDescribe how the student should make, purify and test the purity of the aspirin.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nSynthesis\n• weigh salicylic acid into a flask; add excess ethanoic anhydride (in a fume cupboard)\n• add a few drops of concentrated sulfuric/phosphoric acid as catalyst\n• warm in a water bath (about 10–15 minutes) / heat under reflux\n• add (cold) water to hydrolyse excess anhydride; cool in ice to crystallise aspirin\n• filter under reduced pressure (Büchner)\nPurification\n• recrystallise: dissolve in minimum hot water (or hot ethanol then water)\n• cool slowly then in ice; filter under reduced pressure\n• wash with a little ice-cold water; dry (desiccator / low oven)\nTesting purity\n• melting point: pure sample melts sharply at/close to 136 °C; impure melts lower and over a range\n• TLC alongside pure aspirin and salicylic acid: single spot with Rf matching aspirin\n• neutral iron(III) chloride: no purple colour shows no salicylic acid (no phenol)\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `* Read the following account of the history of aspirin.\n\nWillow bark was used to treat pain and fever in the ancient world. In 1763 Edward Stone reported to the Royal Society that powdered willow bark reduced fevers. In 1828 the compound salicin was isolated from willow bark; in the body it is converted into salicylic acid. Salicylic acid and its sodium salt were later made in large quantities from phenol, and used to treat pain, but they tasted unpleasant and irritated the stomach. In 1897 Felix Hoffmann at the Bayer company made acetylsalicylic acid (aspirin), which was sold from 1899.\n\nSalicylic acid: benzene ring with –COOH and –OH on adjacent carbons.\nAspirin: benzene ring with –COOH and –OCOCH₃ on adjacent carbons.\n\nExplain how chemists developed aspirin from willow bark. In your answer, describe the chemical change made to salicylic acid, explain why the change was made, and explain how a chemist could use simple tests and infrared spectroscopy to show that salicylic acid had been converted into aspirin.\n\nData Sheet: O–H (alcohols/phenols) 3200–3600 cm⁻¹; O–H (carboxylic acids) 2500–3300 cm⁻¹; C=O (esters) 1735–1750 cm⁻¹; C=O (carboxylic acids) 1700–1725 cm⁻¹`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nThe chemical change\n• active compound from natural source (salicin) identified and isolated; converted to salicylic acid, later synthesised (from phenol) to give a reliable supply\n• phenol –OH of salicylic acid is converted into an ester group (acylation / esterification)\n• using ethanoic anhydride; ethanoic acid is the other product\nWhy the change was made\n• salicylic acid / its salt irritated the stomach and tasted unpleasant\n• aspirin keeps the active part of the molecule (–COOH next to the ring substituent) but reduces side effects\n• aspirin is hydrolysed in the body back to salicylic acid, so still effective\nShowing conversion\n• neutral iron(III) chloride: salicylic acid gives purple (phenol); pure aspirin does not\n• IR: both show broad O–H 2500–3300 cm⁻¹ (carboxylic acid) and C=O near 1700 cm⁻¹\n• aspirin shows extra ester C=O absorption at 1735–1750 cm⁻¹; salicylic acid shows phenol O–H in 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ region\n• melting point of product compared with data (136 °C, not 159 °C)\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `* A pharmaceutical company uses an alcohol, X, as a starting material. X has the molecular formula C₄H₁₀O. The following data were collected (data written for this practice question).\n\nMass spectrum of X: molecular ion at m/z = 74; large peaks at m/z = 59 and m/z = 45.\nInfrared spectrum of X: broad absorption at 3350 cm⁻¹.\nWhen X is warmed with acidified potassium dichromate(VI), the solution turns from orange to green. The organic product, Y, has a molecular ion at m/z = 72 and an IR absorption at 1715 cm⁻¹, with no broad absorption between 3200 and 3600 cm⁻¹.\nWhen X is heated with aluminium oxide, three isomeric alkenes are formed.\n\nData Sheet: O–H (alcohols) 3200–3600 cm⁻¹; C=O (aldehydes) 1720–1740 cm⁻¹; C=O (ketones) 1705–1725 cm⁻¹\n\nDeduce the identities of X and Y. Explain how each piece of evidence supports your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMass spectrometry\n• M⁺ at 74 confirms Mr of C₄H₁₀O\n• 59 is loss of CH₃ (M − 15): C₃H₇O⁺ / CH₃CH₂CH(OH)⁺\n• 45 is CH₃CHOH⁺ (loss of C₂H₅), showing –CH(OH)– next to a CH₃ group\n• Y at 72 shows loss of 2H on oxidation\nInfrared spectroscopy\n• X: 3350 cm⁻¹ is O–H of an alcohol\n• Y: 1715 cm⁻¹ is C=O, in the ketone range; no O–H so not an alcohol or carboxylic acid\nChemical reactions\n• orange → green: X is a primary or secondary alcohol (not tertiary)\n• product is a ketone, so X is a secondary alcohol\n• three alkenes: but-1-ene and E/Z but-2-ene, only possible from butan-2-ol\n• X is butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃; Y is butanone, CH₃COCH₂CH₃\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'CI': {
    name: 'The Chemical Industry',
    questions: [
      { q: `Most of the world's ammonia is made by the Haber process, in which nitrogen and hydrogen react over an iron catalyst.\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)    ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nWrite the expression for the equilibrium constant, Kc, for this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Kc = [NH₃(g)]² / ([N₂(g)][H₂(g)]³) ✓\nALLOW without state symbols\nDO NOT ALLOW round brackets used for concentrations\nDO NOT ALLOW expression upside down` },
      { q: `A chemical engineer replaces the iron catalyst in an ammonia plant with a more active catalyst. The temperature and pressure of the reactor are kept the same.\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nState the effect, if any, of the new catalyst on the value of Kc.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `No effect / Kc stays the same ✓\nIGNORE equilibrium is reached faster\nDO NOT ALLOW Kc increases` },
      { q: `Nitrogen dioxide in the tail gas from a nitric acid plant can react with carbon monoxide.\nNO₂(g) + CO(g) → NO(g) + CO₂(g)\nBelow 500 K the rate equation for this reaction is: rate = k[NO₂]²\nRate is measured in mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.\n\nDeduce the units of the rate constant, k.`, marks: 1, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW mol⁻¹ dm³ s⁻¹ in any order` },
      { q: `A student follows the acid-catalysed reaction of propanone with iodine using a colorimeter fitted with a blue filter. Propanone and acid are in large excess. The student converts the absorbance readings to iodine concentration and plots [I₂] against time. The graph is a straight line sloping downwards from 0.0020 mol dm⁻³ at time zero to 0.0000 mol dm⁻³ at 400 s.\n\nDeduce the order of reaction with respect to iodine.`, marks: 1, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Zero order (with respect to I₂) ✓\nALLOW 0\nIGNORE explanations` },
      { q: `A fertiliser company checks that a sample of granules contains ammonium ions. The analyst warms the sample with aqueous sodium hydroxide and holds damp red litmus paper at the mouth of the test tube. The litmus turns blue.\n\nWrite the ionic equation, including state symbols, for the reaction that produces the gas.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `NH₄⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → NH₃(g) + H₂O(l) ✓\nAll formulae and state symbols needed for the mark\nALLOW ⇌ for →\nIGNORE spectator ions only if shown on both sides` },
      { q: `The two feedstocks for the Haber process are nitrogen and hydrogen. The hydrogen is usually made by reacting methane from natural gas with steam.\n\nState the raw material from which the nitrogen is obtained.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Air ✓\nALLOW fractional distillation of (liquefied) air\nALLOW air, after oxygen is removed by burning some of the hydrogen/methane\nDO NOT ALLOW the atmosphere's nitrogen cycle / soil` },
      { q: `Ammonium nitrate fertiliser is made by neutralising nitric acid with ammonia.\nNH₃(g) + HNO₃(aq) → NH₄NO₃(aq)\n\nState the atom economy of this reaction for making ammonium nitrate.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `100% ✓\nIGNORE explanation that there is only one product` },
      { q: `The Haber process is used to make ammonia.\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nDeduce the units of Kc for this equilibrium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `(mol dm⁻³)² / ((mol dm⁻³)(mol dm⁻³)³) OR (mol dm⁻³)⁻² ✓\ndm⁶ mol⁻² ✓\nALLOW mol⁻² dm⁶\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `In an ammonia plant the reactor temperature is raised from 400 °C to 500 °C while the pressure is kept constant.\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)    ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nExplain what happens to the value of Kc.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Kc decreases ✓\n(Forward) reaction is exothermic, so increasing temperature moves the equilibrium in the endothermic direction / to the left OR ratio of [products] to [reactants] at equilibrium decreases ✓\nALLOW backward reaction is endothermic\nDO NOT ALLOW Kc decreases because rate of forward reaction decreases\nSecond mark depends on first` },
      { q: `In the Ostwald process, some of the nitrogen dioxide formed dimerises.\n2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g)\nThe table gives values of Kc at two temperatures (data written for this practice question).\nTemperature 298 K: Kc = 170 mol⁻¹ dm³\nTemperature 350 K: Kc = 6.5 mol⁻¹ dm³\n\nDeduce, with a reason, whether the forward reaction is exothermic or endothermic.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Exothermic ✓\nKc decreases as temperature increases, so the equilibrium shifts towards the reactants / left at higher temperature, which is the endothermic direction ✓\nALLOW the reverse argument for decreasing temperature\nSecond mark depends on first` },
      { q: `The reaction between nitrogen monoxide and hydrogen has been studied as a way of removing NO from waste gases.\n2NO(g) + 2H₂(g) → N₂(g) + 2H₂O(g)\nThe rate equation is: rate = k[NO]²[H₂]\nA chemist proposes this two-step mechanism:\nStep 1: 2NO + H₂ → N₂O + H₂O\nStep 2: N₂O + H₂ → N₂ + H₂O\n\nExplain which step is the rate-determining step.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Step 1 ✓\nThe rate equation contains two NO and one H₂, which are the species/numbers of each species that react in step 1 OR species in the rate equation are those in (or before) the rate-determining step ✓\nALLOW step 1 is the slow step\nSecond mark depends on first` },
      { q: `Dinitrogen pentoxide decomposes when it is heated.\n2N₂O₅(g) → 4NO₂(g) + O₂(g)\nA chemist measures [N₂O₅] at intervals at constant temperature (data written for this practice question).\nTime / s: 0, 600, 1200, 1800, 2400\n[N₂O₅] / mol dm⁻³: 0.0800, 0.0566, 0.0400, 0.0283, 0.0200\n\nExplain how these data show that the reaction is first order with respect to N₂O₅.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Half-life is constant ✓\nConcentration halves every 1200 s, e.g. 0.0800 → 0.0400 in 1200 s AND 0.0400 → 0.0200 in a further 1200 s ✓\nALLOW 0.0566 → 0.0283 in 1200 s\nALLOW successive half-lives quoted from any pair of points in the table` },
      { q: `The thermal decomposition of dinitrogen pentoxide is first order with respect to N₂O₅. At a certain temperature its half-life is 1200 s.\n\nCalculate the rate constant, k, for the reaction at this temperature. Give units in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `k = ln 2 / t½ = 0.693 / 1200 ✓\nk = 5.78 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹ ✓ (value and units)\nALLOW 5.8 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `Farmers compare nitrogen fertilisers by the percentage by mass of nitrogen they contain.\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of nitrogen in ammonium nitrate, NH₄NO₃.\nAr values: H 1.0, N 14.0, O 16.0`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mr NH₄NO₃ = 80.0 ✓\n% N = (2 × 14.0 / 80.0) × 100 = 35.0% ✓\nALLOW 35%\nECF from incorrect Mr\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `In the final stage of the Ostwald process, nitrogen dioxide is absorbed in water.\n3NO₂(g) + H₂O(l) → 2HNO₃(aq) + NO(g)\n\nUse oxidation states to explain why this reaction is described as disproportionation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `N in NO₂ is +4; N in HNO₃ is +5 AND N in NO is +2 ✓\nThe same element (nitrogen) is both oxidised and reduced (in the same reaction) ✓\nALLOW nitrogen goes up and down in oxidation state\nSecond mark can be awarded without all three oxidation states if idea of same element oxidised and reduced is clear` },
      { q: `In a Haber process plant, only about 15% of the nitrogen and hydrogen is converted to ammonia each time the gases pass over the catalyst. However, the overall conversion of the feedstock to ammonia is about 97%.\n\nExplain how the plant achieves such a high overall conversion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Ammonia is removed by cooling / condensing it to a liquid ✓\nUnreacted nitrogen and hydrogen are recycled / passed back over the catalyst ✓\nIGNORE references to Le Chatelier unless contradictory` },
      { q: `A student uses an iodine clock reaction to find the order of reaction with respect to peroxodisulfate ions. She times each run by eye with a stopwatch and estimates the uncertainty in each time to be ±1 s, because of judging when the blue-black colour appears.\nRun A took 42 s. Run B, at a higher concentration, took 8 s.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in each time and comment on what your answers show.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Run A 2.4% AND Run B 12.5% ✓\nALLOW 2% and 13%\nPercentage uncertainty is much larger for the fast/short run, so runs at high concentration are less reliable OR longer times should be used (e.g. by diluting) ✓` },
      { q: `A sample of gases from an ammonia reactor is analysed when equilibrium has been reached at a fixed temperature (data written for this practice question).\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n[N₂] = 0.400 mol dm⁻³, [H₂] = 1.20 mol dm⁻³, [NH₃] = 0.0960 mol dm⁻³\n\nCalculate the value of Kc at this temperature and give its units. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Kc = 0.0960² / (0.400 × 1.20³) ✓\n= 0.0133 ✓\ndm⁶ mol⁻² ✓\nALLOW 1.33 × 10⁻²\nALLOW mol⁻² dm⁶\nUnits mark is independent\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `The operators of an ammonia plant increase the total pressure from 150 atm to 250 atm at constant temperature.\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\nKc = [NH₃]² / ([N₂][H₂]³)\n\nExplain, in terms of Kc, why the equilibrium yield of ammonia increases.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Kc does not change (pressure does not affect Kc; only temperature does) ✓\nIncreasing pressure increases all concentrations, but the denominator (power 4 in total) increases more than the numerator (power 2), so the concentration ratio becomes smaller than Kc ✓\nTo restore the ratio to Kc, [NH₃] must increase / equilibrium moves to the right ✓\nALLOW 4 moles of gas on left and 2 on right only for the second mark if linked to the concentration expression\nDO NOT ALLOW Kc increases` },
      { q: `A chemist investigates the reaction between nitrogen monoxide and hydrogen at 1100 K.\n2NO(g) + 2H₂(g) → N₂(g) + 2H₂O(g)\nThe initial rate was measured for three mixtures (data written for this practice question).\nExperiment 1: [NO] = 0.0020 mol dm⁻³, [H₂] = 0.0040 mol dm⁻³, initial rate = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹\nExperiment 2: [NO] = 0.0040 mol dm⁻³, [H₂] = 0.0040 mol dm⁻³, initial rate = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹\nExperiment 3: [NO] = 0.0040 mol dm⁻³, [H₂] = 0.0080 mol dm⁻³, initial rate = 2.4 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹\n\nDeduce the order with respect to each reactant and write the rate equation.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Second order with respect to NO: from experiments 1 and 2, [NO] doubles and rate increases × 4 ✓\nFirst order with respect to H₂: from experiments 2 and 3, [H₂] doubles and rate doubles ✓\nrate = k[NO]²[H₂] ✓\nECF rate equation from incorrect orders` },
      { q: `For the reaction of nitrogen monoxide with hydrogen at 1100 K, rate = k[NO]²[H₂].\nIn one experiment (data written for this practice question):\n[NO] = 0.0020 mol dm⁻³, [H₂] = 0.0040 mol dm⁻³, initial rate = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹\n\nCalculate the rate constant, k, and give its units.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `k = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ / (0.0020² × 0.0040) ✓\n= 1.9 × 10³ ✓\ndm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW 1875 / 1880 / 1.88 × 10³\nALLOW units in any order\nUnits mark is independent\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `The Ostwald process converts ammonia into nitric acid in three stages.\nStage 1: 4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g)\nStage 2: 2NO(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO₂(g)\nStage 3: 3NO₂(g) + H₂O(l) → 2HNO₃(aq) + NO(g)\n\nGive the oxidation state of nitrogen in NH₃, NO and NO₂.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `NH₃: −3 ✓\nNO: +2 ✓\nNO₂: +4 ✓\nALLOW 3−, 2+, 4+\nDO NOT ALLOW 3, 2, 4 with no sign (penalise once only)` },
      { q: `A technician is given an unlabelled bag of white fertiliser granules and is asked to find out whether they contain ammonium ions.\n\nDescribe the test the technician should carry out and give the result for ammonium ions.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Dissolve (some granules) in water and add aqueous sodium hydroxide ✓\nWarm (gently) ✓\nGas turns damp red litmus / universal indicator paper blue ✓\nALLOW alkaline gas / ammonia given off with correct indicator colour\nDO NOT ALLOW blue litmus turns red\nDO NOT ALLOW dry litmus for third mark` },
      { q: `Only certain bacteria and lightning can convert atmospheric nitrogen into compounds that plants can use. In a lightning flash, nitrogen and oxygen in the air react.\nN₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO(g)\nIn soil, nitrifying bacteria convert ammonium ions into nitrate ions.\n\nExplain why the reaction of nitrogen with oxygen only happens at very high temperatures, and state the type of reaction that happens to nitrogen when NH₄⁺ is converted to NO₃⁻.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `N₂ has a (strong) triple bond / N≡N ✓\nWhich has a very high bond enthalpy so the reaction has a very high activation enthalpy (only enough energy at lightning temperatures) ✓\nOxidation (N from −3 to +5) ✓\nALLOW N≡N bond enthalpy 945 kJ mol⁻¹\nIGNORE nitrogen is unreactive / inert without reason` },
      { q: `In an ammonia converter at a certain temperature, Kc = 0.105 dm⁶ mol⁻² (data written for this practice question). A sample of the gas mixture has the following concentrations:\n[N₂] = 0.500 mol dm⁻³, [H₂] = 0.800 mol dm⁻³, [NH₃] = 0.200 mol dm⁻³\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nDeduce whether the mixture is at equilibrium. If it is not, predict the direction in which the reaction will go.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `[NH₃]² / ([N₂][H₂]³) = 0.200² / (0.500 × 0.800³) ✓\n= 0.156 ✓\nThis is greater than Kc, so the mixture is not at equilibrium and the reaction goes to the left / ammonia decomposes (until the ratio falls to 0.105) ✓\nECF for direction from incorrect value of the ratio\nALLOW 0.15625` },
      { q: `In the Ostwald process, nitrogen monoxide is oxidised to nitrogen dioxide.\n2NO(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g)\nIn a laboratory model of this stage (data written for this practice question), 2.00 mol of NO and 1.00 mol of O₂ were sealed in a 10.0 dm³ vessel and allowed to reach equilibrium at constant temperature. The equilibrium mixture contained 1.60 mol of NO₂.\n\nCalculate the value of Kc at this temperature and give its units.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Equilibrium moles: NO = 0.40 mol AND O₂ = 0.20 mol ✓\nEquilibrium concentrations: [NO₂] = 0.160, [NO] = 0.0400, [O₂] = 0.0200 mol dm⁻³ ✓\nKc = 0.160² / (0.0400² × 0.0200) = 800 ✓\ndm³ mol⁻¹ ✓\nECF from incorrect equilibrium moles\nIf moles used without dividing by volume, Kc = 80 scores max 2 (first mark + units)\nUnits mark is independent` },
      { q: `A student determines Kc for the formation of an ester, a reaction used to make solvents in the chemical industry.\nCH₃COOH(l) + C₂H₅OH(l) ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅(l) + H₂O(l)\nShe mixes 0.200 mol of ethanoic acid with 0.200 mol of ethanol and a few drops of concentrated hydrochloric acid as catalyst, then leaves the sealed flask for a week at room temperature. She titrates the mixture with standard sodium hydroxide. After allowing for the hydrochloric acid, she finds that 0.0680 mol of ethanoic acid remains (data written for this practice question). Assume no water was present at the start.\n\nCalculate the value of Kc and state its units, if any.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Ethanol at equilibrium = 0.0680 mol ✓\nEster AND water at equilibrium = 0.200 − 0.0680 = 0.132 mol ✓\nKc = (0.132 × 0.132) / (0.0680 × 0.0680) = 3.77 ✓\nNo units, because the volume terms cancel ✓\nALLOW 3.8\nECF from incorrect equilibrium moles` },
      { q: `Urea is the most widely used solid nitrogen fertiliser. It is made from ammonia and carbon dioxide.\n2NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) → (NH₂)₂CO(s) + H₂O(l)\nMr values: NH₃ 17.03, CO₂ 44.01, (NH₂)₂CO 60.06\nIn one day a plant uses 1.70 tonnes of ammonia, with carbon dioxide in excess, and makes 2.70 tonnes of urea (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the atom economy of the reaction for making urea and the percentage yield of urea.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Atom economy = 60.06 / (2 × 17.03 + 44.01) × 100 ✓\n= 76.9% ✓\nTheoretical mass of urea = (1.70 / 17.03) × ½ × 60.06 = 3.00 tonnes ✓\nPercentage yield = 2.70 / 3.00 × 100 = 90.0% ✓\nALLOW 77% and 90%\nECF from incorrect theoretical mass\nCorrect answers with no working score all marks` },
      { q: `The catalyst in the Haber process is iron. It is used in a finely divided, porous form.\n\nExplain how the iron catalyst increases the rate of reaction and why it has no effect on the equilibrium yield of ammonia.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Nitrogen and hydrogen are adsorbed onto the iron surface / active sites, weakening the bonds in the reactant molecules ✓\nThis provides an alternative route with a lower activation enthalpy, so more collisions/molecules have enough energy to react ✓\nFinely divided / porous gives a larger surface area, so more active sites ✓\nThe catalyst speeds up forward and reverse reactions equally OR does not change Kc, so the equilibrium position / yield is unchanged (it is reached faster) ✓\nALLOW heterogeneous catalyst for first mark only if adsorption idea present\nIGNORE references to catalyst not being used up` },
      { q: `A student follows the reaction of propanone with iodine in acid solution using a colorimeter. Propanone and acid are in large excess, so their concentrations stay almost constant.\nCH₃COCH₃(aq) + I₂(aq) → CH₃COCH₂I(aq) + H⁺(aq) + I⁻(aq)\nShe records absorbance every 30 s for 6 minutes.\n\nDescribe how she should process her results to find the order with respect to iodine, and explain what a zero-order result suggests about the rate-determining step.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Use a calibration curve (absorbance against known [I₂]) to convert absorbance readings into [I₂] ✓\nPlot [I₂] against time ✓\nA straight line (constant gradient) shows zero order with respect to iodine ✓\nIodine is not involved in the rate-determining step / iodine reacts in a fast step after the rate-determining step ✓\nALLOW absorbance against time is a straight line for the second and third marks if student states absorbance is proportional to [I₂]\nALLOW the rate-determining step involves only propanone and H⁺` },
      { q: `A chemist measures the rate constant for the decomposition of dinitrogen pentoxide at two temperatures (data written for this practice question).\nAt 298 K: k = 3.50 × 10⁻⁵ s⁻¹\nAt 328 K: k = 1.40 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹\nThe Arrhenius equation can be written as ln k = −Ea/RT + ln A.\nR = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the activation enthalpy, Ea, for the reaction in kJ mol⁻¹. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1/T values: 3.356 × 10⁻³ AND 3.049 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹ ✓\nln k values: −10.26 AND −6.571 ✓\nGradient = (−6.571 − (−10.26)) / (3.049 × 10⁻³ − 3.356 × 10⁻³) = −1.20 × 10⁴ K ✓\nEa = −gradient × R = 1.20 × 10⁴ × 8.314 = 9.99 × 10⁴ J mol⁻¹ ✓\nEa = 99.9 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW 99.5–100.5 kJ mol⁻¹ depending on rounding\nALLOW use of ln(k₂/k₁) = (Ea/R)(1/T₁ − 1/T₂)\nECF throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A fertiliser plant makes ammonium nitrate using only ammonia as its nitrogen feedstock. Part of the ammonia goes to an Ostwald plant, where the overall conversion of NH₃ to HNO₃ is 94.0% (one mole of NH₃ gives at most one mole of HNO₃). The rest of the ammonia is used to neutralise all the nitric acid produced.\nNH₃ + HNO₃ → NH₄NO₃\nMr values: NH₃ 17.03, NH₄NO₃ 80.04\n\nCalculate the maximum mass, in tonnes, of ammonium nitrate that can be made from 1.00 tonne of ammonia. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Moles of NH₃ = 1.00 × 10⁶ / 17.03 = 5.87 × 10⁴ mol ✓\nIf x mol goes to the Ostwald plant, HNO₃ formed = 0.940x mol, which needs 0.940x mol NH₃ to neutralise ✓\nx + 0.940x = 5.87 × 10⁴, so x = 3.03 × 10⁴ mol ✓\nMoles NH₄NO₃ = 0.940x = 2.85 × 10⁴ mol ✓\nMass = 2.85 × 10⁴ × 80.04 = 2.28 × 10⁶ g = 2.28 tonnes ✓\nALLOW 2.27–2.28 tonnes\nIf 94% yield ignored, answer 2.35 tonnes scores max 3\nECF throughout` },
      { q: `A typical ammonia plant operates at about 450 °C and 200 atm with an iron catalyst. Under these conditions about 15% of the reactants are converted to ammonia in each pass through the reactor.\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)    ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹\n\n*Explain why these conditions are chosen, referring to the effects of temperature and pressure on the rate of reaction and on the equilibrium yield, and to economic and safety factors.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nTemperature\n• Forward reaction is exothermic, so low temperature gives a higher equilibrium yield / larger Kc\n• Low temperature gives a slow rate; molecules have less energy, fewer exceed the activation enthalpy\n• 450 °C is a compromise between rate and yield; the iron catalyst only works well (is active) at around this temperature\nPressure\n• 4 moles of gas on the left and 2 on the right, so high pressure moves the equilibrium to the right / increases yield\n• High pressure increases concentration so increases rate\n• Very high pressure is expensive (energy for compressors, thick-walled pipes and vessels) and increases the risk of leaks/explosion, so 200 atm is a compromise\nEconomics and other factors\n• A fast rate with a low yield per pass is acceptable because ammonia is removed by condensing and unreacted gases are recycled\n• Catalyst lowers activation enthalpy so a lower temperature can be used, saving energy costs\n• Hydrogen is flammable, so plant must avoid leaks / ignition sources; ammonia is toxic\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `The reaction of propanone with iodine is catalysed by acid.\nCH₃COCH₃(aq) + I₂(aq) → CH₃COCH₂I(aq) + H⁺(aq) + I⁻(aq)\nA student is provided with 1.00 mol dm⁻³ propanone, 1.00 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid, 0.0050 mol dm⁻³ iodine solution, distilled water, a colorimeter with a blue filter, cuvettes, burettes and a stopwatch.\n\n*Plan an experiment using the initial-rates method to determine the orders of reaction with respect to propanone and to hydrogen ions. Explain how the results would be processed and how the orders would be deduced.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• Calibrate colorimeter with distilled water (zero absorbance); make up iodine standards to produce a calibration curve of absorbance against [I₂]\n• Use burettes to measure volumes; keep total volume constant by adding distilled water\n• Vary [propanone] (e.g. 5, 10, 15, 20 cm³) keeping [H⁺] and [I₂] constant; then vary [H⁺] keeping others constant\n• Iodine added last; start stopwatch on mixing; transfer quickly to cuvette; record absorbance every 15–30 s\n• Control temperature (e.g. water bath / same room temperature)\nProcessing\n• Convert absorbance to [I₂] using calibration curve\n• Plot [I₂] against time for each run; initial rate = gradient of the (straight) line / tangent at t = 0\n• Repeat runs and calculate mean rates\nDeducing orders\n• Plot initial rate against [propanone] (or compare rates when concentration doubles)\n• Straight line through origin / rate doubles when concentration doubles means first order; rate × 4 means second order; rate unchanged means zero order\n• Same analysis for [H⁺]; write rate equation\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A company is deciding how to make hydrogen for a new ammonia plant (data written for this practice question).\nRoute A (steam reforming): CH₄(g) + 2H₂O(g) → CO₂(g) + 4H₂(g). Feedstock: natural gas. About 2.0 tonnes of CO₂ released per tonne of ammonia made. Cost of hydrogen: low.\nRoute B (electrolysis of water): 2H₂O(l) → 2H₂(g) + O₂(g). Electricity from a nearby wind farm. Almost no CO₂ released in operation. Cost of hydrogen: about three times that of Route A. Oxygen by-product can be sold.\nAr values: H 1.0, C 12.0, O 16.0\n\n*Evaluate the two routes for making hydrogen for ammonia production. Include atom economy calculations and consider sustainability, cost and other factors.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nAtom economy\n• Route A: 4 × 2.0 / (16.0 + 2 × 18.0) × 100 = 8.0 / 52.0 × 100 = 15.4%\n• Route B: 2 × 2.0 / (2 × 18.0) × 100 = 11.1%; but if the oxygen is sold it becomes a useful product and atom economy is effectively 100%\n• Atom economy alone is misleading; waste CO₂ from Route A is a greenhouse gas\nSustainability\n• Route A uses a finite fossil fuel feedstock (natural gas); Route B uses renewable electricity and water\n• Route A releases large amounts of CO₂, contributing to climate change; Route B almost none in operation\n• CO₂ from Route A could be captured or used (e.g. to make urea), reducing its impact\nCost and other factors\n• Route A is cheaper and is established technology; Route B hydrogen costs about three times as much\n• Route B depends on wind (intermittent supply), so hydrogen storage or backup may be needed\n• Price of natural gas varies; carbon taxes may make Route A more expensive in future\n• Hydrogen is flammable in both routes; electrolysis plant also produces oxygen, which increases fire risk\n• Reasoned conclusion that weighs the factors\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'PL': {
    name: 'Polymers and Life',
    questions: [
      { q: `Nylon-6,6 is manufactured by reacting hexane-1,6-diamine, H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂, with hexanedioic acid, HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH. Each time a new link forms in the chain, a molecule of water is released.\n\nState the type of polymerisation that produces nylon-6,6.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Condensation (polymerisation) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW addition` },
      { q: `Kevlar is a polyamide used in body armour. It can be made in the laboratory from benzene-1,4-diamine and benzene-1,4-dicarbonyl dichloride, ClOC–C₆H₄–COCl.\n\nState the formula of the small molecule eliminated each time an amide link forms in this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `HCl ✓\nALLOW hydrogen chloride\nDO NOT ALLOW H₂O` },
      { q: `Glycine, H₂NCH₂COOH, is the simplest amino acid found in proteins. A sample of glycine is dissolved in hydrochloric acid of pH 1.\n\nWrite the structural formula of the form of glycine present in this solution.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `⁺H₃NCH₂COOH ✓\nALLOW H₃N⁺CH₂COOH or displayed formula with charge on N\nDO NOT ALLOW zwitterion ⁺H₃NCH₂COO⁻` },
      { q: `Threonine is an essential amino acid in the diet. Its structure is CH₃CH(OH)CH(NH₂)COOH.\n\nState the number of chiral centres in a molecule of threonine.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `2 ✓` },
      { q: `In DNA, the two polynucleotide strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between pairs of bases. The four bases are adenine, thymine, cytosine and guanine.\n\nState the base that pairs with cytosine.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Guanine ✓\nALLOW G` },
      { q: `A chemist runs the ¹H NMR spectrum of a sample of hexane-1,6-diamine made for nylon manufacture. A small amount of tetramethylsilane, TMS, is added to the sample.\n\nState why TMS is added.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `As a (chemical shift) reference / standard, giving δ = 0 ✓\nIGNORE to calibrate the machine unless linked to δ = 0\nIGNORE it is inert / volatile` },
      { q: `Keratin in hair is a protein in which much of each polypeptide chain is coiled into an α-helix.\n\nState the type of bond between different parts of the chain that holds the α-helix in shape.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Hydrogen bond(s) ✓\nALLOW between C=O and N–H groups of the backbone\nDO NOT ALLOW peptide bonds / disulfide bridges` },
      { q: `Butylamine, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂, reacts with hydrochloric acid to form a salt, butylammonium chloride.\n\nState the property of butylamine shown by this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `(Acts as a) base / proton acceptor ✓\nALLOW alkaline\nIGNORE nucleophile` },
      { q: `Hexane-1,6-diamine, H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂, is one of the monomers for nylon-6,6.\n\nDeduce the number of peaks in the ¹³C NMR spectrum of hexane-1,6-diamine.`, marks: 1, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `3 ✓` },
      { q: `A student uses thin-layer chromatography to identify the amino acids present in a hydrolysed protein. She draws a baseline on the TLC plate before spotting the samples.\n\nState why the baseline should be drawn in pencil rather than ink.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Pencil (graphite) does not dissolve in / move with the solvent ✓\nALLOW ink would dissolve and form spots / separate\nIGNORE pencil can be rubbed out` },
      { q: `Alanine, CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH, exists as two optical isomers. Only one of these is found in human proteins.\n\nState how the two optical isomers of alanine differ in their effect on plane-polarised light.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `They rotate the plane of plane-polarised light in opposite directions (by the same amount) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW one rotates and the other does not` },
      { q: `Amines are used in the manufacture of nylons and of many drugs. Butylamine, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂, reacts with hydrochloric acid to form a salt.\n\nWrite an equation for this reaction. Show the structure of the salt formed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂ + HCl → CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₃⁺Cl⁻\nCorrect formula of salt showing NH₃⁺ ✓\nRest of equation correct and balanced ✓\nALLOW C₄H₉NH₃⁺Cl⁻ for salt\nALLOW H⁺ in place of HCl, giving C₄H₉NH₃⁺ (2 marks)` },
      { q: `Ethylamine, CH₃CH₂NH₂, dissolves in water to give an alkaline solution.\n\nExplain why ethylamine behaves as a base. Refer to its structure in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Nitrogen (atom) has a lone pair (of electrons) ✓\nThe lone pair accepts a proton / H⁺ (forming a dative covalent bond), giving CH₃CH₂NH₃⁺ ✓\nALLOW lone pair forms bond with H⁺ from water, releasing OH⁻` },
      { q: `Nylon-6,6 for textile fibres has the repeat unit –NH(CH₂)₆NH–CO(CH₂)₄CO–, formula C₁₂H₂₂N₂O₂. A batch of nylon-6,6 has an average Mr of 35 000.\n\nCalculate the average number of repeat units in each polymer chain. Use Ar values: C = 12.0, H = 1.0, N = 14.0, O = 16.0.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mr of repeat unit = 226.0 ✓\nNumber of repeat units = 35 000 ÷ 226.0 = 155 ✓\nALLOW 154.9 / 154.87 / 154 / 155\nECF from incorrect Mr of repeat unit\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Amino acids such as glycine are white crystalline solids that decompose at over 200 °C rather than melting at a low temperature, even though their Mr values are small.\n\nExplain why amino acids have such high melting points.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Amino acids exist as zwitterions (in the solid), e.g. ⁺H₃NCH₂COO⁻ ✓\nStrong (electrostatic) attractions between oppositely charged ions / ionic bonding between zwitterions need a lot of energy to overcome ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW covalent bonds broken` },
      { q: `The tertiary structure of an enzyme is held in place by interactions between the R groups of amino acid residues in different parts of the folded chain.\n\nIdentify two types of interaction between R groups and, for each, give a pair of amino acid side chains that could form it. Side chains you may use: –CH₂SH (cysteine), –CH₂OH (serine), –(CH₂)₄NH₂ (lysine), –CH₂COOH (aspartic acid), –CH(CH₃)₂ (valine).`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Any two from:\nDisulfide bridge (S–S) between two cysteine side chains ✓\nIonic attraction between –NH₃⁺ of lysine and –COO⁻ of aspartic acid ✓\nHydrogen bonding between, e.g., two serine –OH groups / serine and aspartic acid ✓\nInstantaneous dipole–induced dipole bonds between two valine side chains ✓\nEach mark requires type AND correct pair` },
      { q: `Biological washing powders contain proteases that remove protein stains. If the wash temperature is too high, the enzymes stop working.\n\nExplain why a high temperature stops the enzyme working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(Weak) bonds holding the tertiary structure, e.g. hydrogen bonds, are broken ✓\nThe shape of the active site changes so the substrate no longer fits / binds (enzyme is denatured) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW peptide bonds broken\nDO NOT ALLOW enzyme is killed` },
      { q: `Poly(ethylene terephthalate), PET, used in drinks bottles, is a polyester made from benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid and ethane-1,2-diol. In one recycling method, PET is heated with aqueous sodium hydroxide.\n\nGive the names or structures of the two organic products of this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Ethane-1,2-diol / HOCH₂CH₂OH ✓\nBenzene-1,4-dicarboxylate (ion) / disodium benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate / ⁻OOC–C₆H₄–COO⁻ ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid for second mark` },
      { q: `In DNA, adenine always pairs with thymine and cytosine always pairs with guanine.\n\nExplain why adenine pairs with thymine and not with cytosine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Adenine and thymine can form two hydrogen bonds (cytosine and guanine form three) ✓\nOnly with thymine are the hydrogen-bonding groups in the correct positions / shapes complementary so that H bonds can line up (adenine and cytosine cannot H-bond effectively) ✓\nALLOW pairing keeps the distance between the two strands constant (one large and one small base) for second mark` },
      { q: `A student hydrolyses a sample of a protein and runs a thin-layer chromatogram of the hydrolysate. After locating the spots, one spot is 3.6 cm from the baseline and the solvent front is 8.0 cm from the baseline. (data written for this practice question)\n\n| Amino acid | Rf value |\n| --- | --- |\n| glycine | 0.26 |\n| alanine | 0.38 |\n| valine | 0.45 |\n| leucine | 0.60 |\n\nCalculate the Rf value of the spot and identify the amino acid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Rf = 3.6 ÷ 8.0 = 0.45 ✓\nValine ✓\nECF identification from incorrect Rf` },
      { q: `Alanine, CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH, is a chiral amino acid.\n\nDraw 3-D structures of the two optical isomers of alanine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `One correct 3-D (tetrahedral) structure, using wedge and dashed bonds, with H, CH₃, NH₂ and COOH around the central carbon ✓\nSecond structure is the mirror image ✓\nALLOW zwitterion forms\nDO NOT ALLOW structures that are simply rotated versions of each other` },
      { q: `A research chemist makes an amide by adding ethanoyl chloride, CH₃COCl, to excess ethylamine, CH₃CH₂NH₂. The reaction is vigorous at room temperature.\n\nWrite an equation for this reaction, assuming the HCl produced reacts with excess ethylamine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CH₃COCl + 2CH₃CH₂NH₂ → CH₃CONHCH₂CH₃ + CH₃CH₂NH₃⁺Cl⁻\nCorrect amide CH₃CONHCH₂CH₃ ✓\nCorrect salt and balancing (2 mol amine) ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for CH₃COCl + CH₃CH₂NH₂ → CH₃CONHCH₂CH₃ + HCl` },
      { q: `Enzymes such as lactase are used in industry to break down lactose in milk. The reaction happens rapidly at 37 °C, whereas uncatalysed hydrolysis of lactose at this temperature is extremely slow.\n\nExplain, in terms of activation enthalpy, how an enzyme increases the rate of a reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Enzyme provides an alternative reaction route/pathway with a lower activation enthalpy ✓\nSo a greater proportion of collisions/molecules have energy ≥ activation enthalpy (so more successful collisions per second) ✓\nIGNORE enzyme lowers the activation enthalpy of the reaction for the first mark unless alternative route given` },
      { q: `Ethylamine, CH₃CH₂NH₂, shows a quartet in its ¹H NMR spectrum for the protons of the CH₂ group.\n\nExplain why this peak is split into a quartet.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The CH₂ protons are next to a CH₃ group / 3 H on the adjacent carbon ✓\nn + 1 rule: 3 + 1 = 4 peaks ✓\nIGNORE references to NH₂ protons` },
      { q: `In an industrial process, an enzyme converts substrate S into product. When S is in large excess, doubling the concentration of the enzyme doubles the initial rate.\n\nExplain this observation in terms of active sites.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `With substrate in excess, all active sites are occupied (rate limited by number of active sites) ✓\nDoubling enzyme concentration doubles the number of active sites so twice as many substrate molecules are converted per second / reaction is first order with respect to enzyme ✓` },
      { q: `Kevlar is a polyamide made from benzene-1,4-diamine and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid. Its chains are straight and rigid because of the benzene rings, and the chains line up side by side. Kevlar is about five times stronger than steel of the same mass.\n\nExplain why Kevlar is so strong.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Hydrogen bonds form between C=O of one chain and N–H of a neighbouring chain ✓\nStraight chains pack closely/align in parallel so there are many hydrogen bonds (regularly spaced) along the chains ✓\nALLOW sheets of chains held by H bonds` },
      { q: `In the "nylon rope trick" a solution of decanedioyl dichloride, ClOC(CH₂)₈COCl, in cyclohexane is carefully poured onto an aqueous solution of hexane-1,6-diamine. Nylon forms at the interface and can be pulled out as a continuous thread.\n\nSuggest why the diacyl dichloride is used instead of decanedioic acid, and state one safety precaution needed in addition to eye protection and gloves.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Acyl chlorides are more reactive (so the reaction happens quickly at room temperature / no heating or catalyst needed) ✓\nWork in a fume cupboard because HCl (fumes) is produced / cyclohexane vapour is flammable – no naked flames ✓\nALLOW diacyl chloride / amine is corrosive or irritant so avoid contact – DO NOT ALLOW wear gloves (already given)` },
      { q: `Poly(lactic acid), PLA, is a biodegradable polyester used for compostable food packaging. It is made from lactic acid, CH₃CH(OH)COOH.\n\nDraw the repeat unit of PLA and name the other product of the polymerisation.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Repeat unit –O–CH(CH₃)–CO– with correct carbon skeleton ✓\nEster linkage shown in full (C=O and C–O) with open bonds at both ends ✓\nWater / H₂O ✓\nALLOW –CH(CH₃)–COO– or –CO–O–CH(CH₃)–\nDO NOT ALLOW repeat unit with extra H or OH at the ends` },
      { q: `Glycine, H₂NCH₂COOH, and alanine, H₂NCH(CH₃)COOH, can join together to form a dipeptide.\n\nDraw the structure of ONE dipeptide that could be formed from glycine and alanine, showing the peptide link in full, and name the other product.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Correct dipeptide, e.g. H₂NCH₂CONHCH(CH₃)COOH or H₂NCH(CH₃)CONHCH₂COOH ✓\nPeptide link –C(=O)–N(H)– displayed with all bonds ✓\nWater ✓\nALLOW zwitterion of dipeptide` },
      { q: `To find the amino acids in a dipeptide, Gly-Ala (H₂NCH₂CONHCH(CH₃)COOH), a chemist heats it under reflux with 6 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid for several hours.\n\nExplain what happens to the dipeptide and give the structures of the organic products formed in this acidic solution.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The peptide / amide bond is hydrolysed (broken by reaction with water) ✓\n⁺H₃NCH₂COOH ✓\n⁺H₃NCH(CH₃)COOH ✓\nALLOW as chloride salts\nALLOW 1 mark out of the last 2 for neutral amino acids H₂NCH₂COOH and H₂NCH(CH₃)COOH` },
      { q: `Lactase is used in the dairy industry to hydrolyse lactose, making lactose-free milk. Lactase does not catalyse the hydrolysis of sucrose, even though sucrose is also a disaccharide.\n\nExplain why lactase is specific for lactose.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The active site has a specific (3-D) shape ✓\nOnly lactose has a shape complementary to the active site / fits (lock-and-key), or the active site changes shape slightly to fit lactose (induced fit) ✓\nLactose is held by intermolecular bonds/interactions (e.g. hydrogen bonds, ionic interactions) with R groups in the active site; sucrose cannot form these bonds/does not fit ✓` },
      { q: `A student makes nylon-6,10 by the "nylon rope trick". She uses 1.16 g of hexane-1,6-diamine, H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂ (Mr = 116.0), with an excess of decanedioyl dichloride. After washing and drying, the nylon thread has a mass of 1.95 g. The repeat unit of nylon-6,10 is –NH(CH₂)₆NH–CO(CH₂)₈CO–, Mr = 282.0. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of nylon-6,10. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Amount of diamine = 1.16 ÷ 116.0 = 0.0100 mol ✓\nTheoretical mass of nylon = 0.0100 × 282.0 = 2.82 g ✓\nPercentage yield = 1.95 ÷ 2.82 × 100 = 69.1% ✓\nECF at each stage\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Compound X, C₃H₉N, is an amine used to make a herbicide. Its ¹H NMR spectrum is summarised below. (data written for this practice question)\n\n| Chemical shift δ / ppm | Relative peak area | Splitting |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0.9 | 3 | triplet |\n| 1.1 | 2 | singlet |\n| 1.5 | 2 | sextet |\n| 2.6 | 2 | triplet |\n\nDeduce the structure of X. Explain how the splitting supports your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `X is propan-1-amine / CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ ✓\nTriplets at 0.9 and 2.6: CH₃ and CH₂N each next to a CH₂ (2 + 1 = 3) ✓\nSextet at 1.5: central CH₂ has 5 adjacent H (3 + 2) AND singlet (area 2) is NH₂ ✓` },
      { q: `Two isomeric amines, butan-1-amine, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂, and 2-methylpropan-2-amine, (CH₃)₃CNH₂, are both possible products from a reaction in a research laboratory. The ¹³C NMR spectrum of the product shows two peaks.\n\nDeduce which amine was formed. Explain your reasoning.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Butan-1-amine has 4 carbon environments / would give 4 peaks ✓\n2-Methylpropan-2-amine has 2 carbon environments: the three equivalent CH₃ carbons and the C bonded to N ✓\nProduct is 2-methylpropan-2-amine ✓` },
      { q: `A manufacturer of biological washing powder measures the initial rate of breakdown of a protein stain by a protease at different temperatures. (data written for this practice question)\n\n| Temperature / °C | 20 | 30 | 40 | 50 | 60 | 70 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Relative rate | 1.2 | 2.3 | 4.1 | 5.0 | 1.8 | 0.2 |\n\nExplain the changes in rate shown by the data.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `From 20 to 50 °C rate increases because molecules have more kinetic energy so more frequent collisions / more collisions with energy ≥ activation enthalpy ✓\nOptimum (around 50 °C) ✓\nAbove 50 °C rate falls because hydrogen bonds/weak bonds in the tertiary structure break, the active site changes shape / enzyme denatures, so substrate no longer binds ✓` },
      { q: `Pepsin is a protease that works in the stomach at about pH 2. If it passes into the small intestine at about pH 8, it stops working.\n\nExplain why a change in pH can stop an enzyme working.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Change in pH changes the ionisation/charge of R groups, e.g. –COOH/–COO⁻ or –NH₂/–NH₃⁺ ✓\nIonic interactions / hydrogen bonds holding the tertiary structure are disrupted, changing the shape of the active site ✓\nSubstrate can no longer bind / the charged groups in the active site that interact with the substrate are changed ✓` },
      { q: `A short section of one strand of a DNA molecule has the base sequence:\n\nA T G G C T\n\nWrite the base sequence of the complementary section of the other strand. Calculate the total number of hydrogen bonds between the bases of these two sections, and explain why it is important that the strands are held together by hydrogen bonds rather than covalent bonds.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `T A C C G A ✓\n15 (3 A–T pairs × 2 + 3 G–C pairs × 3) ✓\nHydrogen bonds are weak so the strands can separate (unzip) for copying/replication or protein synthesis without breaking the covalent sugar–phosphate backbone ✓` },
      { q: `A food scientist hydrolyses a sample of egg protein and analyses the hydrolysate by thin-layer chromatography alongside standard amino acids. The plate is sprayed with ninhydrin and warmed. The hydrolysate gives three spots with Rf values 0.26, 0.37 and 0.61. (data written for this practice question)\n\n| Amino acid | Rf value |\n| --- | --- |\n| glycine | 0.26 |\n| alanine | 0.38 |\n| serine | 0.36 |\n| leucine | 0.61 |\n\nIdentify the amino acids that are definitely present and suggest how the scientist could confirm the identity of the spot at Rf 0.37.`, marks: 3, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Glycine and leucine ✓\nSpot at 0.37 could be alanine or serine (Rf values too close) ✓\nRun the plate again with a different solvent (and compare with standards) / use two-way chromatography / co-spot hydrolysate with each standard ✓` },
      { q: `Serine, HOCH₂CH(NH₂)COOH, is an amino acid found in silk protein.\n\nWrite the structural formulae of the form of serine present:\n• in the solid state\n• in a solution of pH 1\n• in a solution of pH 13.`, marks: 3, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Solid: HOCH₂CH(NH₃⁺)COO⁻ ✓\npH 1: HOCH₂CH(NH₃⁺)COOH ✓\npH 13: HOCH₂CH(NH₂)COO⁻ ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW the side-chain –OH ionised in any form` },
      { q: `Many drugs are chiral. Often only one optical isomer of a drug fits the active site of the enzyme it is designed to inhibit, and the other is inactive.\n\nExplain why only one optical isomer of a chiral drug is active.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Optical isomers are non-superimposable mirror images / have a different 3-D arrangement of groups around the chiral centre ✓\nThe active site (made of chiral amino acids) is itself chiral / has a specific 3-D shape ✓\nOnly one isomer has its groups positioned to bind/interact with the correct groups in the active site ✓` },
      { q: `Nylon-6,6, –[NH(CH₂)₆NH–CO(CH₂)₄CO]–, is used for carpets. A carpet manufacturer tests how nylon-6,6 fibres are affected by spills of hot acid and hot alkali.\n\nGive the structures of the organic products formed when nylon-6,6 is completely hydrolysed by:\n• hot aqueous hydrochloric acid\n• hot aqueous sodium hydroxide.`, marks: 4, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Hot acid:\nHOOC(CH₂)₄COOH ✓\n⁺H₃N(CH₂)₆NH₃⁺ ✓\nHot alkali:\n⁻OOC(CH₂)₄COO⁻ ✓\nH₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂ ✓\nALLOW sodium / chloride salts\nALLOW 1 mark for each pair if neutral acid and amine given in both cases (max 2)` },
      { q: `An enzyme used in the food industry is tested with and without a substance Q at two substrate concentrations. All other conditions are kept the same. (data written for this practice question)\n\n| [substrate] / mol dm⁻³ | Rate without Q / 10⁻⁶ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ | Rate with Q / 10⁻⁶ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0.010 | 2.0 | 0.8 |\n| 1.00 | 24 | 23 |\n\nDeduce how Q affects the enzyme. Explain your reasoning.`, marks: 4, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Q is a competitive inhibitor ✓\nAt low substrate concentration Q greatly reduces the rate (2.0 to 0.8) ✓\nAt high substrate concentration the rate is almost unchanged ✓\nQ has a similar shape to the substrate and binds (reversibly) to the active site; at high [substrate] substrate molecules outnumber Q and occupy most active sites ✓` },
      { q: `An ester, C₄H₈O₂, is formed when a polyester is broken down. It could be ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₂CH₃, or methyl propanoate, CH₃CH₂COOCH₃. Its ¹H NMR spectrum is summarised below. (data written for this practice question)\n\n| Chemical shift δ / ppm | Relative peak area | Splitting |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1.3 | 3 | triplet |\n| 2.0 | 3 | singlet |\n| 4.1 | 2 | quartet |\n\nDeduce which ester was formed. Explain your answer using the chemical shifts, the splitting and the peak areas.`, marks: 4, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Ethyl ethanoate ✓\nTriplet (3H) and quartet (2H) show a CH₃CH₂ group ✓\nQuartet at 4.1 shows the CH₂ is bonded to O (–OCH₂–), not to C=O (methyl propanoate CH₂ would be at about 2.3) ✓\nSinglet (3H) at 2.0 is CH₃ next to C=O with no adjacent H; methyl propanoate would have an OCH₃ singlet at about 3.7 ✓` },
      { q: `Haemoglobin is a protein made of four polypeptide chains. Each chain is partly folded into α-helices.\n\nDescribe the secondary and quaternary structure of haemoglobin, including the types of bond involved.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Secondary: coiling of the polypeptide chain into an α-helix (or folding into β-pleated sheets) ✓\nHeld by hydrogen bonds between C=O and N–H groups of peptide links in the backbone ✓\nQuaternary: association/arrangement of the four (separate) polypeptide chains ✓\nHeld by interactions between R groups on different chains, e.g. ionic, hydrogen bonds, disulfide bridges, instantaneous dipole–induced dipole bonds (any two) ✓` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of temperature on the rate at which amylase, an enzyme used in brewing, breaks down starch. Her method:\n• Mix 5 cm³ of starch solution with 1 cm³ of amylase solution in a test tube at room temperature.\n• Place the tube in a water bath at the chosen temperature.\n• Every 30 s, remove a drop and add it to iodine solution on a spotting tile. Record the time when the iodine no longer turns blue-black.\n• Repeat once at each of 20, 40, 60 and 80 °C.\n\nEvaluate the method and suggest improvements.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Any two weaknesses each with a matching improvement (2 × 2):\nSolutions mixed before reaching bath temperature ✓ → place starch and amylase in separate tubes in the bath for several minutes before mixing ✓\nSampling only every 30 s gives large uncertainty in time ✓ → sample more often, e.g. every 10 s / use a colorimeter to follow the reaction ✓\nColour end point is subjective ✓ → compare against a standard colour / use a colorimeter ✓\nOnly one result at each temperature ✓ → repeat (at least three times) and calculate a mean, discarding anomalies ✓\nTemperature intervals too wide to find the optimum ✓ → use smaller intervals, e.g. every 5 °C between 30 and 60 °C ✓\npH not controlled ✓ → add a buffer ✓` },
      { q: `A chemist is investigating ethanoyl chloride, CH₃COCl, as a reagent for making esters and amides for new polymers. She adds it, at room temperature, to separate samples of four reagents.\n\nGive the structural formula of the organic product formed with each reagent:\n• water\n• ethanol\n• ammonia\n• methylamine, CH₃NH₂.`, marks: 4, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Water: CH₃COOH ✓\nEthanol: CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ ✓\nAmmonia: CH₃CONH₂ ✓\nMethylamine: CH₃CONHCH₃ ✓\nIGNORE HCl / ammonium salts as by-products` },
      { q: `DNA is a polymer of nucleotides. Each nucleotide is made from a phosphate group, the sugar deoxyribose and one of four bases.\n\nDescribe how nucleotides join to form a single strand of DNA and how the bases are arranged in the strand.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Base is attached to the sugar (deoxyribose) in each nucleotide ✓\nNucleotides join by condensation reactions, eliminating water ✓\nBetween the phosphate group of one nucleotide and an –OH group on the sugar of the next (forming a phosphodiester / ester link) ✓\nGiving an alternating sugar–phosphate backbone with the bases sticking out from the sugars (sequence of bases carries the code) ✓` },
      { q: `A tripeptide is formed from one molecule each of glycine, alanine and serine.\n\n| Amino acid | Mr |\n| --- | --- |\n| glycine | 75.1 |\n| alanine | 89.1 |\n| serine | 105.1 |\n\nCalculate the Mr of the tripeptide (Mr of water = 18.0), state how many different tripeptides could be made using one molecule of each amino acid, and state the reagent and conditions used to hydrolyse the tripeptide back to its amino acids.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Sum = 269.3 and two water molecules removed ✓\nMr = 269.3 − 36.0 = 233.3 ✓\n6 ✓\n(6 mol dm⁻³) HCl(aq), heat under reflux (for several hours) ✓\nALLOW protease enzyme at 37 °C\nCorrect answer of 233.3 with no working scores first 2 marks` },
      { q: `Lactic acid, CH₃CH(OH)COOH, is the monomer for the biodegradable polymer PLA. A sample of lactic acid is dissolved in D₂O and its ¹H NMR spectrum is recorded. Only two peaks are seen. (data written for this practice question)\n\n| Chemical shift δ / ppm | Relative peak area |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1.4 | 3 |\n| 4.3 | 1 |\n\nPredict the splitting of each peak, explaining your reasoning. Explain why only two peaks are seen. Predict the number of peaks in the ¹³C NMR spectrum of lactic acid.`, marks: 5, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `Peak at 1.4 (CH₃) is a doublet ✓\nbecause it is next to one H (CH): 1 + 1 = 2 ✓\nPeak at 4.3 (CH) is a quartet because it is next to CH₃: 3 + 1 = 4 ✓\nThe OH and COOH protons exchange with deuterium in D₂O, so give no peak ✓\n3 peaks (¹³C) ✓` },
      { q: `A biotechnology company measures the initial rate of an enzyme-catalysed hydrolysis at different substrate concentrations, using the same enzyme concentration each time. (data written for this practice question)\n\n| [S] / mol dm⁻³ | 0.005 | 0.010 | 0.020 | 0.50 | 1.00 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Initial rate / mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ | 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ | 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ | 4.0 × 10⁻⁶ | 2.4 × 10⁻⁵ | 2.4 × 10⁻⁵ |\n\nDeduce the order of reaction with respect to S at low and at high concentrations. Calculate the rate constant at low [S], giving units. Explain why the order changes.`, marks: 5, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Low [S]: first order – doubling [S] doubles rate ✓\nk = rate ÷ [S] = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 0.005 = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ ✓\nUnits s⁻¹ ✓\nHigh [S]: zero order – rate does not change when [S] doubles ✓\nAt high [S] all active sites are occupied / enzyme is saturated, so adding more substrate cannot increase rate ✓` },
      { q: `When DNA is heated, the two strands separate. The temperature at which half the DNA has separated is its melting temperature. DNA samples with different percentages of G–C base pairs were tested. (data written for this practice question)\n\n| % G–C base pairs | 30 | 40 | 50 | 60 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Melting temperature / °C | 81.6 | 85.7 | 89.8 | 93.9 |\n\nDescribe the trend, estimate the melting temperature of DNA with 45% G–C base pairs, and explain the trend in terms of the bonding in DNA.`, marks: 5, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Melting temperature increases as % G–C increases (by about 4 °C per 10%) ✓\nEstimate 87.8 °C ✓ ALLOW 87–89 °C\nG–C pairs form three hydrogen bonds but A–T pairs form only two ✓\nMore hydrogen bonds between the strands so more energy needed to separate them ✓\nOnly hydrogen bonds between bases are broken on melting; the covalent sugar–phosphate backbone is not broken ✓` },
      { q: `Proteases are added to biological washing powders to remove protein stains such as blood and egg. Manufacturers recommend a wash at 40 °C and the powder contains a buffer to keep the wash water at about pH 9.\n\nExplain how a protease catalyses the breakdown of a protein stain, and why the temperature and pH of the wash must be controlled. (6*)`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1 – How the enzyme works\n• protease catalyses hydrolysis of peptide/amide bonds\n• substrate binds to the active site, forming enzyme–substrate complex\n• held by intermolecular bonds (H bonds, ionic) with R groups in active site\n• lock-and-key or induced fit; bonds in substrate weakened / strained\n• alternative route with lower activation enthalpy; products released, enzyme unchanged\nStrand 2 – Temperature\n• rate increases with temperature up to optimum: more kinetic energy, more successful collisions\n• above optimum, H bonds and other weak bonds in tertiary structure break\n• active site changes shape, substrate no longer fits – denaturation (irreversible)\n• 40 °C gives a good rate without denaturing; saves energy\nStrand 3 – pH\n• changes in pH alter ionisation of –COOH/–NH₂ R groups\n• ionic interactions / H bonds holding tertiary structure disrupted\n• charges in active site needed to bind substrate changed\n• buffer keeps pH near the enzyme's optimum\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `Enzymes from bacteria that live in hot springs keep working at 80 °C, while the same enzyme from human cells denatures at about 50 °C. Analysis shows the hot-spring enzyme has more cysteine residues (side chain –CH₂SH) and more pairs of lysine (–(CH₂)₄NH₂) and aspartic acid (–CH₂COOH) residues close together in the folded chain.\n\nDescribe the levels of protein structure in an enzyme and the bonds involved at each level, and explain why the hot-spring enzyme is more stable to heat. (6*)`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1 – Primary and secondary structure\n• primary: sequence of amino acids joined by peptide (covalent) bonds\n• formed by condensation reactions\n• secondary: α-helix / β-pleated sheet\n• held by H bonds between C=O and N–H of backbone peptide groups\nStrand 2 – Tertiary (and quaternary) structure\n• overall 3-D folding of the chain, which gives the shape of the active site\n• held by interactions between R groups: disulfide bridges, ionic interactions, H bonds, instantaneous dipole–induced dipole bonds\n• quaternary: association of more than one chain, same types of interaction\nStrand 3 – Heat stability\n• more cysteine allows more disulfide bridges (S–S), which are covalent and need much more energy to break\n• lysine –NH₃⁺ and aspartate –COO⁻ form ionic interactions, stronger than H bonds\n• more/stronger bonds hold the tertiary structure, so active site keeps its shape at higher temperature\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `Three condensation polymers are produced on a large scale:\n• nylon-6,6, from hexane-1,6-diamine and hexanedioic acid, used for clothing and carpets\n• Kevlar, from benzene-1,4-diamine and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid, used in body armour\n• PET, a polyester from benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid and ethane-1,2-diol, used for drinks bottles.\n\nCompare how these polymers are formed, explain the differences in their strength in terms of intermolecular bonds, and explain how they can be broken down by hydrolysis. (6*)`, marks: 6, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1 – Formation\n• all are condensation polymers: water eliminated (or HCl if diacyl dichloride used)\n• nylon-6,6 and Kevlar: amine + carboxylic acid → amide (peptide) link –CONH–\n• PET: alcohol + carboxylic acid → ester link –COO–\n• each monomer has two functional groups\nStrand 2 – Strength / intermolecular bonds\n• nylon and Kevlar: hydrogen bonds between C=O and N–H on neighbouring chains\n• Kevlar: rigid, straight chains (benzene rings) align closely, so many regular H bonds – very strong\n• nylon chains more flexible, fewer aligned H bonds\n• PET: no N–H so no H bonds between chains; permanent dipole–permanent dipole and instantaneous dipole–induced dipole bonds only\nStrand 3 – Hydrolysis\n• amide and ester links can be hydrolysed by hot acid or alkali\n• acid hydrolysis of polyamide gives dicarboxylic acid and diammonium salt\n• alkaline hydrolysis gives carboxylate salt and diamine/diol\n• PET hydrolysis with NaOH gives benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate and ethane-1,2-diol (recycling)\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A sports-nutrition company wants to check which amino acids are present in a new protein supplement. A technician has: the supplement, 6 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid, reflux apparatus, silica TLC plates, a suitable solvent, solutions of standard amino acids and ninhydrin spray.\n\nPlan an experiment to identify the amino acids in the supplement. Include safety precautions and how the results would be analysed. (6*)`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1 – Hydrolysis\n• heat supplement with 6 mol dm⁻³ HCl under reflux for several hours\n• breaks peptide bonds to release amino acids\n• neutralise / evaporate excess acid before chromatography\nStrand 2 – Chromatography\n• pencil baseline about 1 cm from bottom of plate\n• small concentrated spots of hydrolysate and of each standard amino acid on baseline\n• solvent depth below baseline; cover the tank with a lid to saturate with vapour\n• remove when solvent near top; mark solvent front; dry\n• spray with ninhydrin and warm to show (purple) spots\n• safety: HCl corrosive – goggles/gloves; solvent flammable/toxic and ninhydrin harmful – fume cupboard; avoid skin contact\nStrand 3 – Analysis\n• measure distance moved by each spot and solvent front\n• Rf = distance moved by spot ÷ distance moved by solvent front\n• compare Rf values (and positions) with the standards run on the same plate\n• if two amino acids have similar Rf, use a different solvent / two-way chromatography\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'O': {
    name: 'Oceans',
    questions: [
      { q: `Seawater contains dissolved calcium chloride. The energy changes when calcium chloride dissolves can be described using its lattice enthalpy.\n\nWrite an equation, including state symbols, for the change that has an enthalpy change equal to the lattice enthalpy of calcium chloride.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Ca²⁺(g) + 2Cl⁻(g) → CaCl₂(s) ✓\nALLOW multiples\nDO NOT ALLOW the reverse equation (lattice enthalpy refers to formation of the lattice from gaseous ions)\nState symbols required` },
      { q: `Hydrogencarbonate ions, HCO₃⁻, are the most abundant form of dissolved inorganic carbon in seawater. They can donate a proton to water.\n\nIdentify the conjugate base of HCO₃⁻.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `CO₃²⁻ / carbonate ion ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW H₂CO₃ or CO₂` },
      { q: `A student models acid rain falling on the ocean surface using a 0.0250 mol dm⁻³ solution of hydrochloric acid.\n\nCalculate the pH of this solution. Give your answer to 2 decimal places.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `pH = −log 0.0250 = 1.60 ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 1.6` },
      { q: `The pH of seawater depends on the ionisation of water, H₂O(l) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq), as well as on dissolved carbon dioxide.\n\nWrite an expression for the ionic product of water, Kw.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Kw = [H⁺(aq)][OH⁻(aq)] ✓\nALLOW [H₃O⁺][OH⁻]\nDO NOT ALLOW expressions including [H₂O]` },
      { q: `Surface seawater has a pH of about 8.1. At this pH the main species of dissolved inorganic carbon are HCO₃⁻ and CO₃²⁻, and seawater acts as a buffer.\n\nIdentify the conjugate acid–base pair that is mainly responsible for buffering seawater at pH 8.1.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `HCO₃⁻ (acid) and CO₃²⁻ (base) ✓\nALLOW H₂CO₃/HCO₃⁻ or CO₂/HCO₃⁻ ONLY if HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻ also given\nIGNORE references to borate` },
      { q: `An oceanographer records that the pH of a sample of surface seawater is 8.10.\n\nCalculate the concentration of H⁺(aq) ions in the sample.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[H⁺] = 10⁻⁸·¹⁰ = 7.94 × 10⁻⁹ mol dm⁻³ ✓\nALLOW 7.9 × 10⁻⁹ to 7.943 × 10⁻⁹\nIGNORE units` },
      { q: `Carbon dioxide in the troposphere absorbs some of the infrared radiation emitted by the Earth's surface, including the oceans.\n\nState what happens to a CO₂ molecule when it absorbs infrared radiation.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `the bonds vibrate more / the molecule gains vibrational energy ✓\nALLOW bonds bend or stretch more\nDO NOT ALLOW bonds break / electrons are excited` },
      { q: `One method of carbon capture and storage is to react captured carbon dioxide with metal oxides, such as magnesium oxide, to form a solid carbonate that can be stored.\n\nWrite an equation for the reaction of magnesium oxide with carbon dioxide.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `MgO + CO₂ → MgCO₃ ✓\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `The chloride ion concentration in a sample of seawater is found by adding aqueous silver nitrate. A white precipitate forms.\n\nWrite the ionic equation, including state symbols, for the formation of the precipitate.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s) ✓\nState symbols required\nDO NOT ALLOW full equation including NO₃⁻ or Na⁺` },
      { q: `A student measures the enthalpy change of solution of potassium chloride. She dissolves the solid in water in a polystyrene cup and records the lowest temperature reached. She places a lid on the cup.\n\nSuggest why the lid improves the accuracy of the result.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `reduces heat transfer (gain) from the surroundings / reduces heat exchange with the air ✓\nALLOW reduces heat loss to the surroundings\nIGNORE prevents evaporation unless linked to energy` },
      { q: `Carbon dioxide dissolved in the ocean reacts with water to form hydrogen ions:\nCO₂(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + HCO₃⁻(aq)\n\nWrite the expression for the acid dissociation constant, Ka, for this equilibrium and give its units.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Ka = [H⁺(aq)][HCO₃⁻(aq)] / [CO₂(aq)] ✓\nALLOW [H₂CO₃] in the denominator\nDO NOT ALLOW [H₂O] in the expression\nunits: mol dm⁻³ ✓` },
      { q: `Sodium hydroxide solution is used to remove carbon dioxide from the air in some carbon capture trials. A technician prepares a 0.0150 mol dm⁻³ solution of NaOH at 25 °C.\nKw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶ at 25 °C.\n\nCalculate the pH of this solution. Give your answer to 2 decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[H⁺] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 0.0150 = 6.67 × 10⁻¹³ (mol dm⁻³) ✓\npH = 12.18 ✓\nALLOW pOH = 1.82 so pH = 12.18\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `Limewater, a saturated solution of calcium hydroxide, is used to test for carbon dioxide. A sample of limewater has a Ca(OH)₂ concentration of 0.0200 mol dm⁻³.\nKw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶.\n\nCalculate the pH of this sample. Give your answer to 2 decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[OH⁻] = 2 × 0.0200 = 0.0400 mol dm⁻³ ✓\npH = −log(1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 0.0400) = 12.60 ✓\nECF from [OH⁻] = 0.0200 gives pH = 12.30 for 1 mark` },
      { q: `In seawater, hydrogencarbonate ions react with water:\nHCO₃⁻(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ CO₃²⁻(aq) + H₃O⁺(aq)\n\nIdentify the two conjugate acid–base pairs in this equilibrium. In each pair, state which species is the acid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `HCO₃⁻ (acid) and CO₃²⁻ (base) ✓\nH₃O⁺ (acid) and H₂O (base) ✓\nBoth species and correct acid labelling needed for each mark` },
      { q: `The hydrogencarbonate ion can act both as a Brønsted–Lowry acid and as a Brønsted–Lowry base in seawater.\n\nWrite two equations to show HCO₃⁻ acting as an acid in one reaction and as a base in the other.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `as an acid: HCO₃⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CO₃²⁻ + H₃O⁺ ✓\nALLOW HCO₃⁻ + OH⁻ → CO₃²⁻ + H₂O / HCO₃⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + CO₃²⁻\nas a base: HCO₃⁻ + H₃O⁺ → H₂CO₃ + H₂O ✓\nALLOW HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O / HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂CO₃\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `Deep ocean water is at about 10 °C. At this temperature Kw = 2.9 × 10⁻¹⁵ mol² dm⁻⁶.\n\nCalculate the pH of pure water at 10 °C and state whether the water is acidic, neutral or alkaline.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[H⁺] = √(2.9 × 10⁻¹⁵) = 5.4 × 10⁻⁸ so pH = 7.27 ✓\nALLOW 7.3\nneutral because [H⁺] = [OH⁻] ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW neutral because pH is 7 / alkaline` },
      { q: `Some of the carbon dioxide in the atmosphere dissolves in the oceans:\nCO₂(g) → CO₂(aq)\n\nPredict the sign of the entropy change of the system, ΔS_sys, for this change. Explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `ΔS_sys is negative ✓\ngas molecules become dissolved so fewer ways of arranging the molecules / energy (aqueous is more ordered than gas) ✓\nALLOW the CO₂ goes from a gas to a solution so is less disordered\nSecond mark depends on the first` },
      { q: `Ammonium nitrate dissolves in water endothermically. The enthalpy change of solution of ammonium nitrate is +25.7 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the entropy change of the surroundings, ΔS_surr, when 1 mole of ammonium nitrate dissolves at 298 K. Give your answer in J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔS_surr = −ΔH/T = −25 700 / 298 ✓\n= −86.2 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW −86 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\nSign required for 2nd mark; −0.0862 (kJ units) scores 1 mark\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `The oceans store huge amounts of energy and so moderate the Earth's climate. Water has an unusually high specific heat capacity of 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ compared with other liquids made of molecules of similar size.\n\nExplain, in terms of intermolecular bonds, why water has a high specific heat capacity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `water molecules are held together by hydrogen bonds (as well as other intermolecular bonds) ✓\nenergy is needed to break / overcome hydrogen bonds as temperature rises (so more energy is needed per gram per kelvin) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW breaking of covalent bonds / O–H bonds` },
      { q: `A sample of seawater is tested for sulfate ions by adding acidified barium chloride solution.\n\nWrite the ionic equation, including state symbols, for the reaction and state what is observed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s) ✓\nState symbols required\nwhite precipitate ✓` },
      { q: `A student dissolves potassium chloride in water and measures the temperature change with a thermometer that can be read to ±0.5 °C. The initial temperature is 21.3 °C and the final temperature is 16.2 °C.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the temperature change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `uncertainty in ΔT = 2 × 0.5 = ±1.0 °C ✓\npercentage uncertainty = 1.0 / 5.1 × 100 = 19.6% ✓\nALLOW 20%\nECF using ±0.5 °C gives 9.8% for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour all contribute to the greenhouse effect, but their contributions are very different.\n\nState two factors that determine how much a gas contributes to the greenhouse effect.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `Any two from:\nconcentration (abundance) of the gas in the atmosphere ✓\nhow effectively each molecule absorbs infrared radiation / range of IR frequencies absorbed ✓\nlifetime (residence time) of the gas in the atmosphere ✓\nIGNORE ozone depletion` },
      { q: `The oceans act as a carbon sink. The first step is the equilibrium:\nCO₂(g) ⇌ CO₂(aq)\n\nExplain why an increase in the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere increases the concentration of dissolved carbon dioxide in the oceans.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `increase in [CO₂(g)] / partial pressure of CO₂ ✓\nposition of equilibrium shifts to the right (to oppose the change) so more CO₂ dissolves ✓\nALLOW the rate of dissolving increases (more collisions with the surface) until a new equilibrium is reached` },
      { q: `Magnesium chloride is one of the main salts in seawater. Some enthalpy data are given below (data written for this practice question).\nLattice enthalpy of MgCl₂ = −2526 kJ mol⁻¹\nEnthalpy change of hydration of Mg²⁺ = −1920 kJ mol⁻¹\nEnthalpy change of hydration of Cl⁻ = −364 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of solution of magnesium chloride.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔsolH = −(lattice enthalpy) + Σ(ΔhydH) ✓\nΣΔhydH = −1920 + 2(−364) = −2648 (kJ mol⁻¹) ✓\nΔsolH = +2526 − 2648 = −122 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nSign required\nECF −1920 − 364 used once gives +242 for 2 marks\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `The enthalpy change of hydration of Mg²⁺ is −1920 kJ mol⁻¹ and that of Ca²⁺ is −1650 kJ mol⁻¹ (data written for this practice question).\n\nExplain why the enthalpy change of hydration of Mg²⁺ is more exothermic than that of Ca²⁺.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Mg²⁺ is smaller (than Ca²⁺) / has a smaller ionic radius ✓\nMg²⁺ has a higher charge density ✓\nstronger attraction to the δ− oxygen atoms of water molecules (so more energy released when ion–dipole bonds form) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW Mg²⁺ has a higher charge\nDO NOT ALLOW reference to atoms instead of ions for the first mark` },
      { q: `Both sodium fluoride and magnesium oxide contain ions with the electron configuration of neon. Their lattice enthalpies are given below (data written for this practice question).\nNaF: −918 kJ mol⁻¹\nMgO: −3791 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nExplain why the lattice enthalpy of magnesium oxide is much more exothermic than that of sodium fluoride.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ have greater charges than Na⁺ and F⁻ (2+/2− compared with 1+/1−) ✓\nthe ions in MgO are also smaller / Mg²⁺ is smaller than Na⁺ (so the ions are closer together) ✓\nstronger electrostatic attraction between the ions in MgO (so more energy released when the lattice forms) ✓\nALLOW higher charge density for the first two marks if both charge and size are covered\nDO NOT ALLOW reference to intermolecular forces or covalent bonds` },
      { q: `Rainwater is naturally acidic because it contains dissolved carbon dioxide. In unpolluted rainwater, [CO₂(aq)] = 1.4 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³.\nCO₂(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + HCO₃⁻(aq)   Ka = 4.5 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³\n\nCalculate the pH of unpolluted rainwater. Give your answer to 2 decimal places.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[H⁺]² = Ka × [CO₂(aq)] = 4.5 × 10⁻⁷ × 1.4 × 10⁻⁵ = 6.3 × 10⁻¹² ✓\n[H⁺] = 2.51 × 10⁻⁶ mol dm⁻³ ✓\npH = 5.60 ✓\nALLOW [H⁺] = [HCO₃⁻] assumption stated or implied\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student uses a calibrated pH meter to find Ka for ethanoic acid, a weak acid used as a model for acids in seawater experiments. A 0.100 mol dm⁻³ solution of ethanoic acid has a pH of 2.88.\n\nCalculate Ka for ethanoic acid. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[H⁺] = 10⁻²·⁸⁸ = 1.32 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ ✓\nKa = [H⁺]² / [CH₃COOH] = (1.32 × 10⁻³)² / 0.100 ✓\n= 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ ✓\nALLOW 1.76 × 10⁻⁵ if [HA] = 0.100 − 1.32 × 10⁻³ is used\nIGNORE units` },
      { q: `Limestone and shells are mostly calcium carbonate. When heated strongly they decompose:\nCaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)\nStandard entropies, S / J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹: CaCO₃(s) 92.9; CaO(s) 39.7; CO₂(g) 213.6\n\nCalculate the entropy change of the system, ΔS_sys, for this reaction, and explain why its sign is as expected.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔS_sys = (39.7 + 213.6) − 92.9 ✓\n= +160.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ ✓\npositive because a gas is produced from a solid / number of moles of gas increases so more ways of arranging the particles and energy ✓\nSign required for 2nd mark` },
      { q: `The dissolving of carbon dioxide in seawater is exothermic:\nCO₂(g) → CO₂(aq)   ΔH = −19.4 kJ mol⁻¹\nAs the oceans warm, they absorb carbon dioxide less well.\n\nExplain, in terms of entropy, why carbon dioxide is less soluble in warmer water.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ΔS_sys is negative (gas becomes dissolved) ✓\nΔS_surr = −ΔH/T is positive, but becomes smaller (less positive) as T increases ✓\nso ΔS_total (= ΔS_sys + ΔS_surr) becomes less positive / more negative, so dissolving is less feasible / equilibrium lies further to the left ✓\nALLOW equilibrium shifts in the endothermic direction (to the left) for the 3rd mark only if linked to entropy` },
      { q: `Seawater is a buffer that contains hydrogencarbonate ions and carbonate ions:\nHCO₃⁻(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + CO₃²⁻(aq)\n\nExplain how this buffer resists a change in pH when a small amount of acid is added.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `added H⁺ reacts with CO₃²⁻ / equilibrium shifts to the left ✓\nlarge reservoir of CO₃²⁻ and HCO₃⁻ so the ratio [HCO₃⁻]/[CO₃²⁻] changes only slightly ✓\nso [H⁺] (= Ka[HCO₃⁻]/[CO₃²⁻]) and pH change very little ✓\nALLOW H⁺ + CO₃²⁻ → HCO₃⁻` },
      { q: `Coral reefs are built by organisms that make skeletons of calcium carbonate. As the oceans absorb more carbon dioxide, these skeletons are harder to make and existing structures can start to dissolve.\n\nExplain, using equations, why an increase in dissolved carbon dioxide reduces the concentration of carbonate ions in seawater.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `CO₂(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + HCO₃⁻(aq) ✓\nthe H⁺ ions produced react with carbonate ions: H⁺ + CO₃²⁻ → HCO₃⁻ ✓\nso [CO₃²⁻] decreases (and CaCO₃(s) ⇌ Ca²⁺ + CO₃²⁻ shifts to the right) ✓\nALLOW overall equation CO₂ + H₂O + CO₃²⁻ → 2HCO₃⁻ for the first 2 marks` },
      { q: `Calcium carbonate precipitates when the ion product [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻] exceeds its solubility product, Ksp. In a sample of tropical surface seawater (data written for this practice question):\n[Ca²⁺] = 0.0103 mol dm⁻³\n[CO₃²⁻] = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³\nKsp of calcite in seawater = 4.3 × 10⁻⁷ mol² dm⁻⁶\n\nDeduce whether existing calcite shells in this water will dissolve. Show your working.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `ion product = 0.0103 × 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.06 × 10⁻⁶ (mol² dm⁻⁶) ✓\nthis is greater than Ksp / about 5 times Ksp (so the water is supersaturated) ✓\nso shells will not dissolve (calcite can still precipitate) ✓\nThird mark depends on a comparison with Ksp\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect ion product` },
      { q: `The upper 1.00 m³ of a patch of ocean surface warms by 0.10 K during a sunny morning. Seawater has a density of 1.03 g cm⁻³ and a specific heat capacity of 3.99 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the energy absorbed by this volume of seawater. Give your answer in kJ to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mass = 1.00 × 10⁶ cm³ × 1.03 = 1.03 × 10⁶ g ✓\nq = mcΔT = 1.03 × 10⁶ × 3.99 × 0.10 = 4.11 × 10⁵ J ✓\n= 411 kJ ✓\nECF from incorrect mass (e.g. 1.03 × 10³ g gives 0.411 kJ for 2 marks)\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student determines the enthalpy change of solution of potassium chloride, KCl (Mr = 74.6).\n• 50.0 cm³ of water is placed in a polystyrene cup. Initial temperature = 21.3 °C.\n• 5.00 g of KCl is added and the mixture is stirred. Lowest temperature = 16.2 °C.\nAssume the specific heat capacity of the solution is 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ and its mass is 50.0 g.\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of solution of potassium chloride. Give your answer in kJ mol⁻¹ to 3 significant figures and with a sign.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `q = 50.0 × 4.18 × 5.1 = 1066 J ✓\nn(KCl) = 5.00 / 74.6 = 0.0670 mol ✓\nΔsolH = 1.066 / 0.0670 = 15.9 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\npositive sign / endothermic, as the temperature fell: +15.9 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW use of 55.0 g giving +17.5 kJ mol⁻¹ for 3 marks\nECF throughout` },
      { q: `Calcium ions in seawater are hydrated. Data for calcium chloride (data written for this practice question):\nLattice enthalpy of CaCl₂ = −2258 kJ mol⁻¹\nEnthalpy change of solution of CaCl₂ = −83 kJ mol⁻¹\nEnthalpy change of hydration of Cl⁻ = −364 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nDraw an enthalpy cycle linking these quantities and use it to calculate the enthalpy change of hydration of Ca²⁺.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `cycle with CaCl₂(s), Ca²⁺(g) + 2Cl⁻(g) and Ca²⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq) correctly linked with arrows labelled lattice enthalpy (gaseous ions → solid), ΔsolH (solid → aqueous) and ΔhydH (gaseous ions → aqueous) ✓\nΔhydH(Ca²⁺) + 2ΔhydH(Cl⁻) = lattice enthalpy + ΔsolH ✓\n= −2258 + (−83) = −2341 (kJ mol⁻¹) ✓\nΔhydH(Ca²⁺) = −2341 + 728 = −1613 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nECF from use of 1 × Cl⁻ gives −1977 for 3 marks\nState symbols required on cycle` },
      { q: `A technician needs a buffer of pH 5.00 to calibrate equipment for analysing seawater samples. She adds solid sodium ethanoate, CH₃COONa (Mr = 82.0), to 250 cm³ of 0.200 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid.\nKa (ethanoic acid) = 1.7 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³. Assume the volume does not change.\n\nCalculate the mass of sodium ethanoate she must add. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[H⁺] = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ ✓\n[CH₃COO⁻] = Ka[CH₃COOH]/[H⁺] = 1.7 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.200 / 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ = 0.340 mol dm⁻³ ✓\nmoles in 250 cm³ = 0.340 × 0.250 = 0.0850 mol ✓\nmass = 0.0850 × 82.0 = 6.97 g ✓\nECF throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Calcium carbonate from shells can be heated to make calcium oxide, which is being trialled as a material for carbon capture:\nCaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)   ΔH = +178 kJ mol⁻¹   ΔS_sys = +160.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\n\nUse ΔS_total = ΔS_sys − ΔH/T to show that the reaction is not feasible at 298 K, and calculate the minimum temperature at which it becomes feasible.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔS_surr = −178 000 / 298 = −597 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ ✓\nΔS_total = 160.4 − 597 = −437 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹; negative so not feasible ✓\nfeasible when ΔS_total ≥ 0, so T = ΔH / ΔS_sys = 178 000 / 160.4 ✓\n= 1110 K ✓\nALLOW 1109.7 K / 837 °C\nDO NOT ALLOW use of 178 with 160.4 without unit conversion (gives 1.11 K) for the final mark` },
      { q: `In seawater the following equilibrium sets the concentration of carbonate ions:\nHCO₃⁻(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + CO₃²⁻(aq)   Ka = 1.1 × 10⁻⁹ mol dm⁻³ in seawater\nToday's surface seawater has pH 8.00. Some models predict a pH of 7.80 by 2100.\n\nCalculate the ratio [CO₃²⁻]/[HCO₃⁻] at pH 8.00 and at pH 7.80, and use your answers to comment on the effect on shell-forming organisms.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[CO₃²⁻]/[HCO₃⁻] = Ka / [H⁺] ✓\nat pH 8.00: 1.1 × 10⁻⁹ / 1.0 × 10⁻⁸ = 0.11 ✓\nat pH 7.80: 1.1 × 10⁻⁹ / 1.58 × 10⁻⁸ = 0.069 ✓\ncarbonate ion proportion falls (by about 37%), so less CO₃²⁻ available to make CaCO₃ shells / shells more likely to dissolve ✓\nALLOW 0.0694` },
      { q: `When calcium carbonate precipitates from seawater, the entropy of the system increases even though a solid forms.\nCa²⁺(aq) + CO₃²⁻(aq) → CaCO₃(s)   ΔH = +12.8 kJ mol⁻¹\nStandard entropies, S / J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹: Ca²⁺(aq) −53.1; CO₃²⁻(aq) −56.9; CaCO₃(s) 92.9\n\nCalculate ΔS_total for the precipitation at 298 K and suggest why ΔS_sys is positive.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔS_sys = 92.9 − (−53.1 + −56.9) = +202.9 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ ✓\nΔS_surr = −12 800 / 298 = −43.0 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ ✓\nΔS_total = +160 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ (so precipitation is feasible) ✓\nsuggestion: the small, highly charged ions hold water molecules in ordered hydration shells; these water molecules are released when the solid forms, increasing disorder ✓\nECF throughout` },
      { q: `Scientists are concerned that increasing concentrations of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere are causing ocean surface temperatures to rise.\n\nExplain how carbon dioxide in the troposphere causes the Earth's surface to warm.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the Sun's radiation (mainly visible/UV) passes through the atmosphere and warms the Earth's surface ✓\nthe warm surface emits infrared radiation ✓\nCO₂ molecules absorb some of this IR, so their bonds vibrate more (increased vibrational energy) ✓\nenergy is transferred to other molecules (by collisions) / IR is re-emitted in all directions including back towards the surface, so the troposphere and surface warm ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW references to the ozone layer / UV being trapped` },
      { q: `A student models the buffering of seawater in the laboratory. She makes 100 cm³ of a buffer containing 0.100 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid and 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium ethanoate. Ka (ethanoic acid) = 1.7 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³.\nShe then adds 5.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid.\n\nCalculate the pH of the buffer before and after the hydrochloric acid is added, and compare the change with the pH of 5.0 cm³ of the same hydrochloric acid added to 100 cm³ of pure water. Give pH values to 2 decimal places.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `before: [H⁺] = Ka (as [HA] = [A⁻]) so pH = pKa = 4.77 ✓\nmoles H⁺ added = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴; n(CH₃COOH) = 0.0105 and n(CH₃COO⁻) = 0.0095 mol ✓\n[H⁺] = 1.7 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.0105/0.0095 = 1.88 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ ✓\npH after = 4.73 (change of only 0.04) ✓\nin water: [H⁺] = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ / 0.105 = 4.76 × 10⁻³ so pH = 2.32, a fall of about 4.7 units (from 7) ✓\nALLOW pH 2.30 if volume taken as 100 cm³\nECF throughout` },
      { q: `A student finds the percentage by mass of calcium carbonate in a crushed seashell by back titration.\n• 0.500 g of shell is added to 50.0 cm³ of 0.200 mol dm⁻³ HCl (an excess).\n• When the reaction stops, the remaining HCl is titrated with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ NaOH. Mean titre = 22.40 cm³.\nCaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂   Mr(CaCO₃) = 100.1\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of calcium carbonate in the shell. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(HCl) at start = 0.0500 × 0.200 = 0.0100 mol ✓\nn(HCl) left = n(NaOH) = 0.02240 × 0.100 = 2.24 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nn(HCl) reacted with CaCO₃ = 7.76 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nn(CaCO₃) = 3.88 × 10⁻³ mol, mass = 0.388 g ✓\npercentage = 0.388 / 0.500 × 100 = 77.7% ✓\nECF throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Ammonium nitrate dissolves readily in water even though the process is endothermic, so the solution cools.\nNH₄NO₃(s) → NH₄⁺(aq) + NO₃⁻(aq)   ΔsolH = +25.7 kJ mol⁻¹\nStandard entropies, S / J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹: NH₄NO₃(s) 151.1; NH₄⁺(aq) 113.4; NO₃⁻(aq) 146.4\n\nCalculate ΔS_total for dissolving ammonium nitrate at 298 K. Use your answer to explain why ammonium nitrate dissolves even though the process is endothermic.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔS_sys = (113.4 + 146.4) − 151.1 = +108.7 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ ✓\nΔS_surr = −25 700 / 298 = −86.2 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ ✓\nΔS_total = 108.7 − 86.2 = +22.5 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ ✓\nΔS_total is positive so the process is feasible / spontaneous ✓\nthe increase in entropy of the system (ions spread through the solution) outweighs the decrease in entropy of the surroundings caused by the endothermic change ✓\nECF throughout` },
      { q: `Sodium chloride and magnesium chloride are both soluble salts found in seawater, but their enthalpy changes of solution differ (data written for this practice question):\n\nSalt | Lattice enthalpy / kJ mol⁻¹ | Sum of hydration enthalpies of ions / kJ mol⁻¹ | ΔsolH / kJ mol⁻¹\nNaCl | −787 | −770 | +17\nMgCl₂ | −2526 | −2648 | −122\n\nExplain the values in the table in terms of the sizes and charges of the ions, and explain why both salts dissolve in water even though their enthalpy changes of solution have different signs.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1: Enthalpy cycle\n• ΔsolH = −(lattice enthalpy) + sum of hydration enthalpies\n• NaCl: +787 − 770 = +17 (endothermic); MgCl₂: +2526 − 2648 = −122 (exothermic)\n• energy needed to separate the ions is balanced against energy released when ions are hydrated\nStrand 2: Effect of charge and size\n• Mg²⁺ has a greater charge and is smaller than Na⁺ (higher charge density)\n• so stronger attraction between ions in the lattice (more exothermic lattice enthalpy)\n• and stronger attraction to δ− O of water molecules (more exothermic hydration enthalpy)\n• MgCl₂ has two Cl⁻ ions to hydrate per formula unit\nStrand 3: Entropy and feasibility\n• dissolving is feasible if ΔS_total = ΔS_sys − ΔH/T is positive\n• ΔS_sys is usually positive as ions spread out through the solution\n• for NaCl, ΔH is small and positive so ΔS_surr is small and negative; ΔS_sys outweighs it\n• for MgCl₂, ΔS_surr is positive, adding to ΔS_total\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `The oceans have absorbed roughly a quarter of the carbon dioxide released by human activity. This has reduced the pH of surface seawater from about 8.2 to about 8.1, and there is concern about the effect on coral reefs and shelled organisms.\n\nExplain, using equations, how the absorption of carbon dioxide lowers the pH of seawater, why the change in pH is small, and how the change affects calcium carbonate shells and reefs.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1: Lowering pH\n• CO₂(g) ⇌ CO₂(aq); more atmospheric CO₂ shifts equilibrium to the right\n• CO₂(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + HCO₃⁻(aq)\n• [H⁺] increases so pH falls; a fall of 0.1 is a rise in [H⁺] of about 26%\nStrand 2: Buffering\n• seawater contains HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻ buffer: H⁺ + CO₃²⁻ → HCO₃⁻\n• large reservoir of carbonate/hydrogencarbonate so [HCO₃⁻]/[CO₃²⁻] ratio changes only a little\n• the buffering uses up carbonate ions, so capacity to absorb more CO₂ falls\nStrand 3: Shells and reefs\n• [CO₃²⁻] falls, so CaCO₃(s) ⇌ Ca²⁺(aq) + CO₃²⁻(aq) shifts right\n• overall CaCO₃ + CO₂ + H₂O → Ca²⁺ + 2HCO₃⁻\n• ion product [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻] approaches Ksp, so organisms find it harder to build shells/skeletons and existing reefs may dissolve\n• effect greater in cold water where CO₂ is more soluble\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A student wants to determine the acid dissociation constant, Ka, of a weak acid that is used as a model for carbonic acid in ocean chemistry. She has a 0.100 mol dm⁻³ solution of the acid, 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide, a pH meter, buffer solutions of pH 4.00 and 7.00, and standard laboratory apparatus.\n\nDescribe an experiment the student could use to find a reliable value of Ka using the half-neutralisation method. Include how the result is obtained from the measurements.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1: Preparing and measuring\n• calibrate the pH meter using the pH 4.00 and 7.00 buffers; rinse electrode with deionised water between solutions\n• pipette 25.0 cm³ of the acid into a beaker/conical flask\n• add NaOH from a burette in small portions, stirring, recording pH after each addition\n• add smaller portions near the equivalence point\nStrand 2: Processing the results\n• plot pH against volume of NaOH; find the equivalence volume from the steep part of the curve\n• at half the equivalence volume, [HA] = [A⁻]\n• so Ka = [H⁺], i.e. pH = pKa; Ka = 10⁻ᵖᴴ at half-neutralisation\nStrand 3: Reliability and accuracy\n• keep temperature constant (Ka depends on temperature) / record temperature\n• repeat the titration and find a mean value\n• alternative: titrate to find equivalence, then mix a second 25.0 cm³ portion of acid with half that volume of NaOH and measure pH directly\n• uncertainty of pH meter / burette readings considered\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `Two ways of reducing the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere have been proposed:\n• Method A: capture CO₂ from power station flue gases and pump it, as a liquid, into the deep ocean.\n• Method B: capture CO₂ and react it with metal oxides or silicate minerals to form solid carbonates, which are stored underground.\n\nExplain why reducing atmospheric carbon dioxide would reduce global warming, and evaluate the two methods of carbon capture and storage.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1: Greenhouse effect\n• Earth's surface emits IR radiation; CO₂ absorbs IR, increasing bond vibrational energy\n• energy is passed to other molecules/re-emitted, warming the troposphere\n• CO₂ is abundant and long-lived so reducing its concentration reduces the enhanced greenhouse effect\nStrand 2: Method A (deep ocean storage)\n• large capacity; at high pressure and low temperature CO₂ is more soluble/dense\n• CO₂ may be released back to the atmosphere over time\n• increases acidity of deep water: CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻; harms marine organisms and dissolves carbonate shells\nStrand 3: Method B (mineral storage)\n• e.g. MgO + CO₂ → MgCO₃ / CaO + CO₂ → CaCO₃\n• carbonate is stable so storage is permanent and does not affect ocean pH\n• requires mining and transport of large amounts of mineral / slow reaction / energy cost (which may release more CO₂)\n• both methods need energy to capture and compress CO₂; neither removes CO₂ already in the atmosphere unless captured from air\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'DM': {
    name: 'Developing Metals',
    questions: [
      { q: `Brass is an alloy of copper and zinc. When brass is dissolved in acid, the zinc forms Zn²⁺ ions.\n\nWrite the full electron configuration of a Zn²⁺ ion.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ ✓\nALLOW 4s⁰ written at the end\nDO NOT ALLOW [Ar] 3d¹⁰ (question asks for the full configuration)\nDO NOT ALLOW 3d⁸ 4s²` },
      { q: `Copper has been used for thousands of years, first as the native metal and later extracted from ores such as malachite and chalcopyrite.\n\nWrite the full electron configuration of a copper atom.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ ✓\nALLOW 4s¹ written before 3d¹⁰\nDO NOT ALLOW 3d⁹ 4s²` },
      { q: `Stainless steel contains about 18% chromium. The chromium forms a thin oxide layer that stops the steel from rusting.\n\nWrite the electron configuration of a chromium atom, using [Ar] to represent the inner electrons.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `[Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹ ✓\nALLOW 4s¹ 3d⁵\nDO NOT ALLOW [Ar] 3d⁴ 4s²` },
      { q: `Copper(II) ions can be removed from industrial waste water by adding EDTA⁴⁻, a hexadentate ligand. One EDTA⁴⁻ ion bonds to one Cu²⁺ ion to form the complex [Cu(EDTA)]²⁻.\n\nState the coordination number of copper in [Cu(EDTA)]²⁻.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `6 ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 1` },
      { q: `Chromium plating baths contain dichromate(VI) ions, Cr₂O₇²⁻. Before the waste is disposed of, the dichromate ions are reduced to Cr³⁺ ions.\n\nDeduce the oxidation state of chromium in Cr₂O₇²⁻.`, marks: 1, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `+6 ✓\nALLOW 6+ or VI\nIGNORE working` },
      { q: `Water that has been standing in old iron pipes is often discoloured. A sample of the water is tested for Fe³⁺ ions by adding a few drops of aqueous sodium hydroxide.\n\nState what would be observed if Fe³⁺ ions are present.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Orange-brown precipitate ✓\nALLOW rust-coloured / red-brown / brown precipitate\nDO NOT ALLOW orange-brown solution (must be a precipitate)` },
      { q: `A student uses a colorimeter to measure the concentration of copper(II) ions in a solution made by dissolving a sample of brass in acid. The solution is pale blue.\n\nState the colour of the filter the student should use in the colorimeter.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Orange OR red ✓\nALLOW red-orange\nDO NOT ALLOW blue` },
      { q: `A student sets up a cell from a Cu²⁺/Cu half-cell and a Zn²⁺/Zn half-cell. The two solutions are connected by a strip of filter paper soaked in saturated potassium nitrate solution.\n\nState the function of the strip of filter paper.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `It completes the circuit by allowing ions to move between the half-cells ✓\nALLOW acts as a salt bridge to allow ions to flow / maintains electrical neutrality by movement of ions\nDO NOT ALLOW allows electrons to flow` },
      { q: `Brass contains copper and zinc. Both are d-block elements, but only copper is classed as a transition metal.\n\nExplain why copper is a transition metal but zinc is not.\nUse electron configurations of ions in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Copper forms Cu²⁺ which has an incomplete d sub-shell: [Ar] 3d⁹ ✓\nZinc only forms Zn²⁺ which has a full d sub-shell: [Ar] 3d¹⁰ ✓\nALLOW a transition metal forms one or more stable ions with an incomplete d sub-shell for the idea, if applied to both metals\nIGNORE references to atoms` },
      { q: `Solutions of zinc sulfate are colourless, whereas solutions of copper(II) sulfate are blue.\n\nExplain why solutions containing [Zn(H₂O)₆]²⁺ ions are colourless.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Zn²⁺ has a full 3d sub-shell / 3d¹⁰ ✓\nSo no electron can be promoted between the split d orbitals / no d–d transitions, so no visible light is absorbed ✓\nALLOW all visible light is transmitted\nDO NOT ALLOW Zn²⁺ has no d electrons` },
      { q: `Iron(III) forms the complex ion [Fe(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻ with ethanedioate ions, C₂O₄²⁻. Ethanedioate is a bidentate ligand.\n\nExplain what is meant by the term bidentate ligand, with reference to ethanedioate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `A ligand that forms two coordinate (dative covalent) bonds to the metal ion ✓\nEthanedioate has two O atoms that each donate a lone pair to the Fe³⁺ ion ✓\nALLOW two donor atoms\nDO NOT ALLOW two lone pairs on the same atom` },
      { q: `When concentrated hydrochloric acid is added to copper(II) sulfate solution, the complex ion [CuCl₄]²⁻ forms.\n\nState the shape of the [CuCl₄]²⁻ ion and the Cl–Cu–Cl bond angle.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `Tetrahedral ✓\n109.5° ✓\nALLOW 109° to 110°\nDO NOT ALLOW square planar / 90°` },
      { q: `Copper(II) sulfate solution contains [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ ions. When concentrated hydrochloric acid is added, a ligand substitution reaction takes place and [CuCl₄]²⁻ ions form.\n\nWrite an equation for this reaction and state the colour change seen.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ → [CuCl₄]²⁻ + 6H₂O ✓\nALLOW ⇌\nBlue to yellow ✓\nALLOW blue to green, then yellow / blue to yellow-green\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `Rusting of iron is an electrochemical process. Two of the half-reactions involved are:\nFe²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Fe(s)   E⦵ = −0.44 V\nO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) + 4e⁻ ⇌ 4OH⁻(aq)   E⦵ = +0.40 V\n\nCalculate E⦵cell for the cell formed and explain which species is oxidised.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `E⦵cell = 0.40 − (−0.44) = (+)0.84 V ✓\nIron (Fe) is oxidised because the Fe²⁺/Fe half-cell has the more negative electrode potential, so it releases electrons ✓\nALLOW Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻ as evidence of oxidation of Fe\nDO NOT ALLOW −0.84 V` },
      { q: `Waste from chromium plating contains Cr³⁺ ions. A technician adds aqueous sodium hydroxide dropwise to a sample of the waste. A grey-green precipitate of Cr(OH)₃ forms. When excess sodium hydroxide is added, the precipitate dissolves.\n\nWrite an equation for the precipitate dissolving and state the colour of the final solution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Cr(OH)₃ + 3OH⁻ → [Cr(OH)₆]³⁻ ✓\nALLOW Cr(OH)₃(H₂O)₃ + 3OH⁻ → [Cr(OH)₆]³⁻ + 3H₂O\n(Dark) green solution ✓\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `A solution made by dissolving scrap iron in dilute sulfuric acid contains Fe²⁺ ions. Aqueous sodium hydroxide is added and a green precipitate forms.\n\nWrite the ionic equation, including state symbols, for the formation of the green precipitate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂ ✓\nCorrect state symbols: Fe²⁺(aq), OH⁻(aq), Fe(OH)₂(s) ✓\nALLOW [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₂(H₂O)₄(s) + 2H₂O(l)\nState symbol mark depends on a correct equation` },
      { q: `The Daniell cell, one of the earliest practical batteries, used a zinc electrode in zinc sulfate solution and a copper electrode in copper(II) sulfate solution.\nZn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Zn(s)   E⦵ = −0.76 V\nCu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu(s)   E⦵ = +0.34 V\n\nCalculate the standard cell potential, E⦵cell, and state which electrode is the positive electrode.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `E⦵cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = (+)1.10 V ✓\nCopper (electrode) is positive ✓\nALLOW Cu²⁺/Cu half-cell is positive\nDO NOT ALLOW 1.10 V with a negative sign\nCorrect answer with no working scores the first mark` },
      { q: `One way of recovering copper from a dilute solution of copper(II) sulfate is to add scrap iron. Copper metal is deposited.\nFe²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Fe(s)   E⦵ = −0.44 V\nCu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu(s)   E⦵ = +0.34 V\n\nWrite the ionic equation for the reaction and use the E⦵ values to explain why it is feasible.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Fe + Cu²⁺ → Fe²⁺ + Cu ✓\nE⦵ for Cu²⁺/Cu is more positive (than Fe²⁺/Fe), so Cu²⁺ is reduced / gains electrons from Fe ✓\nALLOW E⦵cell = (+)0.78 V, which is positive, so the reaction is feasible\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `Iron(II) ions catalyse the slow reaction between peroxodisulfate ions and iodide ions:\nS₂O₈²⁻(aq) + 2I⁻(aq) → 2SO₄²⁻(aq) + I₂(aq)\nThe catalysis works because iron can change between the +2 and +3 oxidation states.\n\nWrite equations for the two steps of the catalysed reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺ ✓\n2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂ ✓\nALLOW the two steps in either order\nALLOW multiples\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `Copper(II) sulfate solution, CuSO₄(aq), is blue because it contains [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ ions.\n\nExplain why solutions containing [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ ions are blue.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The (water) ligands cause the 3d orbitals to split into two energy levels ✓\nAn electron is promoted from a lower to a higher d orbital by absorbing visible light / photon of energy ΔE = hν ✓\nRed / orange light is absorbed and the complementary colour, blue, is transmitted / reflected ✓\nALLOW Cu²⁺ has an incomplete d sub-shell (3d⁹) for the idea that electrons can be promoted, if linked to splitting\nDO NOT ALLOW blue light is absorbed\nDO NOT ALLOW electrons fall back and emit blue light` },
      { q: `Aluminium has a very negative standard electrode potential:\nAl³⁺(aq) + 3e⁻ ⇌ Al(s)   E⦵ = −1.66 V\n2H₂O(l) + 2e⁻ ⇌ H₂(g) + 2OH⁻(aq)   E⦵ = −0.83 V\nThe E⦵ values predict that aluminium should react with water, but aluminium window frames do not react with rain water.\n\nExplain why E⦵ values do not always predict whether a reaction will be observed.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `E⦵ values only predict whether a reaction is (thermodynamically) feasible, not how fast it is ✓\nThe reaction may have a high activation energy and so be very slow / aluminium is protected by an (impermeable) oxide layer ✓\nE⦵ values apply to standard conditions (1 mol dm⁻³, 298 K, 100 kPa); actual conditions are not standard, so electrode potentials differ ✓\nALLOW kinetically stable / kinetic reasons for the first two marks\nIGNORE references to Ecell being small` },
      { q: `A student plans to find the concentration of Cu²⁺ ions in a solution using a colorimeter. They have a stock solution of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ copper(II) sulfate, distilled water and volumetric glassware.\n\nDescribe how the student should produce a calibration curve.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Make up solutions of known concentration (at least 4) by diluting the stock solution, e.g. using a burette or pipette and volumetric flask ✓\nUse an orange / red filter AND zero the colorimeter with distilled water (in a cuvette) ✓\nMeasure the absorbance of each standard and plot absorbance (y-axis) against concentration (x-axis) ✓\nALLOW use a blank for zeroing\nALLOW transmission plotted against concentration for the last mark\nIGNORE references to the unknown solution` },
      { q: `A sample of scrap metal from a recycling centre is dissolved in acid. The technician has three unlabelled solutions. Each contains one of the ions Cu²⁺, Fe³⁺ or Zn²⁺.\n\nDescribe how the technician could use aqueous sodium hydroxide to identify each ion. Include the observations for each ion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Add NaOH(aq) dropwise, then in excess, to a sample of each solution AND Cu²⁺ gives a blue precipitate ✓\nFe³⁺ gives an orange-brown precipitate ✓\nZn²⁺ gives a white precipitate which dissolves in excess NaOH (to give a colourless solution) ✓\nALLOW pale blue for Cu²⁺; ALLOW rust / red-brown for Fe³⁺\nDO NOT ALLOW 'solution' instead of 'precipitate'\nThe 'dissolves in excess' point must be given for Zn²⁺` },
      { q: `Copper(II) sulfate solution is used in a school test for the copper content of brass. A student adds aqueous ammonia dropwise to a solution containing [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ ions until the ammonia is in excess.\n\nDescribe what the student would see and write an equation for the reaction that occurs with excess ammonia.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `(Pale) blue precipitate forms at first ✓\nPrecipitate dissolves in excess to give a deep blue / dark blue / royal blue solution ✓\nCu(OH)₂(H₂O)₄ + 4NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 2H₂O + 2OH⁻ ✓\nALLOW [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 4H₂O\nALLOW Cu(OH)₂ + 4NH₃ + 2H₂O → [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 2OH⁻\nDO NOT ALLOW purple solution` },
      { q: `EDTA⁴⁻ is used in the treatment of copper poisoning. It replaces the water ligands around Cu²⁺ ions:\n[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺(aq) + EDTA⁴⁻(aq) → [Cu(EDTA)]²⁻(aq) + 6H₂O(l)\nThe enthalpy change for this reaction is very small.\n\nExplain, in terms of entropy, why [Cu(EDTA)]²⁻ is much more stable than [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 particles / moles on the left form 7 particles / moles on the right ✓\nSo the entropy of the system increases / ΔS(system) is positive ✓\nΔS(total) is positive (as ΔH is close to zero, ΔS(surroundings) is approximately zero), so the reaction is feasible / equilibrium lies far to the right ✓\nALLOW 'more ways of arranging the particles' for increase in entropy\nDO NOT ALLOW stronger bonds as the reason` },
      { q: `The iron content of a sample can be found by titration with acidified potassium manganate(VII). The half-equations are:\nMnO₄⁻(aq) + 8H⁺(aq) + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺(aq) + 4H₂O(l)\nFe²⁺(aq) → Fe³⁺(aq) + e⁻\n\nWrite the overall equation for the reaction and explain why no indicator is needed for this titration.`, marks: 3, command: 'Write', markScheme: `MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O ✓✓\n1 mark for correct species, 1 mark for correct balancing\nMnO₄⁻ is purple and is decolourised (Mn²⁺ is almost colourless), so the end point is the first permanent pale pink colour ✓\nIGNORE state symbols\nDO NOT ALLOW the solution turns colourless at the end point` },
      { q: `Copper extracted from ores is purified by electrolysis. Impure copper is the anode, pure copper is the cathode and the electrolyte is copper(II) sulfate solution. The impure copper contains small amounts of silver and zinc.\nZn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Zn(s)   E⦵ = −0.76 V\nCu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu(s)   E⦵ = +0.34 V\nAg⁺(aq) + e⁻ ⇌ Ag(s)   E⦵ = +0.80 V\n\nExplain why only pure copper is deposited at the cathode. Include a half-equation in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu ✓\nSilver is not oxidised at the anode (Ag⁺/Ag is more positive than Cu²⁺/Cu), so it falls to the bottom as sludge / does not enter the solution ✓\nZinc is oxidised to Zn²⁺, but Zn²⁺ is not reduced at the cathode because Zn²⁺/Zn is more negative than Cu²⁺/Cu (Cu²⁺ is reduced in preference) ✓\nALLOW Zn²⁺ remains in solution\nIGNORE references to the anode half-equation` },
      { q: `Iron(III) chloride solution is used to etch copper circuit boards. A student wonders whether iron(III) ions will also oxidise iodide ions.\nFe³⁺(aq) + e⁻ ⇌ Fe²⁺(aq)   E⦵ = +0.77 V\nI₂(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ 2I⁻(aq)   E⦵ = +0.54 V\n\nDeduce whether Fe³⁺ ions will oxidise I⁻ ions under standard conditions. Give the E⦵cell value and an equation for any reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `E⦵cell = 0.77 − 0.54 = (+)0.23 V ✓\nPositive, so Fe³⁺ will oxidise I⁻ / reaction is feasible ✓\n2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂ ✓\nALLOW Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ has the more positive E⦵, so Fe³⁺ gains electrons from I⁻, for the second mark\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `A student wants to measure the standard electrode potential of the Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ half-cell using a standard hydrogen electrode.\n\nDraw a labelled diagram of the apparatus the student should use. Include the conditions needed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Hydrogen electrode: H₂ gas bubbling over a platinum electrode dipping into H⁺(aq) ✓\nFe³⁺/Fe²⁺ half-cell: platinum electrode in a solution containing both Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ ✓\nSalt bridge connecting the two solutions AND (high resistance) voltmeter connecting the electrodes ✓\nConditions: 1.00 mol dm⁻³ H⁺ AND equal concentrations (1.00 mol dm⁻³) of Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ AND H₂ at 100 kPa AND 298 K ✓\nALLOW 1 atm / 101 kPa, 25 °C\nDO NOT ALLOW an iron electrode in the Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ half-cell\nDO NOT ALLOW a wire in place of the salt bridge` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA 0.500 g sample of brass was dissolved in nitric acid and the solution was made up to 250.0 cm³ with distilled water. A colorimeter was used to measure the absorbance of the solution. A calibration was carried out using standard solutions of Cu²⁺(aq):\nConcentration of Cu²⁺ / mol dm⁻³: 0.000, 0.010, 0.020, 0.030, 0.040, 0.050\nAbsorbance: 0.00, 0.12, 0.24, 0.36, 0.48, 0.60\nThe brass solution had an absorbance of 0.27.\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of copper in the brass. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[Cu²⁺] = 0.27 ÷ 12 = 0.0225 mol dm⁻³ ✓\nMoles of Cu²⁺ in 250.0 cm³ = 0.0225 × 0.2500 = 5.625 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nMass of Cu = 5.625 × 10⁻³ × 63.5 = 0.357 g ✓\nPercentage = 0.357 ÷ 0.500 × 100 = 71.4% ✓\nALLOW concentration read from the line as 0.022 to 0.023 mol dm⁻³ with ECF\nECF at each stage\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student determines the percentage of iron in a piece of steel wire. A 1.40 g sample of the wire is dissolved in excess dilute sulfuric acid, which converts all the iron into Fe²⁺ ions. The solution is made up to 250.0 cm³. 25.0 cm³ portions are titrated with 0.0200 mol dm⁻³ potassium manganate(VII). The mean titre is 24.60 cm³.\nMnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of iron in the steel wire. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Moles of MnO₄⁻ = 0.0200 × 24.60 ÷ 1000 = 4.92 × 10⁻⁴ mol ✓\nMoles of Fe²⁺ in 25.0 cm³ = 5 × 4.92 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.46 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nMoles of Fe in 250.0 cm³ = 2.46 × 10⁻² mol; mass = 2.46 × 10⁻² × 55.8 = 1.37 g ✓\nPercentage = 1.37 ÷ 1.40 × 100 = 98.0% ✓\nALLOW 98.1% (from rounding of the mass)\nECF at each stage\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Iron railings near the sea rust faster than iron railings inland. Rusting is an electrochemical process that needs both oxygen and water.\n\nExplain how iron rusts and why it rusts faster near the sea. Include half-equations in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻ ✓\nO₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻ ✓\nFe²⁺ and OH⁻ form Fe(OH)₂, which is oxidised (by O₂) to Fe(OH)₃ / hydrated iron(III) oxide, Fe₂O₃·xH₂O (rust) ✓\nSalt (from sea spray) increases the conductivity of the water / dissolved ions act as an electrolyte, so the electrochemical reaction is faster ✓\nALLOW Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂ for the third mark if oxidation to iron(III) is mentioned\nDO NOT ALLOW salt acts as a catalyst` },
      { q: `A recycling plant dissolves scrap steel in acid. A technician tests two samples of the solution, A and B. Each contains one metal ion.\nSample A: aqueous sodium hydroxide gives an off-white / pale brown precipitate that darkens on standing in air. The precipitate does not dissolve in excess NaOH(aq).\nSample B: aqueous sodium hydroxide gives a green precipitate that turns orange-brown at the surface on standing in air. The precipitate does not dissolve in excess NaOH(aq).\n\nIdentify the metal ions in A and B. Explain why both precipitates change colour on standing, and write an ionic equation for the formation of the precipitate in A.`, marks: 4, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `A: Mn²⁺ ✓\nB: Fe²⁺ ✓\nThe precipitates are oxidised by oxygen in the air (to a higher oxidation state, e.g. Fe(OH)₂ to Fe(OH)₃) ✓\nMn²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Mn(OH)₂ ✓\nALLOW [Mn(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Mn(OH)₂(H₂O)₄ + 2H₂O\nIGNORE state symbols\nDO NOT ALLOW Fe³⁺ for B` },
      { q: `A student builds a Daniell cell using copper and zinc electrodes in 1.00 mol dm⁻³ solutions, connected by a salt bridge. The predicted E⦵cell is 1.10 V, but the student measures 1.02 V. The student cleaned the electrodes, but did not check the temperature, and used solutions prepared a week earlier that had been left uncovered.\n\nSuggest two reasons why the measured value is lower than 1.10 V. For each, explain how the procedure could be improved.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two reasons, each with a matching improvement (2 × 2 marks):\nConcentrations may not be 1.00 mol dm⁻³ (e.g. evaporation / contamination) ✓ → make up fresh solutions accurately using a volumetric flask ✓\nTemperature not 298 K ✓ → use a thermostatically controlled water bath at 298 K ✓\nA low-resistance voltmeter / current drawn from the cell lowers the voltage ✓ → use a high-resistance (digital) voltmeter ✓\nHigh resistance of salt bridge / salt bridge dried out ✓ → use a freshly soaked salt bridge of saturated KNO₃ ✓\nOxide layer on zinc electrode ✓ → clean with emery paper immediately before use ✓\nThe improvement mark must match the reason given\nIGNORE 'human error' / 'repeat the experiment'` },
      { q: `The copper content of brass can be found by an iodine–thiosulfate titration. A 1.25 g sample of brass is dissolved in nitric acid, neutralised and made up to 250.0 cm³. A 25.0 cm³ portion is added to excess potassium iodide solution:\n2Cu²⁺(aq) + 4I⁻(aq) → 2CuI(s) + I₂(aq)\nThe iodine released is titrated with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium thiosulfate, using starch indicator near the end point. The mean titre is 13.20 cm³.\nI₂(aq) + 2S₂O₃²⁻(aq) → 2I⁻(aq) + S₄O₆²⁻(aq)\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of copper in the brass. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Moles of S₂O₃²⁻ = 0.100 × 13.20 ÷ 1000 = 1.32 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nMoles of Cu²⁺ in 25.0 cm³ = 1.32 × 10⁻³ mol (2Cu²⁺ ≡ I₂ ≡ 2S₂O₃²⁻, so 1 : 1) ✓\nMoles of Cu in 250.0 cm³ = 1.32 × 10⁻² mol ✓\nMass of Cu = 1.32 × 10⁻² × 63.5 = 0.838 g ✓\nPercentage = 0.838 ÷ 1.25 × 100 = 67.1% ✓\nECF at each stage\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Iron(II) sulfate crystals, FeSO₄·xH₂O, can be made by dissolving scrap iron in dilute sulfuric acid. A student dissolves 6.95 g of the crystals in dilute sulfuric acid and makes the solution up to 250.0 cm³. 25.0 cm³ portions are titrated with 0.0200 mol dm⁻³ potassium manganate(VII). The mean titre is 25.00 cm³.\nMnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O\n\nDetermine the value of x. Show your working.`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `Moles of MnO₄⁻ = 0.0200 × 25.00 ÷ 1000 = 5.00 × 10⁻⁴ mol ✓\nMoles of Fe²⁺ in 25.0 cm³ = 5 × 5.00 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.50 × 10⁻³ mol; in 250.0 cm³ = 2.50 × 10⁻² mol ✓\nMr of FeSO₄·xH₂O = 6.95 ÷ 0.0250 = 278 ✓\nMr of FeSO₄ = 151.9, so mass of water = 278 − 151.9 = 126(.1) ✓\nx = 126 ÷ 18.0 = 7 ✓\nECF at each stage\nALLOW x = 7.0` },
      { q: `Copper is recovered from waste printed circuit boards by dissolving the metal in acid. The resulting solution contains Cu²⁺ ions at a concentration of roughly 0.02–0.05 mol dm⁻³. A chemist wants to find the Cu²⁺ concentration accurately using colorimetry. Available: a colorimeter with a set of filters, cuvettes, a 0.100 mol dm⁻³ copper(II) sulfate stock solution, distilled water and standard laboratory glassware.\n\nDescribe an experiment to determine the concentration of Cu²⁺ ions in the solution. Explain how the results would be processed and how the reliability and accuracy of the result would be ensured.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1 – Preparing standards and setting up\n• Dilute the stock solution to make at least 5 standards, e.g. 0.010–0.060 mol dm⁻³, covering the expected range\n• Use a burette / graduated pipette and volumetric flasks\n• Choose the complementary filter (orange/red) so absorbance is greatest / most sensitive\n• Zero the colorimeter with a cuvette of distilled water (blank)\nStrand 2 – Measurements and processing\n• Measure the absorbance of each standard and of the unknown, using the same cuvette / same filter\n• Plot a calibration curve of absorbance against concentration\n• Read off the concentration of the unknown from its absorbance\n• Allow for any dilution of the unknown\nStrand 3 – Reliability and accuracy\n• Repeat absorbance readings and take a mean\n• Wipe cuvettes, avoid fingerprints on the clear faces, align them the same way\n• Check the unknown lies within the calibration range; dilute if absorbance is too high\n• Re-zero between readings; keep temperature constant\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `The steel hull of a ship corrodes in sea water. Engineers can paint the hull, attach blocks of magnesium or zinc, or coat steel parts with another metal such as tin or zinc.\nMg²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Mg(s)   E⦵ = −2.37 V\nZn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Zn(s)   E⦵ = −0.76 V\nFe²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Fe(s)   E⦵ = −0.44 V\nSn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Sn(s)   E⦵ = −0.14 V\nO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) + 4e⁻ ⇌ 4OH⁻(aq)   E⦵ = +0.40 V\n\nExplain the chemistry of how the steel hull corrodes and evaluate these methods of protecting it. Use the E⦵ values in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1 – Corrosion chemistry\n• Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻ (Fe²⁺/Fe is more negative than O₂/OH⁻)\n• O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻\n• Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂, then oxidised by O₂ to Fe(OH)₃ / Fe₂O₃·xH₂O\n• Dissolved salt in sea water is a good electrolyte, increasing the rate\nStrand 2 – Barrier methods\n• Paint keeps out oxygen and water\n• Cheap and easy, but protection is lost when scratched / chipped and needs renewing\n• Tin plating is a barrier, but Sn²⁺/Sn is less negative than Fe²⁺/Fe, so if scratched the iron is oxidised in preference and corrodes faster\nStrand 3 – Sacrificial protection\n• Mg and Zn have more negative E⦵ than Fe, so they are oxidised in preference and supply electrons to the iron\n• Works even if the surface is scratched (e.g. galvanised zinc coating)\n• Blocks must be replaced as they are used up; Mg corrodes fastest (most negative)\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nMost copper ore now mined contains less than 1% copper. One extraction route leaches the crushed ore with dilute sulfuric acid to give a solution of Cu²⁺ ions; copper is then obtained by adding scrap iron or by electrolysis, and purified by electrolysis. The table compares this with recycling scrap copper.\nEnergy needed per tonne of copper / GJ: extraction from ore 110; recycling scrap 15\nWaste rock produced per tonne of copper / tonnes: extraction from ore 200; recycling scrap 0\nCO₂ released per tonne of copper / tonnes: extraction from ore 4.0; recycling scrap 0.6\n\nDescribe the chemistry of obtaining copper from a low-grade ore and evaluate whether copper should be obtained by extraction from ore or by recycling.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1 – Chemistry of extraction\n• Leaching: copper compounds react with H₂SO₄ to give Cu²⁺(aq), e.g. CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O\n• Displacement by scrap iron: Fe + Cu²⁺ → Fe²⁺ + Cu, feasible as Cu²⁺/Cu (+0.34 V) is more positive than Fe²⁺/Fe (−0.44 V)\n• Electrolysis: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu at the cathode\nStrand 2 – Chemistry of recycling / purification\n• Scrap copper is melted or used as an impure anode: Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻\n• Pure copper deposited at the cathode; less reactive metals (e.g. Ag) collect as anode sludge, more reactive metals (e.g. Zn from brass) stay in solution\nStrand 3 – Evaluation using the data\n• Recycling uses about one seventh of the energy (15 vs 110 GJ), so fewer fossil fuels burnt and less CO₂ (0.6 vs 4.0 t)\n• Recycling produces no waste rock, so less land damage and fewer spoil heaps\n• Ore is a finite resource; recycling conserves it\n• But scrap must be collected, sorted and separated from other materials (e.g. plastics, zinc in brass), which costs money and energy; recycling alone cannot meet all demand\n• Conclusion: recycle where possible, extraction still needed\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'CD': {
    name: 'Colour by Design',
    questions: [
      { q: `Many azo dyes used to colour cotton fabrics contain one or more –SO₃H groups attached to a benzene ring. These groups are introduced by sulfonation of the arene.\n\nState the reagent used to sulfonate benzene.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Fuming sulfuric acid OR concentrated sulfuric acid (containing dissolved SO₃) ✓\nALLOW SO₃ in H₂SO₄ / oleum\nDO NOT ALLOW dilute sulfuric acid` },
      { q: `Methyl orange is an azo dye used as an indicator and, in the past, to dye wool. Its structure can be written as\n\n(CH₃)₂N–C₆H₄–N=N–C₆H₄–SO₃⁻ Na⁺\n\nIdentify the group of atoms that links the two benzene rings and gives the dye class its name.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `–N=N– (azo group) ✓\nALLOW diazene / nitrogen–nitrogen double bond\nDO NOT ALLOW –N≡N– OR diazonium` },
      { q: `Linseed oil is used as the binding medium in many oil paintings. Conservators analyse old paint by heating a tiny sample of the oil with aqueous sodium hydroxide, which breaks the triglyceride into propane-1,2,3-triol and the sodium salts of fatty acids.\n\nState the type of reaction that occurs.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `(Alkaline) hydrolysis ✓\nALLOW saponification\nIGNORE substitution / esterification` },
      { q: `Bromobenzene is an intermediate in the manufacture of some dyes. It is made by reacting benzene with bromine in the presence of a catalyst.\n\nState a suitable catalyst for this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Iron(III) bromide / FeBr₃ ✓\nALLOW iron (filings) OR AlBr₃ OR halogen carrier with correct formula\nDO NOT ALLOW UV light` },
      { q: `A food scientist analyses a strawberry flavouring by gas–liquid chromatography (GLC). Each component of the flavouring appears as a peak on the chromatogram at a characteristic retention time.\n\nState what is meant by the retention time of a component.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `The time taken from injection of the sample for the component to pass through the column and reach the detector ✓\nALLOW time between injection and the peak / time spent in the column\nIGNORE references to distance travelled` },
      { q: `To make an azo dye in the laboratory, a student first reacts phenylamine with nitrous acid to form benzenediazonium chloride. The reaction mixture is kept in an ice bath below 10 °C.\n\nSuggest why the mixture must be kept below 10 °C.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Benzenediazonium ion / diazonium salt is unstable and decomposes (to phenol and nitrogen) at higher temperatures ✓\nALLOW diazonium salt would react with water / N₂ evolved\nIGNORE 'reaction is exothermic' on its own` },
      { q: `A red food colouring has its maximum absorbance in the visible spectrum at a wavelength of 520 nm.\n\nCalculate the energy, in J, of one photon of light of wavelength 520 nm. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.\nh = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s;  c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `E = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸) ÷ (520 × 10⁻⁹) ✓ (correct conversion of nm to m)\n= 3.83 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW 3.825 × 10⁻¹⁹ for first mark only if not rounded to 3 sf\nDO NOT ALLOW answer using λ = 520 (no conversion) for any marks` },
      { q: `Nitrobenzene is the starting material for many azo dyes used on fabrics. It is made from benzene on an industrial scale.\n\nState the reagents and the conditions used to nitrate benzene in the laboratory.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `Concentrated nitric acid AND concentrated sulfuric acid ✓\nTemperature below about 55 °C / warm at 50 °C (to avoid further nitration) ✓\nALLOW 'reflux at 50–60 °C'\nDO NOT ALLOW dilute acids for first mark` },
      { q: `In the nitration of benzene, concentrated sulfuric acid reacts with concentrated nitric acid to produce the electrophile, the nitronium ion.\n\nWrite an equation, or equations, to show the formation of the nitronium ion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Correct species: NO₂⁺ formed from HNO₃ and H₂SO₄ ✓\nBalanced: HNO₃ + H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + HSO₄⁻ + H₂O ✓\nALLOW two steps: HNO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂NO₃⁺ + HSO₄⁻ then H₂NO₃⁺ → NO₂⁺ + H₂O\nALLOW HNO₃ + 2H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + H₃O⁺ + 2HSO₄⁻\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `Phenylamine, C₆H₅NH₂, is made from nitrobenzene in the first stage of making a yellow azo dye for polyester.\n\nGive the reagents used to convert nitrobenzene into phenylamine and state the type of reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `Tin AND concentrated hydrochloric acid (heated under reflux), followed by (excess) sodium hydroxide ✓\nReduction ✓\nALLOW Sn/HCl then NaOH; ALLOW iron/HCl\nIGNORE 'redox' for second mark unless reduction stated` },
      { q: `A student uses a colorimeter to find the concentration of a red azo dye (E129, Allura Red) in a fizzy drink. The dye looks red because it absorbs mainly blue-green light.\n\nExplain why the student should use a blue-green filter rather than a red filter in the colorimeter.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Blue-green light is the colour absorbed (most) by the dye / red light is transmitted (not absorbed) ✓\nSo absorbance changes most with concentration / gives greatest sensitivity / largest absorbance readings ✓\nALLOW complementary colour of the solution\nIGNORE 'more accurate' unqualified` },
      { q: `A student suspects that a sample of a liquid used to make a fragrance for scented dyes is a carbonyl compound. She adds a few drops of the liquid to 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine (2,4-DNPH) solution.\n\nDescribe the observation for a positive result and explain how the product can then be used to identify the carbonyl compound.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Orange / yellow / red precipitate (crystals) forms ✓\nPrecipitate filtered, recrystallised and its melting point measured and compared with data-book values for 2,4-DNPH derivatives ✓\nALLOW orange solid\nDO NOT ALLOW orange solution` },
      { q: `Benzophenone, C₆H₅COC₆H₅, is used in some coatings that protect painted surfaces from fading. A chemist reacts benzophenone with sodium tetrahydridoborate(III), NaBH₄, in aqueous ethanol.\n\nGive the structural formula of the organic product and state the type of reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `C₆H₅CH(OH)C₆H₅ ✓\nReduction ✓\nALLOW diphenylmethanol (name) for first mark\nALLOW nucleophilic addition for second mark\nDO NOT ALLOW oxidation` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nThe strawberry flavouring is analysed by GLC. Three peaks are obtained. The detector is assumed to respond equally to each component.\n\nPeak | Retention time / min | Peak area / mm²\nA | 2.4 | 4.6\nB | 3.8 | 7.9\nC | 6.1 | 2.1\n\nCalculate the percentage of component B in the flavouring. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Total area = 4.6 + 7.9 + 2.1 = 14.6 (mm²) ✓\n% B = 7.9 ÷ 14.6 × 100 = 54.1 % ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Benzaldehyde, C₆H₅CHO, gives almond flavouring and is an intermediate in making triphenylmethane dyes such as malachite green. Its mass spectrum shows peaks at m/z = 106, 105 and 77.\n\nIdentify the species responsible for the peaks at m/z = 105 and m/z = 77.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `m/z 105: C₆H₅CO⁺ ✓\nm/z 77: C₆H₅⁺ ✓\nPositive charge needed on each species; penalise missing charge once only\nALLOW structural formulae / displayed formulae with charge` },
      { q: `A student adds bromine water to cyclohexene and to benzene in separate test tubes at room temperature. Cyclohexene decolourises the bromine water immediately; benzene does not.\n\nExplain the difference in reactivity in terms of the bonding in each compound.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Cyclohexene has a localised π bond / C=C with high electron density ✓\nBenzene has (six) π electrons delocalised over the ring, so lower electron density between any two carbons / cannot polarise Br₂ enough ✓\nAddition to benzene would destroy the delocalised system (which gives extra stability), so benzene does not react without a catalyst ✓\nALLOW benzene needs a halogen carrier / reacts by substitution not addition\nIGNORE 'benzene is stable' unqualified` },
      { q: `The enthalpy change of hydrogenation of cyclohexene is −120 kJ mol⁻¹. The enthalpy change of hydrogenation of benzene to cyclohexane is −208 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nUse these data to calculate how much more stable benzene is than the Kekulé structure (cyclohexa-1,3,5-triene) and explain what the result shows about the structure of benzene.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Predicted for Kekulé structure = 3 × (−120) = −360 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nBenzene is 360 − 208 = 152 kJ mol⁻¹ more stable ✓\nBenzene does not have three separate (localised) C=C bonds; π electrons are delocalised, which lowers the energy ✓\nALLOW 150 kJ mol⁻¹\nECF for difference from incorrect predicted value` },
      { q: `Phenylethanone, C₆H₅COCH₃, is used to make some yellow dyes. It is made by a Friedel–Crafts acylation of benzene.\n\nGive the reagents and conditions for this reaction, and write an equation to show how the electrophile is formed.`, marks: 3, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Ethanoyl chloride / CH₃COCl ✓\nAnhydrous aluminium chloride / AlCl₃ (as catalyst / halogen carrier) AND heat under reflux / dry conditions ✓\nCH₃COCl + AlCl₃ → CH₃CO⁺ + AlCl₄⁻ ✓\nALLOW iron(III) chloride as catalyst\nDO NOT ALLOW aqueous AlCl₃` },
      { q: `An orange azo dye for wool is made by reacting a cold solution of benzenediazonium chloride with phenol.\n\nState the conditions needed for the coupling reaction and write an equation for the reaction, using structural formulae. The phenol couples at the 4-position.`, marks: 3, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Phenol dissolved in (aqueous) sodium hydroxide / alkaline conditions AND below 10 °C ✓\nC₆H₅N₂⁺ + C₆H₅OH → C₆H₅N=NC₆H₄OH ✓ (product correct)\n+ H⁺ ✓ (balanced)\nALLOW Cl⁻ salts: C₆H₅N₂Cl + C₆H₅OH → C₆H₅N=NC₆H₄OH + HCl\nALLOW equation with phenoxide ion: C₆H₅N₂⁺ + C₆H₅O⁻ → C₆H₅N=NC₆H₄OH\nDO NOT ALLOW –N≡N– in product` },
      { q: `Azobenzene, C₆H₅N=NC₆H₅, is pale orange. Benzene, from which it is made, is colourless.\n\nExplain why azobenzene is coloured but benzene is not.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Azobenzene has a chromophore / extended delocalised (conjugated) system including the –N=N– group and both rings ✓\nThe energy gap between electron energy levels is smaller, so it absorbs light in the visible region (benzene absorbs in the UV) ✓\nThe colour seen is the complementary colour / the light transmitted or reflected (not absorbed) ✓\nALLOW electrons excited to higher energy levels by visible light\nIGNORE 'gives out' light` },
      { q: `Propanal and propanone are both colourless liquids with formula C₃H₆O. They are used as solvents in some paints.\n\nExplain how the ¹H NMR spectra of propanal and propanone would allow them to be distinguished.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Propanone: one peak / one hydrogen environment (singlet) ✓\nPropanal: three peaks / three hydrogen environments (in ratio 3:2:1) ✓\nPropanal has a peak at δ 9–10 ppm for –CHO hydrogen OR CH₃ peak in propanal is a triplet (split by CH₂) ✓\nALLOW peak at δ 2.1–2.6 for propanone CH₃\nALLOW any other correct splitting pattern for propanal` },
      { q: `A student has two unlabelled bottles. One contains benzaldehyde, C₆H₅CHO; the other contains phenylethanone, C₆H₅COCH₃. Both are used in making dyes.\n\nDescribe a chemical test that would distinguish between the two compounds. Include the reagent, the conditions and the observation for each compound.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Tollens' reagent (ammoniacal silver nitrate) AND warm in a water bath ✓\nBenzaldehyde: silver mirror / grey-black precipitate ✓\nPhenylethanone: no change / stays colourless ✓\nALLOW acidified (potassium) dichromate(VI), warm: benzaldehyde orange → green; phenylethanone stays orange\nIGNORE Fehling's solution (aromatic aldehydes give a poor result) — but ALLOW if blue → red/brick-red precipitate stated for benzaldehyde\nDo not credit observations if reagent is wrong` },
      { q: `Nitrobenzene is made by the reaction of benzene with the nitronium ion, NO₂⁺.\n\nDraw the mechanism for the reaction of benzene with NO₂⁺. Show curly arrows, relevant charges and the structure of the intermediate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Curly arrow from the delocalised ring (circle) to the N of NO₂⁺ ✓\nIntermediate: broken (horseshoe) delocalised ring with + charge, covering five carbons, with H and NO₂ on the sp³ carbon ✓\nCurly arrow from the C–H bond back into the ring ✓\nProducts: nitrobenzene AND H⁺ ✓\nALLOW H⁺ removed by HSO₄⁻ to give H₂SO₄\nCurly arrow must start from the ring, not from an individual carbon atom\nMechanism name not required` },
      { q: `Ethanal reacts with hydrogen cyanide in the presence of potassium cyanide to form 2-hydroxypropanenitrile, CH₃CH(OH)CN, an intermediate in making some synthetic dye precursors.\n\nDraw the mechanism for this reaction. Show curly arrows, relevant dipoles and lone pairs, and the intermediate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Dipole on C=O: Cδ+ and Oδ− ✓\nCurly arrow from lone pair (or negative charge) on C of ⁻CN to the carbonyl carbon ✓\nCurly arrow from C=O π bond to O ✓\nIntermediate CH₃CH(O⁻)CN AND curly arrow from O⁻ to H of HCN (or H⁺), regenerating ⁻CN ✓\nALLOW H⁺ from H₃O⁺\nDO NOT ALLOW arrow from N of CN⁻` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA student prepares the dye 4-hydroxyazobenzene, C₁₂H₁₀N₂O, starting from 2.33 g of phenylamine, C₆H₅NH₂. Each mole of phenylamine can produce one mole of dye. After purification the student obtains 3.62 g of dry dye.\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of the dye. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.\nAr: H 1.008, C 12.01, N 14.01, O 16.00`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mr phenylamine = 93.1 AND Mr dye = 198.2 ✓\nAmount of phenylamine = 2.33 ÷ 93.1 = 0.0250 mol ✓\nTheoretical mass of dye = 0.0250 × 198.2 = 4.96 g ✓\nPercentage yield = 3.62 ÷ 4.96 × 100 = 73.0 % ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nECF throughout\nALLOW 72.9–73.0 %` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA chemist analysing a yellow paint isolates an organic compound X. It contains only C, H and O.\n\nTechnique | Result\nMass spectrum | molecular ion peak at m/z = 120; other peaks at m/z = 105 and 77\nInfrared spectrum | strong absorption at 1685 cm⁻¹; no broad absorption between 2500 and 3500 cm⁻¹\n¹H NMR spectrum | δ 7.4–8.0 ppm (5H, multiplet); δ 2.6 ppm (3H, singlet)\n\nDeduce the structure of compound X. Explain your reasoning using the data.`, marks: 4, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `IR: 1685 cm⁻¹ shows C=O; no O–H (so not acid or alcohol) ✓\nMS: M = 120 / formula C₈H₈O; m/z 105 = C₆H₅CO⁺ (loss of CH₃) and/or 77 = C₆H₅⁺ ✓\nNMR: 5H at 7.4–8.0 = (monosubstituted) benzene ring; 3H singlet = CH₃ with no adjacent H (next to C=O) ✓\nX is phenylethanone, C₆H₅COCH₃ ✓\nALLOW any correct structural / displayed / skeletal formula` },
      { q: `A red azo dye for polyester fibres is made from benzene in five stages. The final structure is\n\nCH₃–C₆H₄–N=N–(naphthalene ring)–OH\n\nwhere the methyl group and the azo group are in the 1,4-positions on the benzene ring and the naphthalene part comes from naphthalen-2-ol.\n\nOutline a synthetic route from benzene to the dye. For each stage give the reagents and conditions and the organic product.`, marks: 5, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `Stage 1: chloromethane AND AlCl₃ (anhydrous), reflux → methylbenzene ✓\nStage 2: conc HNO₃ AND conc H₂SO₄, below 55 °C → 4-methylnitrobenzene (CH₃C₆H₄NO₂) ✓\nStage 3: Sn AND conc HCl, reflux, then NaOH → 4-methylphenylamine (CH₃C₆H₄NH₂) ✓\nStage 4: NaNO₂ AND HCl (nitrous acid) below 10 °C → 4-methylbenzenediazonium chloride (CH₃C₆H₄N₂⁺) ✓\nStage 5: naphthalen-2-ol in NaOH(aq), below 10 °C → the dye ✓\nALLOW any halogenomethane for stage 1\nEach mark needs reagent(s), condition AND product\nALLOW 2-methyl isomers formed in stage 2 if separation mentioned` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nMargarine is made by partially hydrogenating vegetable oils. Glyceryl trioleate, C₅₇H₁₀₄O₆, is a triglyceride in olive oil. Each molecule contains three C=C double bonds.\n\nCalculate the volume of hydrogen, in cm³ at RTP, needed to hydrogenate fully 10.0 g of glyceryl trioleate, and explain why the hydrogenated product has a higher melting point than the oil.\nAr: H 1.008, C 12.01, O 16.00. Molar gas volume at RTP = 24.0 dm³ mol⁻¹`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mr = 885.4 AND amount = 10.0 ÷ 885.4 = 0.0113 mol ✓\nAmount H₂ = 3 × 0.0113 = 0.0339 mol ✓\nVolume = 0.0339 × 24 000 = 813 cm³ ✓\nSaturated chains are straight / have no kinks (from cis C=C), so molecules pack more closely ✓\nStronger / more instantaneous dipole–induced dipole bonds between chains, so more energy needed to separate the molecules ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores the first 3 marks\nECF from incorrect Mr\nALLOW 812–814 cm³\nALLOW London forces / dispersion forces for 'instantaneous dipole–induced dipole bonds'` },
      { q: `In 1865 Kekulé proposed that benzene has a ring of six carbon atoms with alternating single and double bonds. The modern model has a delocalised ring of π electrons.\n\nExplain how the evidence supports the delocalised model of benzene rather than the Kekulé structure. Your answer should include physical evidence and evidence from reactions.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1: Physical evidence (bond lengths)\n• X-ray diffraction shows all C–C bonds are the same length (0.139 nm)\n• This is between C–C single (0.154 nm) and C=C double (0.134 nm) bond lengths\n• Kekulé structure would have two different bond lengths / an irregular hexagon\n• Molecule is planar and a regular hexagon, bond angles 120°\nStrand 2: Thermochemical evidence\n• Enthalpy change of hydrogenation of cyclohexene is −120 kJ mol⁻¹\n• Kekulé structure predicts 3 × −120 = −360 kJ mol⁻¹\n• Actual value for benzene is −208 kJ mol⁻¹, so benzene is about 150 kJ mol⁻¹ more stable\n• Extra stability explained by delocalisation of π electrons\nStrand 3: Evidence from reactions\n• Benzene does not decolourise bromine water / does not readily undergo addition\n• Reacts by electrophilic substitution (e.g. nitration, bromination with FeBr₃) so delocalised ring retained\n• Only one isomer of 1,2-dibromobenzene exists (Kekulé predicts two)\n• π electron density is spread out so less able to polarise electrophiles; halogen carrier needed\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A student plans to prepare a pure, dry sample of the orange dye 4-hydroxyazobenzene from phenylamine and phenol, and then to check that the product is pure.\n\nDescribe how the student should carry out the preparation, purify the solid product and test its purity. Include reagents, conditions and safety precautions.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nStrand 1: Preparation\n• Dissolve phenylamine in (dilute) hydrochloric acid and cool in an ice bath below 5–10 °C\n• Add cold sodium nitrite solution slowly, keeping temperature below 10 °C (forms benzenediazonium chloride)\n• Dissolve phenol in aqueous sodium hydroxide and cool\n• Add the cold diazonium solution to the alkaline phenol; orange precipitate forms\n• Safety: phenylamine and phenol are toxic/corrosive — gloves, fume cupboard; diazonium salts unstable, use immediately\nStrand 2: Purification\n• Filter under reduced pressure (Büchner funnel)\n• Wash with a little cold water\n• Recrystallise: dissolve in minimum volume of hot solvent (e.g. ethanol), filter hot if needed, cool to crystallise\n• Filter under reduced pressure again, wash with cold solvent and dry (in a desiccator / low oven)\nStrand 3: Testing purity\n• Measure melting point; pure sample has a sharp melting point close to the data-book value\n• Impure sample melts lower and over a range\n• TLC: pure sample gives a single spot; compare Rf with a pure standard\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  }
};
